{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LBzoNZoaBo7fcv2E9X5XE4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Machine learning and the nature of MOSP — findings\n2\t*Started 2026-09-25. The report `reports/ml_nature_plan.md` lays out the\n3\tquestions; this file collects the answers, one section per Ralph-loop\n4\titeration, appended and never rewritten. Where a later section contradicts an\n5\tearlier one it says so and the earlier one stays. Every number here is\n6\tregenerated by the command named in its section. Nothing in this file is a\n7\tbound.*\n8\t## 1. Isomorphism classes: how many instances does the corpus really hold? (§2.1a)\n9\t*Iteration 1, 2026-09-25. Code: `learning/canonical.py`. Regenerate:*\n10\t```bash\n11\tpip install pynauty                      # optional; without it only WL hashes are computed\n12\tpython -m learning.canonical --workers 16 --out reports/canonical_tables.md\n13\t```\n14\t*Under a second on 16 cores. Writes `learning/data/canonical.csv` (one row per\n15\tinstance: matrix digest, bipartite and MOSP-graph nauty certificates, WL hash,\n16\tautomorphism group order) and the tables below.*\n17\t**Question.** The optimum is `pathwidth(MOSP graph) + 1`, so two instances with\n18\tisomorphic MOSP graphs have the same optimum and are, for every question about\n19\tthe optimum, one instance. How many distinct instances does the corpus of 6,376\n20\tcertified optima actually contain, per collection and per size, and do\n21\tisomorphic instances carry equal optima?\n22\t**Method.** Three nested notions of \"the same instance\", each a canonical form:\n23\t(1) the matrix byte for byte; (2) the matrix up to renaming customers and\n24\tproducts, as a nauty certificate of the two-coloured bipartite graph\n25\tcustomers–products; (3) the MOSP graph up to renaming customers, as a nauty\n26\tcertificate of `M·Mᵀ > 0`. Alongside (3), the 3-iteration Weisfeiler–Lehman\n27\thash from networkx, which is cheap and what graph kernels and message-passing\n28\tGNNs can see, but is not a complete invariant. Classes are counted per\n29\tcollection, per `(n_customers, n_patterns)` and per size band, and within every\n30\tlevel-3 class the optima are compared.\n31\t**Baseline.** The count everything else in this repository has been using:\n32\t6,376. And, for the WL hash, nauty's exact class count.\n33\t### Corpus totals\n34\t### Per collection\n35\tWithin-collection counts hide the cross-collection duplicates, which the next\n36\ttable shows: `MOSP_Instances/Challenge` is 46 distinct instances *on its own*\n37\tand zero new ones for the corpus.\n38\t### Classes shared across collections (level 2)\n39\tAll 44 byte-identical duplicates are these: `MOSP_Instances/Challenge` is a copy\n40\tof the Miller, Shaw and Wilson files of `ChallengeInstances2005`. The two\n41\tChallenge instances not matched at level 2, `NWRS7_0` and `NWRS8_0`, are the\n42\tWilson `NWRS7` and `NWRS8` with one all-zero product column removed\n43\t(25×59 against 25×60); their MOSP graphs are isomorphic and their optima agree\n44\t(10 and 16). So every one of the 46 Challenge instances is certified twice under\n45\ttwo names, and the corpus count of 6,376 includes each of them twice. The\n46\tremaining 19 level-2 duplicates are all inside Harvey (2,130 → 2,111), the only\n47\tgenerator that re-emitted a matrix up to relabelling.\n48\t### Per size band\n49\t### Per (n_customers, n_patterns), sizes with at least 20 instances\n50\t### Where the redundancy comes from: complete graphs\n51\tThe four largest classes are the complete graphs K₁₀, K₁₅, K₃₀ and K₂₀, and\n52\tthe next four are the same graphs minus one edge, optimum `n − 1`. A quarter of\n53\tthe corpus is instances whose optimum is `n_customers` and whose MOSP graph is\n54\tcomplete, which the trivial lower bound alone certifies if any single product is\n55\trequired by every customer, and which contraction degeneracy certifies\n56\totherwise. Every one of them comes from Harvey or Simonis at densities that\n57\tsaturate the graph; no other collection contains a single complete graph.\n58\t### The WL hash against nauty\n59\tAll five collisions are sparse Harvey instances with `edges = n` or `n − 1`,\n60\tthat is, unions of paths and cycles, which 1-WL cannot tell apart when the\n61\tcycle lengths differ. All 14 hidden classes have optimum 3. So on this corpus\n62\t1-WL loses 0.4% of the classes and no information about the optimum, which\n63\tbounds in advance what the §2.2 GNN check can lose to expressivity: nothing that\n64\tshows in the optimum.\n65\t### The audit: isomorphic instances have equal optima\n66\t**Zero disagreements** over 3,667 classes, of which 203 have more than one\n67\tmember and together hold 2,912 instances. Each redundant instance's certified\n68\tvalue equals that of a class representative certified separately, 157 times\n69\tacross different files and 46 times across different collections. This is not\n70\tindependent of the solver, which produced both certificates, but it is\n71\tindependent of the *run*: the Challenge copies were solved in different sweeps\n72\tunder different names, and the giant Harvey + Simonis classes were certified in\n73\tup to eleven files each. No instance needed re-certification, so none was\n74\tre-solved and nothing was written to `solutions/`.\n75\t**Finding.** The corpus holds 3,667 distinct MOSP graphs, not 6,376 instances:\n76\t42.5% of it is isomorphic copies, and a quarter of it is complete graphs with\n77\toptimum `n`. The redundancy is almost entirely a small-instance phenomenon,\n78\t44.6% at `n ≤ 30` against 13.2% at `n > 30`, and is confined to Harvey and\n79\tSimonis, whose generators at high density saturate the 10-to-30-customer graph,\n80\tplus the 46 Challenge instances present under two names. Chu & Stuckey, SCOOP,\n81\tShaw and Wilson contain no duplicates at any level. Two consequences for every\n82\tstudy that follows. First, the effective sample size at `n ≤ 30` is 3,287, and\n83\tat `n > 30` it is 380, of which 200 are Chu & Stuckey. Second, grouping by\n84\t`source_file` does not isolate isomorphic siblings: 157 graph classes span more\n85\tthan one file, and the largest spans eleven, so a \"grouped\" split still trains\n86\ton copies of its test instances. `learning/data/canonical.csv` now carries a\n87\t`graph_cert` column, and the grouped protocol in `learning/study_optimum.py`\n88\tshould group by it (or drop to one representative per class) before any\n89\tgrouped number from `reports/learning.md` is quoted again; that is a change to\n90\tthe split, not to any solver default, and is left for the study that next\n91\ttouches the split. The 150 graph classes containing more than one matrix class,\n92\t2,796 matrix classes in all, are free material for §2.9 (same graph, different\n93\tclique cover). The audit found what it should: no pair of isomorphic instances\n94\tcarries different certified optima.\n95\t**Size range covered.** All 6,376 instances, 9 ≤ n ≤ 134. The redundancy\n96\tfigures are dominated by `n ≤ 30` (5,938 instances); at `n > 30` they rest on\n97\t438 instances and 380 classes.\n98\t**Not a bound, not a solver change.** Nothing here touches `_lower_bound` or any\n99\tdecision path; `learning.canonical` is analysis only.\n100\t## 2. Generator fingerprint and the map of instance space (§2.1b)\n101\t*Iteration 2, 2026-09-25. Code: `learning/fingerprint.py`. Regenerate:*\n102\t```bash\n103\tpip install umap-learn                   # optional; without it the map falls back to PCA\n104\tpython -m learning.dataset               # if learning/data/instances.csv is stale, ~20 s\n105\tpython -m learning.canonical --workers 16 --csv learning/data/canonical.csv   # for the leak-free split\n106\tpython -m learning.fingerprint --out reports/fingerprint_tables.md\n107\t```\n108\t*About 50 s on 16 cores. Writes `reports/figures/instance_space.png`,\n109\t`learning/data/instance_space.csv` (the 2-D coordinates, git-ignored) and the\n110\traw tables in `reports/fingerprint_tables.md`, from which every number below is\n111\tcopied.*\n112\t**Question.** Can a classifier tell which collection an instance came from\n113\tusing only its structure? If it can, the benchmark families occupy different\n114\tregions of instance space and a result measured on one says nothing about\n115\tanother. Its accuracy is the finding; the classifier is used for nothing.\n116\t**Method.** The label is the sub-collection, nine of them: Harvey, Simonis,\n117\tShaw, Wilson and Miller from `ChallengeInstances2005`; Chu & Stuckey,\n118\tFaggioli–Bentivoglio, SCOOP and the `Challenge` copies from `MOSP_Instances`.\n119\tFeatures are the 28 structure-only columns of `learning.features` (matrix\n120\tshape and degree statistics, MOSP-graph statistics; no bound), and, separately,\n121\ta 19-column *size-free* set in which every count is divided by the dimension it\n122\tscales with (`col_cv = col_std / col_mean`, `g_degeneracy / (n − 1)` and so on)\n123\tand `n`, `m`, `n_ones`, `g_nodes`, `g_edges` are dropped. The model is a random\n124\tforest of 300 trees; gradient boosting was tried first and scored 0.81 on a\n125\trandom split where the forest and LightGBM both score 0.98, thrown by the\n126\tclasses of one and twenty instances, so it was replaced. Three splits, all\n127\tshuffled: grouped by `source_file` as the loop's rules require; grouped by the\n128\tconnected components of files and MOSP-graph isomorphism classes from §1\n129\t(512 groups against 617 files), which is the split that does not leak\n130\tisomorphic copies; and a random split, printed only to show the leak. Plain\n131\t`GroupKFold` had to be replaced by a shuffled `StratifiedGroupKFold`: on the\n132\tChu & Stuckey files, which sort as `Random-n-m-d-1, -2, …`, its round-robin\n133\tassignment held out exactly one seed index per fold and made the control below\n134\tscore exactly zero.\n135\t**Baselines.** The majority class (Simonis, 56.9%); the two size columns\n136\t`(n_customers, n_patterns)` alone; and a ceiling: features are constant on a\n137\tmatrix-isomorphism class, so where a class holds rows from two collections the\n138\tminority rows are wrong under any model. From §1's classes the ceiling is\n139\t**0.993** (the 46 Challenge copies of Miller, Shaw and Wilson instances).\n140\t### The collection from structure\n141\tSize alone is *worse than the majority class* under a grouped split, because\n142\tevery file is one `(n, m)` cell: hold the file out and its size has never been\n143\tseen with its label. Structure with the size removed does as well as structure\n144\twith it, so the fingerprint is not the size.\n145\tThe balanced accuracy of about 0.5 is these last four rows. Shaw and Miller are\n146\tone file each, so under any grouped split they are never in the training set\n147\twhen they are tested; Wilson's five files are five different sizes. All 25 Shaw\n148\tinstances and 18 of 20 Wilson are predicted \"Challenge\", which is where their\n149\tbyte-identical copies sit in the training folds: that is the ceiling, and the\n150\tmodel finds it. These four collections cannot be fingerprinted from this corpus\n151\tbecause there is nothing to generalise from, not because they are\n152\tindistinguishable.\n153\tConfusion among the generators that can be learned (rows true, columns\n154\tpredicted, structure (all), grouped by file, 6,376 instances):\n155\tThe Simonis errors are two files: `problem_30_30` and `problem_40_20` lose 74\n156\tinstances to Chu & Stuckey, and `problem_10_20` loses 104 to\n157\tFaggioli–Bentivoglio. The 13 Chu & Stuckey errors are all `Random-30-30` at\n158\tdensities 6 to 10, the densest of their smallest size, predicted Simonis. SCOOP\n159\tis the only collection of real industrial instances and the only one whose\n160\terrors scatter over three generators.\n161\t### What the fingerprint is\n162\tPermutation importance on held-out files, accuracy points: `col_std` 0.196,\n163\t`row_std` 0.147, `density` 0.114, `col_max_frac` 0.113, then nothing above\n164\t0.02. A depth-3 tree on the size-free features scores **0.940** grouped by file\n165\tand reads:\n166\t```\n167\tcol_cv <= 0.017                         -> Harvey\n168\tcol_cv >  0.017 and row_cv <= 0.035     -> Harvey\n169\totherwise, col_max_frac <= 0.290        -> Faggioli–Bentivoglio\n170\totherwise                               -> Simonis\n171\t```\n172\tChecked directly against the files: **every Harvey instance has constant\n173\tcolumn sums, constant row sums, or both**, and no instance of any other\n174\tgenerator has either. Harvey's three file families are exactly these\n175\tconstraints: `wbo_*` (every product has the same number of customers,\n176\t64.3% of Harvey), `wbp_*` (every customer has the same number of products) and\n177\t`wbop_*` (both, a biregular bipartite graph). Faggioli–Bentivoglio's largest\n178\tproduct covers at most half the customers (`col_max_frac` 0.10 to 0.50, mean\n179\t0.22); Simonis's covers 0.23 to 0.95, mean 0.65, at a mean density of 0.455.\n180\tChu & Stuckey are recognised by their sparsity at sizes where nothing else is\n181\tsparse. So three of the four large collections are separated by two lines a\n182\tperson can state, and the fourth by density and size.\n183\t### Collections at the same (n, m)\n184\tRestricted to the eleven `(n, m)` cells where at least two collections each\n185\tcontribute ten or more instances, so that size cannot help. Grouped by file.\n186\tAt equal size the generators are told apart on 98.8% of instances; the 20×20\n187\tcell is the Shaw/Challenge ceiling again, and 30×30 is where dense Chu & Stuckey\n188\tmeets Simonis.\n189\t### Within Chu & Stuckey: `Random-n-m-d-k`\n190\tThe data says what the name means: `col_mean` tracks `d` to within 0.3 at\n191\tevery size (`d` is the mean number of customers per product), and `k` is a\n192\tseed index. Grouped by file, which is one instance per file, 200 instances.\n193\tThe density class is recovered from `col_mean` alone at 98%, and adding the\n194\tother 27 features adds nothing. The seed index sits at chance, as it must; a\n195\tclassifier that could predict `k` would be reading the file, not the instance.\n196\t(The size-only rows below 0.2 are the stratified split's arithmetic on a\n197\tfeature that carries no signal, not information.)\n198\t### The map\n199\t![instance space](figures/instance_space.png)\n200\tUMAP (`n_neighbors` 30, `min_dist` 0.1) of the standardised structure features,\n201\tleft coloured by collection, middle by number of customers, right the same for\n202\tthe size-free features. The map is not a continuum: it is a lattice of streaks,\n203\tone per `(n, m)` cell (74 cells in the corpus), which the middle panel shows\n204\tdirectly. Within a streak the generators separate; across streaks the layout is\n205\tsize. In the size-free panel the cells merge and the collections form their own\n206\tregions, with Chu & Stuckey at the sparse end of the Simonis band and\n207\tFaggioli–Bentivoglio and Wilson beside them.\n208\tNeighbourhood purity, the share of an instance's 10 nearest neighbours that\n209\tcarry its collection:\n210\tThe map keeps what the feature space has. The 24 SCOOP instances, the only\n211\treal-world data, have almost no SCOOP neighbours: they sit inside the sparse\n212\tHarvey and Faggioli–Bentivoglio regions. Of the twelve k-means regions of the\n213\tmap (`reports/fingerprint_tables.md`), one holds all 200 Chu & Stuckey\n214\tinstances together with 431 Harvey, 161 Faggioli–Bentivoglio, 113 Simonis and 9 SCOOP at\n215\tmean density 0.24 and mean `optimum / n` 0.60: the sparse, low-optimum corner is\n216\twhere the hard instances live and where the industrial ones fall too. The\n217\tregions that are pure Simonis (mean density 0.45, `optimum / n` 0.8 to 0.94)\n218\tare the complete and near-complete graphs of §1.\n219\t**Finding.** The benchmark families are fingerprintable: 94.4% of instances are\n220\tassigned to their collection from structure alone with the file held out\n221\t(93.3% with every isomorphic copy held out too), and 98.8% at equal size, where\n222\tthe baseline is 61.6%. The fingerprint is not size and is not subtle. Harvey is\n223\ta regularity constraint (constant column or row sums, present in all 2,130 of\n224\tits instances and in no other generator's); Faggioli–Bentivoglio caps the\n225\tlargest product at half the customers; Simonis is dense with large products;\n226\tChu & Stuckey is sparse where nothing else is, and within it the density class\n227\tis `col_mean` to within rounding while the seed index is at chance. What cannot\n228\tbe fingerprinted is what has no siblings to learn from: Shaw, Miller and Wilson\n229\tare one file or one size each and their copies under the Challenge name set a\n230\tceiling of 0.993 that the model hits exactly. The consequence for every study\n231\tthat follows is the one the plan anticipated: a result on Harvey is a result\n232\tabout biregular-ish random bipartite graphs, a result on Simonis is about dense\n233\trandom matrices at 10 to 40 customers, and neither transfers to the sparse\n234\tregion where Chu & Stuckey, the timeouts and the only industrial instances all\n235\tsit. The instance-space map says the corpus is a lattice of 74 size cells with\n236\tlarge empty regions between them, not a sample from a distribution over\n237\tinstances; §2.4's generated ensembles are what fills it.\n238\t**Size range covered.** All 6,376 instances, 9 ≤ n ≤ 134. The fingerprint\n239\tnumbers rest on the four large collections (6,260 instances, 9 ≤ n ≤ 125); the\n240\tsame-size test covers 10 ≤ n ≤ 40 only; the Chu & Stuckey result covers its\n241\t30 ≤ n ≤ 125. Nothing here has been re-certified because no finding depends on\n242\tany instance's optimum.\n243\t**Not a bound, not a solver change.** The classifier and the embedding are\n244\tdescriptive; nothing touches `_lower_bound` or any decision path, and nothing\n245\twas written to `solutions/`.\n246\t## 3. Node counts into the ledger (§2.4 prerequisite)\n247\t**Question.** Can the hardness of a decision call be recorded in a unit that\n248\tsurvives a change of hardware, and what does that unit look like on the part of\n249\tthe corpus where certification is cheap? §2.4 wants nodes visited by the\n250\trefutation at `optimum − 1`, not seconds; before this item the complete\n251\tcustomer search counted its branch decisions and no driver stored them\n252\t(`reports/ml_nature_plan.md` §0, \"node counts are printed, not stored\").\n253\t**Method.** Instrumentation, not a study. The search itself is unchanged: its\n254\t`Decision.nodes` and `Solution.nodes` were already returned by both the Python\n255\tand the C path. What changed is every seam above them.\n256\t- `satisfiability.mosp_solver.solve_mosp_exact` takes an optional `stats` dict\n257\t  and fills it with `nodes`, `seconds`, `proof` and `procedure`. Its return\n258\t  value is unchanged, so every existing caller keeps unpacking two values.\n259\t  `nodes` is `None`, not 0, for a cached answer and for the SAT procedure, which\n260\t  has none.\n261\t- `benchmarks.compute.record` writes a fifth ledger column, `nodes`, and takes\n262\t  rows of `(instance, seconds)` or `(instance, seconds, nodes)`; a driver with\n263\t  no search behind it (`reheuristic`) leaves the cell empty. A new\n264\t  `ensure_fields` migrates an older ledger in place once, appending the column\n265\t  and leaving every existing row empty; `python -m benchmarks.compute\n266\t  --migrate-ledger` runs it, and `record` runs it before appending.\n267\t- `benchmarks.csearch`: the worker posts `result.nodes` as a sixth element of\n268\t  its row and `sweep` records it; the first five fields are unchanged, which is\n269\t  what `benchmarks.marathon` indexes.\n270\t- `benchmarks.recertify`: already returned `nodes` from its worker and wrote it\n271\t  into `recertify/results.json` and the solution file's `recertified` note; it\n272\t  now also appends one ledger row per finished entry, as it lands, with the\n273\t  seconds and nodes of the call, under `--ledger` (default the project's).\n274\t- `learning/node_counts.py` checks the instrumentation end to end: it\n275\t  summarises the ledger's `nodes` column per driver, then runs `decide` at\n276\t  `optimum − 1` on every certified instance with at most 40 customers under\n277\t  the two configurations whose counts reach the ledger (`decide` defaults, as\n278\t  `solve_mosp_exact` runs; and the `csearch` driver's rule, Theorem 2 on sparse\n279\t  instances with every candidate a dominator), recording status, nodes and\n280\t  seconds. The status is a free audit: `sat` there would mean the stored\n281\t  optimum is wrong.\n282\t**Baseline.** The ledger before this item: 279 rows, 174 from `csearch` and 105\n283\tfrom `reheuristic`, none carrying a node count. That is the row every later\n284\t§2.4 run is compared against, and it is the first table below.\n285\t### The compute ledger on the day the column landed\n286\tThe 279 existing rows now read `\"\"` in `nodes`. The compute total is unchanged\n287\tat 667 core-hours (`python -m benchmarks.compute --quote`), since nodes are not\n288\tcompute and are not summed.\n289\t### The audit: every stored optimum at n ≤ 40 refutes at optimum − 1\n290\t12,270 decision calls, all `unsat`, none at the 10 s deadline; the whole run\n291\ttakes about a second on 16 workers. This is a second independent refutation\n292\tfor 6,135 of the 6,376 certified optima, under two configurations, and it\n293\tagrees with the corpus everywhere. It does not touch the 241 instances above\n294\t40 customers, which is where the two known false-refutation incidents were.\n295\t### Nodes to refute optimum − 1, by size band\n296\t### Nodes to refute optimum − 1, by collection\n297\t### The two configurations, nodes per instance\n298\t**Finding.** The node count now comes back through every path a decision call\n299\ttakes and lands in the ledger from both drivers that make decision calls; the\n300\tsearch itself was not touched and the corpus was not written to. What the\n301\tfirst look shows is how little of the small corpus is a search problem at all:\n302\t38.5% of the 6,135 refutations at `optimum − 1` visit **zero** nodes (53.7% at\n303\tn ≤ 10, falling to 25% at 31–40), meaning the root is refuted by the search's\n304\town cost check before any branch, and 2,357 of those 2,360 are Challenge\n305\tinstances, where §1 found 1,669 complete graphs. Where the search does branch,\n306\tthe count grows about an order of magnitude per ten customers at the tail\n307\t(p90: 4 → 23 → 108 → 1,764) while the median stays in single or double digits,\n308\tand the single worst call, 29,138 nodes on `Random-40-40-2-2_0`, is at Chu &\n309\tStuckey density 2: over the fifty `Random-30` and `Random-40` instances the\n310\tmedian nodes fall monotonically with density (1,068 → 428 → 140 → 22 → 6 from\n311\tdensity 2 to 10), which is the direction §2.4 predicts and the sign that the\n312\thardness peak, if there is one, sits at or below density 2 for these sizes.\n313\tThe `csearch` driver's configuration never costs more than 1.1× the defaults\n314\tand is cheaper on 1,302 instances, so mixing the two in one ledger is a small\n315\tdistortion, and the `config` is recorded per row so it can be separated. None\n316\tof this is the §2.4 study: density is confounded with collection here (§2),\n317\tnothing is deduplicated by isomorphism class (§1 says 42.5% of the corpus is\n318\tredundant), and 50 instances at two sizes is not a density sweep.\n319\t**Size range covered.** The audit and the tables cover the 6,135 certified\n320\tinstances with 2 ≤ optimum and n ≤ 40 (9 ≤ n ≤ 40), which is 96% of the corpus\n321\tby count and none of the instances on which any run has ever taken more than a\n322\tsecond. The ledger change covers every future row of every size. Nothing was\n323\tre-certified because nothing disagreed; the run itself is a re-refutation of\n324\tevery optimum it touched.\n325\t**Not a bound, not a solver change.** No default changed; `_lower_bound` and\n326\tevery decision path are untouched; nothing was written to `solutions/`. The\n327\trunning `benchmarks.recertify` process (PIDs under `pgrep -f\n328\tbenchmarks.recertify`) predates these edits and was not restarted, so it will\n329\tnot write `nodes` rows; its `recertify/results.json` carries the counts for\n330\tthose calls.\n331\t**Regenerate.** `python -m benchmarks.compute --migrate-ledger` (once;\n332\tidempotent), then `python -m learning.node_counts --max-customers 40 --out\n333\treports/node_count_tables.md` (about 1 s on 16 workers; writes\n334\t`learning/data/node_counts.csv`). The by-density line: group\n335\t`learning/data/node_counts.csv` rows whose `instance_name` starts with\n336\t`Random-` by the fourth dash-separated field. Tests: `python -m pytest\n337\ttests/test_node_counts.py -q`.\n338\t## 4. Pathwidth-adjacent invariants as features (§2.2a)\n339\t*Iteration 4, 2026-09-25. Code: `learning/features.py` (group `invariants`),\n340\t`learning/invariants_study.py`. Regenerate:*\n341\t```bash\n342\tpython -m learning.dataset --workers 16                                   # 6,376 x 49 features, ~90 s\n343\tpython -m learning.invariants_study --workers 16 --out reports/invariants_tables.md   # ~140 s\n344\tpython -m pytest tests/test_invariants.py -q\n345\t```\n346\t*Writes `learning/data/instances.csv` (git-ignored) and the tables below.\n347\t`learning.study_optimum` and `learning.fingerprint` leave the new group out by\n348\tdefault, so `reports/learning.md` §1 and §2 above still regenerate from the\n349\tcolumns they report.*\n350\t**Question.** The optimum is `pathwidth(MOSP graph) + 1`, and §1 of\n351\t`reports/learning.md` found that of the 36 features it is the MOSP graph's\n352\tedge count and mean degree that carry the signal. Which of the invariants\n353\tthat pathwidth is known to sit beside carry more of it, and do they explain\n354\tthe part of the optimum the proved bound misses?\n355\t**Method.** Thirteen new columns in a fourth feature group: the min-fill and\n356\tmin-degree elimination widths (`tw_min_fill`, `tw_min_degree`, networkx's\n357\ttreewidth heuristics; upper bounds on treewidth, which is at most pathwidth,\n358\tso bounds on nothing here); the bandwidth of the reverse Cuthill–McKee order\n359\t(`bw_rcm`; bandwidth is at least pathwidth, so `bw_rcm + 1` is a valid upper\n360\tbound on the optimum and the corpus is checked against that); the adjacency\n361\tspectral radius and the Laplacian Fiedler value, on the whole graph and on\n362\tits largest component; two edge clique cover counts (`cc_products`, the\n363\tdistinct products containing an edge, which is the upper bound the item asks\n364\tfor, and `cc_greedy`, after dropping every product all of whose edges another\n365\tproduct also covers); a balanced separator (`sep_size`, the smallest\n366\tone-sided boundary over the middle-third cuts of the Fiedler order and of the\n367\tRCM order, and `sep_frac = sep_size / n`); and the random intersection graph\n368\tquantities: with `p̂ = density` and `(n, m) = (n_customers, n_patterns)`\n369\talready in the matrix group, the pairwise edge probability\n370\t`1 − (1 − p̂²)^m`, the expected degree `(n − 1)` times it, and the ratio of the\n371\tobserved MOSP-graph density to that probability. The four greedy invariants\n372\trun on a copy relabelled by a label-free key (degree, neighbour degrees,\n373\ttwo-hop degree sums) so tie-breaks do not depend on the file's row order, and\n374\tthe residual order-dependence is measured by re-featuring 623 shuffled\n375\tinstances (400 at random plus all 241 with `n ≥ 50`, minus overlap). The\n376\tablation fits the same `HistGradientBoostingRegressor` as `learning.study_optimum`\n377\ton four feature sets under three splits: grouped by file, grouped by file ∪\n378\tMOSP-graph isomorphism class (`learning.fingerprint.union_groups`), and\n379\trandom for contrast; on the optimum and on the residual `optimum − lb_best`.\n380\tPermutation importance is on a held-out fifth of the file ∪ class groups.\n381\t**Baseline.** The 36-feature model, grouped by file (`reports/learning.md`\n382\t§1), and the solver's own `lb_best` and `ub_best` as point estimates. Note\n383\tthat the table was rebuilt: `lb_best` now includes the expansion bound\n384\t(higher on 1,329 rows than the table §1 of `reports/learning.md` was computed\n385\tfrom) and `Random-100-100-2-2_0` carries its corrected optimum of 20, so the\n386\tbaseline rows below are the current corpus, not the September 22 numbers.\n387\t### Invariants as point estimates of the optimum\n388\t`below`/`above` count instances where the estimate sits under/over the\n389\toptimum. A valid lower bound has `above = 0`; a valid upper bound `below = 0`.\n390\t### The leading estimates by size band\n391\t### Ablation: the same model with and without the invariants\n392\t`delta_mae` is against the same feature set without the group; negative is\n393\tbetter; the plan's kill criterion is 0.02. `mae_gap_rows` restricts the\n394\tscoring to the 1,467 instances where `ub_best > lb_best`.\n395\tThe file ∪ class grouping is far harsher than the file grouping because it is\n396\tfar more unbalanced: its largest group holds 1,620 instances (25.4% of the\n397\tcorpus; the complete graphs of §1 chain Harvey and Simonis files together)\n398\tand its five largest hold 88.0%, against 8.6% and 43.1% for the file\n399\tgrouping. `GroupKFold` keeps a group whole, so the file ∪ class split is in\n400\teffect a five-region hold-out, and its absolute numbers are extrapolation\n401\terrors between generators; the grouped-by-file rows are the ones comparable\n402\twith `reports/learning.md`.\n403\t### By size band (grouped by file ∪ class, target optimum)\n404\t### Permutation importance, all 49 features, target optimum\n405\tMAE points on a held-out fifth of the file ∪ class groups; the top 20, then\n406\twhere every invariant landed.\n407\tInvariants' ranks: `tw_min_fill` #1 (2.011), `tw_min_degree` #6 (0.064),\n408\t`spectral_radius` #10 (0.025), `bw_rcm` #11 (0.024), `cc_greedy` #19\n409\t(0.005), `sep_frac` #21 (0.005), `rig_deg_expected` #24 (0.002),\n410\t`rig_density_ratio` #30 (0.001), `cc_products` #31 (0.000), `rig_edge_prob`\n411\t#34 (0.000), `fiedler` #41 (−0.000), `sep_size` #47 (−0.002), `fiedler_lcc`\n412\t#48 (−0.003).\n413\tOn the residual `optimum − lb_best` the ranking is `bound_gap` 0.283,\n414\t`bound_gap_frac` 0.229, `ub_cs_dfs` 0.020, then `bw_rcm` 0.017 as the first\n415\tinvariant, `density` 0.016, `sep_size` 0.010, `tw_min_fill` 0.007 and\n416\t`fiedler` 0.007: nothing in the group moves the residual by more than two\n417\thundredths of a stack.\n418\t### Each invariant alone, with the two sizes (grouped by file ∪ class)\n419\t### Order-dependence of the greedy invariants\n420\t623 instances re-featured after shuffling customers and products.\n421\t**Finding.** One invariant carries almost all of what the group adds, and it\n422\tis the min-fill elimination width. `tw_min_fill + 1` is, on this corpus, a\n423\tcloser point estimate of the optimum than either of the solver's own bounds:\n424\texact on 85.7% of the 6,376 instances against 84.6% for `ub_best` and 77.0%\n425\tfor `lb_best`, at MAE 0.188 against 0.239 and 0.431, and it leads in every\n426\tsize band, including the 120 instances with 61–134 customers where it is off\n427\tby 2.6 stacks on average against 3.0 for the upper bound and 8.7 for the\n428\tlower. It is a bound on nothing: it sits below the optimum on 481 instances\n429\t(by up to 3) and above it on 428 (by up to 7), and treewidth being at most\n430\tpathwidth does not make a treewidth *heuristic* either. In the model it is\n431\tthe single most important feature of all 49 at 2.01 MAE points, above\n432\t`ub_cs_dfs` at 1.21 and `lb_best` at 0.43, and alone with the two sizes it\n433\tpredicts the optimum better under the harshest grouping (MAE 0.997, exact\n434\t73.7%) than `lb_best` with the same sizes does (1.081, 68.6%). The plan's\n435\tkill criterion of 0.02 MAE is cleared by an order of magnitude on the\n436\tstructure-only set — grouped by file the MAE falls from 0.436 to 0.220 and\n437\tthe exact rate rises from 71.2% to 87.1%, which is to say that structure plus\n438\tinvariants, with no solver bound computed, now predicts the optimum as well as\n439\tstructure plus the bounds did (0.191, 88.3%) — and cleared narrowly or not at\n440\tall once the bounds are already present (−0.012 by file, −0.044 by file ∪\n441\tclass). On the **residual over the proved bound the group adds nothing**:\n442\t−0.004, −0.000 and −0.009 MAE, and no invariant moves that target by more than\n443\t0.017 points. So the invariants know what the bound knows, expressed\n444\tdifferently, and not what it is missing. Where the residual does correlate\n445\twith anything it is with the random-model sparsity: the pairwise edge\n446\tprobability `1 − (1 − p̂²)^m` has Spearman −0.52 with the gap, the separator\n447\tfraction −0.42 and the Fiedler value −0.32, so the bound falls short where the\n448\tgraph is sparse and weakly connected, which is a direction for §2.3 rather\n449\tthan an explanation. Of the rest: `tw_min_degree` (#6), the spectral radius\n450\t(#10) and `bw_rcm` (#11) each add a few hundredths; the Fiedler value, the\n451\tseparator, both clique cover counts and the random intersection parameters\n452\tcontribute nothing once min-fill is present, and the random-model expected\n453\tdegree predicts the optimum worse (MAE 2.13) than the observed mean degree\n454\t(1.47), with the density ratio spanning 0.49–1.38, which says again what §2\n455\tsaid: these graphs are not random intersection graphs at their own `(n, m,\n456\tp̂)`. `bw_rcm + 1` was never below the optimum on any of the 6,376 instances,\n457\tas bandwidth ≥ pathwidth requires — a corpus-wide consistency check against a\n458\ttheorem that cost nothing — and it is exact on 51.4% of them. The relabelling\n459\tby a label-free key makes the greedy invariants order-independent in practice:\n460\tover 623 shuffled instances the two treewidth heuristics and the separator\n461\tnever changed, the RCM bandwidth changed once by one, and only `cc_greedy`\n462\tmoved (26 instances, by at most 2), which is documented and harmless since it\n463\tcarries no importance.\n464\t**Size range covered.** 9–134 customers, 5,938 of 6,376 at n ≤ 30. The\n465\tablation and importance tables are dominated by the small instances; at 31–60\n466\tcustomers (318 instances) every model's grouped MAE is above 4 and at 61–134\n467\t(120 instances) above 26, so no model here predicts the optimum at those\n468\tsizes and the only claim that reaches them is the point-estimate comparison\n469\tin the by-band table. The order-dependence check covers every instance with\n470\tn ≥ 50.\n471\t**Not a bound, not a solver change.** No default changed; `_lower_bound` and\n472\tevery decision path are untouched; nothing was written to `solutions/`. No\n473\tfinding rests on a handful of instances, so nothing was re-certified; the\n474\t`bw_rcm + 1 ≥ optimum` check is a whole-corpus property and it passed.\n475\t**For §2.2b.** The residual is the target the formula search should aim at,\n476\tand this section says which columns to leave out of it: the treewidth\n477\theuristics track the bound, not the gap. The candidates with any signal on the\n478\tgap are `rig_edge_prob`, `sep_frac`, `fiedler`, `cc_greedy` (ρ = 0.38) and\n479\t`bw_rcm`.\n480\t## 5. A formula for the residual, and for the optimum (§2.2b)\n481\t*Iteration 7, 2026-09-25 (iteration 5 wrote the search and was cut short before\n482\tits run finished; this iteration finished, fixed the run and wrote it up).\n483\tCode: `learning/formula_search.py`. Regenerate:*\n484\t```bash\n485\tpython -m learning.dataset --workers 16                                  # if the table is stale (~90 s)\n486\tpython -m learning.formula_search --workers 16 --out reports/formula_tables.md   # ~400 s; --no-pysr for the enumerated part alone (~7 min)\n487\tpython -m pytest tests/test_formula_search.py -q\n488\t```\n489\t*Writes `reports/formula_tables.md` (every table below, in full). The\n490\tenumerated rows regenerate to the digit; the PySR rows do not, because\n491\tmultithreaded PySR is not deterministic even with a seed, and see the note on\n492\tJulia crashes under Method.*\n493\t**Question.** §4 found that a boosting model on structure and the\n494\tpathwidth-adjacent invariants predicts the optimum as well as the solver's\n495\tbounds do, and that no invariant explains the residual `optimum − lb_best`\n496\tbeyond what the bound knows. A boosting model explains nothing. Is there a\n497\t*closed-form* formula in structure-only features for the residual, and one for\n498\tthe optimum, and is either clean enough to state as a conjecture?\n499\t**Method.** Two feature pools, both without any `lb_*`, `ub_*` or `bound_*`\n500\tcolumn: *structure* (38 columns: the matrix and MOSP-graph features minus\n501\t`g_nodes`, which equals `n_customers`, plus the §4 invariants minus the two\n502\ttreewidth heuristics) and *structure + treewidth heuristics* (40). Two\n503\ttargets: `optimum − lb_best` (structure pool only, since §4 showed the\n504\ttreewidth heuristics track the bound, not the gap) and `optimum` (both pools).\n505\tTwo searches. (1) An **enumerated** search over every product and ratio of at\n506\tmost three features, `f₁^e₁ · f₂^e₂ · f₃^e₃` with exponents in\n507\t{−2, −1, −½, ½, 1, 2} for one feature, {−1, −½, ½, 1, 2} for two and {−1, 1}\n508\tfor three, a negative exponent only on a strictly positive column: 58,221\n509\tterms on the structure pool, 68,690 with the treewidth heuristics. Each term\n510\t`t` is scored as the two-parameter fit `y ≈ a·t + b`, constants fitted on the\n511\ttraining folds and error measured on the held-out folds of a five-fold grouped\n512\tsplit: least squares as the first pass over every term, then least absolute\n513\tdeviation (the metric is MAE and the target's tail pulls a least-squares line\n514\toff the bulk) for the leading 150, ranked by that. A second stage fits\n515\t`y ≈ a·t₁ + b·t₂ + c` over all pairs of those 150. Splits: grouped by\n516\t`source_file`, grouped by file ∪ MOSP-graph isomorphism class\n517\t(`learning.fingerprint.union_groups`, §1), and random for contrast. Because the\n518\tsearch *selects* a term by its grouped error, that error is optimistic, so a\n519\t**nested** protocol runs the whole search inside each outer training fold and\n520\tscores its winner on the held-out files; those are the honest numbers.\n521\t(2) **PySR** (`pip install pysr`, optional; Julia bootstraps on first use) with\n522\t`+ − × ÷`, `sqrt`, `square`, `log`, L1 loss, maxsize 20, on every fifth file in\n523\tsorted order held out (124 files, 719 instances), its whole Pareto front scored\n524\ton the held-out rows beside the enumerated winner and boosting fitted on the\n525\tsame training rows. Finally the two invariants both searches keep returning\n526\tare tabulated with their **constants fixed by hand** rather than fitted, so\n527\tthere is nothing to hold out. *A note on the PySR runs:* PySR 2.5 on this\n528\tmachine crashed inside Julia's garbage collector on six of seven fits at 16\n529\tthreads and 10,000 iterations (segfault or abort, one of them on the\n530\twall-clock stop path), whichever process it ran in; at 4 threads and 2,000\n531\titerations every fit finished. Each fit now runs in a child process with one\n532\tretry, the tables above it are written to disk before PySR starts, and the\n533\tdefaults are the ones that survive.\n534\t**Baseline.** LightGBM on the same pool and the same grouped folds (the item's\n535\tbaseline), the constant formula `residual = 0` (which is `lb_best` itself, MAE\n536\t0.431), and `tw_min_fill + 1`, the best point estimate §4 found (MAE 0.188).\n537\t**The searches** (pooled held-out MAE / exact rate of the LAD fit `y ≈ a·t + b`;\n538\t`file` = grouped by file, `class` = grouped by file ∪ isomorphism class,\n539\t`random` leaks isomorphic copies across folds):\n540\tThe formulas do not care which split they are scored under, to three decimals;\n541\tboosting does, badly, under the class grouping (which is a five-region\n542\thold-out in practice, §4). The LAD constants fitted on all rows are\n543\t**Nested** (the formula selected on the training files only, scored on the\n544\theld-out files; the honest grouped MAE) and the winners per outer fold:\n545\tSelection inflates the monomials' error by at most 0.001, so the grouped\n546\tnumbers above are what a fresh file should expect.\n547\t**Point estimates of the optimum**, grouped by file, constants refitted per\n548\tfold, the residual formulas added to `lb_best`; `below`/`above` count rounded\n549\testimates under and over the optimum (a valid lower bound has `above = 0`):\n550\tBy size band (MAE, grouped by file):\n551\tBy collection the residual monomial beats boosting only on Harvey (0.266 vs\n552\t0.274) and Simonis (0.172 vs 0.184), the two collections that are 90% of the\n553\tcorpus and almost all at n ≤ 30; it loses on every other: Faggioli–Bentivoglio\n554\t0.505 vs 0.426, Chu & Stuckey 2.430 vs 1.191, SCOOP 1.245 vs 0.519, Wilson\n555\t1.329 vs 0.286.\n556\t**The sandwich**, constants fixed by hand (nothing fitted, nothing held out).\n557\tDegeneracy ≤ treewidth ≤ pathwidth and bandwidth ≥ pathwidth, so\n558\t`g_degeneracy + 1` is a proved lower bound on the optimum and `bw_rcm + 1` a\n559\tproved upper bound; the corpus is checked against both, and the optimum is\n560\t`g_degeneracy + 1 + λ · (bw_rcm − g_degeneracy)` for some λ ∈ [0, 1]:\n561\t**PySR** on the held-out fifth of the files (719 instances; the enumerated\n562\tmonomial and pair selected by grouped CV on the training files only, boosting\n563\tfitted on the same rows):\n564\t\"Best on held-out\" picks among the ≤ 20 equations of a Pareto front by their\n565\tscore on the test rows, so it is optimistic by that selection; PySR's own pick\n566\tis chosen from training loss alone. The fronts themselves are in\n567\t`reports/formula_tables.md`. For the residual the front reads, in order of\n568\tcomplexity: `0`, `spectral_radius − g_deg_mean`, `(0.19 · g_deg_std)²`,\n569\t`0.09 · (bw_rcm − g_degeneracy)`, `(0.07 · (bw_rcm − g_deg_mean))²`, then\n570\t`g_deg_std` times a squared sparsity term from complexity 7 on — the degree\n571\tspread, and the width of the sandwich, and nothing else.\n572\t**Finding.** There is no clean formula for the residual. The best closed form\n573\tin the structure features is `optimum − lb_best ≈ 0.01 · g_deg_std · sep_size /\n574\tg_density − 0.02`, grouped MAE 0.303 (0.304 nested) against 0.431 for the\n575\tconstant zero and 0.260 for LightGBM on the same pool: a formula recovers\n576\tthree quarters of what boosting recovers, and its constants are not ones\n577\tanybody would conjecture. What the term says is intelligible even so. Since\n578\t`g_density = g_deg_mean / (n − 1)`, it is `(n − 1) · sep_size · σ_deg / μ_deg`:\n579\tthe residual grows with the *dispersion of the degree sequence*, times the\n580\tseparator size. That is the same fact §4 reached through correlations and\n581\t`CLAUDE.md` records from the bound side, that the degree-based bounds saturate\n582\tat the average degree, now with the degree spread as the explicit variable:\n583\ton the 1,721 instances where the term is zero the gap is zero on 99.8%, and\n584\ton the 1,705 degree-regular graphs it is zero on every one — but those are\n585\tcomplete graphs, unions of cycles and one Miller graph, where a tight bound\n586\tis a triviality rather than a finding, so no conjecture is stated for the\n587\tresidual. For the *optimum*, the clean formula exists and is the same in both\n588\tsearches: with the treewidth heuristics allowed it is `tw_min_fill + 1`, LAD\n589\tconstants (1.000, 1.000), MAE 0.188, and nothing the enumerator or the pair\n590\tstage adds improves it beyond 0.187; without them it is\n591\t`1 + √(g_degeneracy · bw_rcm)`, again with constants exactly (1, 1), chosen in\n592\tall five outer folds, MAE 0.672 and exact on 67.1% — the geometric mean of a\n593\tproved lower bound and a proved upper bound on the optimum. That is the\n594\tfinding worth keeping: the two invariants the searches converge on sit on\n595\topposite sides of the optimum, `degeneracy + 1 ≤ optimum ≤ bw_rcm + 1` holds\n596\ton all 6,376 instances (as the two theorems behind it require, a\n597\twhole-corpus consistency check that passed), they coincide and force the\n598\toptimum on 2,823 of them, and where they differ the optimum sits at the\n599\tmidpoint on median, with interquartile range ⅓–⅔, its position λ tracking\n600\tsparsity (Spearman 0.40 with the separator fraction) and not size (0.05).\n601\tPySR's whole Pareto front for the optimum is that same statement written out,\n602\t`g_degeneracy + 1 + λ(·) · (bw_rcm − g_degeneracy)` with λ a function of\n603\t`sep_frac`, `row_mean` or `n_ones`; in this run PySR's own pick for the optimum from structure alone is\n604\t`distinct_row_frac + sqrt(g_degeneracy · bw_rcm)`, the enumerated formula\n605\tfound by a second method, and with the treewidth heuristics allowed its pick\n606\tis `tw_min_fill + 1`, held-out MAE 0.224 against boosting's 0.229. The one\n607\tplace a formula beats boosting is the residual on the held-out files: PySR's\n608\t`((g_components · rig_edge_prob · distinct_row_frac − 0.89)² · distinct_row_frac · g_deg_std)²`\n609\tscores 0.235 against boosting's 0.276, selected on the test rows and so\n610\toptimistic, and it is again the degree standard deviation times a sparsity\n611\tterm; its own pick, `(0.07 · (bw_rcm − g_deg_mean))²` at 0.321, says the gap\n612\tgrows with the square of how far the bandwidth runs above the mean degree. **None of this is a\n613\tbound.** `1 + √(g_degeneracy · bw_rcm)` is below the optimum on 1,495 instances\n614\tand above it on 600, by up to 34 and 9; the residual formula added to\n615\t`lb_best` overshoots on 237; the geometric mean is *worse* than `lb_best` at\n616\t61–134 customers (13.06 vs 8.71), because on the sparse Chu & Stuckey graphs\n617\t`bw_rcm` runs far above the pathwidth. And the one clean lower bound in it,\n618\t`g_degeneracy + 1`, is dominated by the contraction degeneracy + 1 that\n619\t`_lower_bound` already computes (contraction degeneracy ≥ degeneracy), so\n620\tthere is nothing here to hand to the bound work; a conjecture of the form\n621\t\"pathwidth ≥ f(·)\" would have to come from a different family than degree\n622\tstatistics, which is what §4's kill statement already said.\n623\t**Size range covered.** 9–134 customers, 5,938 of 6,376 at n ≤ 30. Every\n624\tgrouped MAE in the searches is dominated by that band. At 31–60 (318\n625\tinstances) the residual formula is at 0.627 against boosting's 0.483 and\n626\t`lb_best`'s 1.132; at 61–134 (120 instances) it is 3.88 against 1.64 and 8.71,\n627\tand no formula here predicts the optimum at that size. The sandwich\n628\tinequalities and the forced-optimum count are whole-corpus properties and\n629\thold at every size; the λ ≈ ½ statement rests on 3,169 instances at n ≤ 30,\n630\t266 at 31–60 and 118 above, with the same median in each band to within 0.1.\n631\t**Not a bound, not a solver change.** No default changed; `_lower_bound` and\n632\tevery decision path are untouched; nothing was written to `solutions/`. No\n633\tfinding rests on a handful of instances: the two inequalities and the forced\n634\tcount are corpus-wide, and the 36 non-complete regular graphs are all\n635\tcertified by their bound (`lb_best = optimum`), which is a proof independent\n636\tof any search refutation, so nothing was re-certified.\n637\t**For §2.3 and the next loop.** The residual's variable is degree dispersion,\n638\tso the gap-vs-tight classifier of §2.3 should start from `g_deg_std / g_deg_mean`\n639\tand `sep_size` beside `rig_edge_prob` and `sep_frac`. For §2.5 (does the optimum\n640\tconcentrate on random instances) the sandwich is the natural frame: on\n641\t`G(n, m, p)` the degeneracy and RCM bandwidth have their own concentration\n642\tresults, and the question becomes whether λ concentrates at ½.\n643\t## 6. Where the bounds fail (§2.3)\n644\t*Iteration 8, 2026-09-25 (iteration 6 wrote `learning/bound_gap.py` and its\n645\ttests and ran it, but ended before reading the run; its classifier tables\n646\tscored 1.000 everywhere because the study's own label column `y` had been\n647\tswept into the \"structure\" feature set by an every-numeric-column selector.\n648\tThis iteration fixed the leak, added a guard and a test for it, added the\n649\tsize-free feature set, the treewidth ceiling and the per-instance treewidth\n650\tand simplicial counts, re-ran, and wrote this section.) Code:\n651\t`learning/bound_gap.py`. Regenerate:*\n652\t```bash\n653\tpython -m learning.dataset --workers 16                                    # if the table is stale (~90 s)\n654\tpython -m learning.canonical --workers 16                                  # the isomorphism classes (~1 s)\n655\tpython -m learning.bound_gap --workers 16 --out reports/bound_gap_tables.md   # ~120 s; --no-recertify skips the re-solve of the ten\n656\tpython -m pytest tests/test_bound_gap.py -q\n657\t```\n658\t*Writes `reports/bound_gap_tables.md` (every table below in full, plus the ten\n659\tinstances drawn) and `learning/data/bound_gap.csv` (git-ignored, the 338 gap\n660\tinstances with their cluster). Nothing is written to `solutions/`: the\n661\tre-certification calls `solve_mosp_exact` with the corpus directory, which\n662\tre-verifies the cached witness by simulation, and refutes `optimum − 1` through\n663\t`learning.node_counts.refute`, which writes nothing.*\n664\t**Question.** `lb_best` — the best of the trivial bound, the clique bound,\n665\tcontraction degeneracy + 1 and the expansion bound — equals the optimum on\n666\t77.0% of the corpus, misses by one on 17.7% and by two or more on 5.3% (338\n667\tinstances). Do the instances it misses by two or more share a recognisable\n668\tstructure, and which of the four component bounds is blind to it?\n669\t**Method.** The label is `gap = optimum − lb_best` from the feature table,\n670\tthree-way (tight, gap 1, gap ≥ 2); the classifier sees tight against gap ≥ 2\n671\twith the gap-1 rows left out, 5,247 rows and 338 positives. Three models — a\n672\tdepth-3 decision tree (balanced class weight, 20 rows per leaf), an\n673\texplainable boosting machine (`interpret`, soft import, no interactions) and\n674\t`HistGradientBoostingClassifier` as a ceiling — on five feature sets: the\n675\tplan's baseline `n_customers`, `n_patterns`, `density`; the 28 matrix and\n676\tMOSP-graph features; those plus the 13 invariants of §4; a 32-column\n677\t*size-free* set (`learning.fingerprint.size_free_features` plus the invariants\n678\tdivided by their dimension, so a tree can split on `g_deg_cv` or `bw_rcm / n`);\n679\tand structure + invariants + the three lower bounds. No upper bound anywhere,\n680\tsince `ub_best` equals the optimum on 85% of the corpus and `ub − lb` would be\n681\tthe label in disguise. Out-of-fold probabilities under `GroupKFold` grouped by\n682\tfile ∪ MOSP-graph isomorphism class (`learning.fingerprint.union_groups`, the\n683\thonest split) with the file-only grouping beside it; AUC, average precision\n684\tand balanced accuracy, whole and per size band. Because gap ≥ 2 never occurs\n685\tbelow 20 customers and is the norm above 60, the same models are scored again\n686\ton the 2,407 rows in the eleven `(n, m)` cells holding at least three of each\n687\tclass, where size cannot be the answer. Then each size-free feature alone,\n688\tz-scored within its cell, as a ranker of gap ≥ 2 against tight over those\n689\tcells (AUC and Cohen's d); the EBM's term importances there; the depth-3\n690\ttrees as text; k-means over the standardised size-free features of the 338\n691\tgap instances with k by silhouette; and the ten smallest gap ≥ 2 instances\n692\t(fewest customers, then fewest ones, one per isomorphism class), drawn as\n693\tmatrices with their degree sequences, every bound recomputed from the\n694\tinstance, the number of customers in exactly one product, and a treewidth\n695\tupper bound from 200 randomised min-fill / min-degree eliminations. Each of\n696\tthe ten is re-certified: the exact solver's value and an independent\n697\trefutation of `optimum − 1`.\n698\tOne table not in the plan came out of a consistency check. The trivial bound\n699\tis a clique, the clique bound is a clique, and contraction degeneracy is a\n700\tlower bound on treewidth (Bodlaender, Koster & Wolle), so three of the four\n701\tcomponents of `lb_best` are lower bounds on **treewidth + 1**, while\n702\t`tw_min_fill + 1` from §4 is an upper bound on it. Hence\n703\t`optimum > tw_min_fill + 1` certifies `pathwidth > treewidth` on that\n704\tinstance, with no heuristic in the certificate (min-fill is an upper bound,\n705\tthe optimum is exact). The \"treewidth ceiling\" table counts those\n706\tcertificates per gap class and checks the two inequalities the theorems\n707\trequire; the module raises if either is violated.\n708\t**Baseline.** Density and size alone, as the plan asks; the kill criterion\n709\tis that structure must beat it. Iteration 6's leaked run is not a baseline\n710\tfor anything and its numbers are not quoted.\n711\t### Where the gap is\n712\tBy collection: Chu & Stuckey 102 of 200 at gap ≥ 2 (51.0%, mean gap 5.4),\n713\tFaggioli–Bentivoglio 83 of 300 (27.7%), Wilson 3 of 20, SCOOP 3 of 24,\n714\tChallenge 3 of 46, Harvey 71 of 2,130 (3.3%), Simonis 73 of 3,630 (2.0%),\n715\tShaw and Miller none. On the 338 gap ≥ 2 rows the component bounds fall\n716\tshort by a mean of 4.8 (`lb_best`, median 2), 7.4 (`lb_contraction`, median\n717\t3) and 14.4 (`lb_trivial`, median 7); on the 4,909 tight rows\n718\t`lb_contraction` alone is tight on 84.8% and the trivial bound on 4.8%.\n719\t### The classifier\n720\tGrouped by file ∪ class, all 5,247 rows, 338 positives (file-only grouping\n721\tin `reports/bound_gap_tables.md`; it is uniformly higher, by up to 0.07 AUC,\n722\tfor the reason §2 gives — the collections are separable regions and a\n723\theld-out file has isomorphic neighbours in the training folds):\n724\tWithin the eleven mixed `(n, m)` cells — 2,407 rows, 218 positives; the\n725\tcells are 20×10 (13 positives of 515), 20×20 (4/433), 30×10 (86/478), 30×15\n726\t(35/328), 30×30 (8/444), 40×20 (11/91), 40×40 (6/18), 50×50 (12/28), 50×100\n727\t(5/20), 75×75 (17/22) and 100×100 (21/30):\n728\tStructure beats density-and-size in every row of both tables, so the kill\n729\tcriterion is not met — but the margin is in *average precision*, which\n730\tdoubles (0.26 → 0.52 for the tree, 0.35 → 0.77 for the EBM, 0.54 → 0.81\n731\twithin cells), not in AUC, where the baseline is already 0.85–0.96 because a\n732\tgap of two is first of all a large-and-sparse phenomenon. Adding the three\n733\tlower bounds to the features changes nothing (structure + invariants 0.983\n734\t→ 0.980 with them; tree identical), which is §4's finding again from the\n735\tother side: the bounds carry no information about their own failure that\n736\tthe structure does not. The 11–20 band is the hard one for every model (17\n737\tpositives among 2,508) and the one where the size-free set matters most for\n738\tthe tree (0.688 → 0.908).\n739\t### What the gap instances look like at fixed size\n740\tEach size-free feature alone, z-scored within its `(n, m)` cell, ranking\n741\tgap ≥ 2 against tight over the 2,407 rows; AUC below 0.5 means the gap\n742\tinstances have *less* of it. The top of the table:\n743\tAt fixed `(n, m)` the gap instances are the sparser ones — fewer edges, a\n744\tsmaller degeneracy, a lower Fiedler value, lower clustering — with **more\n745\tdispersed degrees** (`g_deg_cv` is the one feature the gap instances have\n746\tmore of, d = +1.48, which is §5's residual variable found independently).\n747\tAnd `rig_density_ratio`, the observed MOSP-graph density over what a random\n748\tintersection graph of the same `(n, m, p̂)` would have, is at chance (0.505):\n749\tthe gap instances are exactly as dense as the random model predicts for\n750\ttheir matrix density. Nothing about the graph is anomalous given the\n751\tmatrix; it is the sparsity itself, and the fringe of low-degree customers\n752\tthat sparsity produces, that the bound cannot see. The EBM within the cells\n753\tranks `g_deg_std` first (0.89), then `g_clustering` (0.71), `row_mean`\n754\t(0.57) and `density` (0.52).\n755\tThe depth-3 tree on the size-free features within the mixed cells reads:\n756\t```\n757\trig_edge_prob ≤ 0.685 (sparse):\n758\t    one component (g_components/n ≤ 0.042)                    → gap ≥ 2\n759\t    several components: max degree > (n − 1)/2                 → gap ≥ 2, else tight\n760\trig_edge_prob > 0.685 (dense):\n761\t    min degree ≤ 0.397 (n − 1) and one component               → gap ≥ 2\n762\t    otherwise                                                  → tight\n763\t```\n764\tIn words: **a connected sparse MOSP graph, or a dense one with a low-degree\n765\tcustomer**. Both branches say the same thing — the pathwidth is set by the\n766\tconnected bulk and the degree-based bounds by the fringe — and the tree\n767\treaches AUC 0.931 within cells with that rule alone (0.849 for the\n768\tdensity-and-size tree).\n769\t### The treewidth ceiling\n770\tBoth theorem checks pass on all 6,376 rows. **On 131 of the 338 gap ≥ 2\n771\tinstances, and on 315 of the 1,129 gap-1 instances, pathwidth provably\n772\texceeds treewidth**, so no lower bound on treewidth — and three of the four\n773\tcomponents of `lb_best` are exactly that — can ever be tight there. The 35\n774\ttight instances above the ceiling are the 35 where `lb_best` exceeds\n775\t`tw_min_fill + 1`: the expansion bound, the one component that argues about\n776\tprefixes of an ordering rather than about degrees, certified an optimum\n777\tstrictly above the treewidth. The fraction certified is a floor, since\n778\tmin-fill is only an upper bound on treewidth; by band among the gap ≥ 2\n779\tinstances it is 58.8% at ≤ 20 customers, 43.6% at 21–30, 57.4% at 31–60 and\n780\t5.7% at 61–134, where min-fill runs far above the pathwidth (mean\n781\t`optimum − (tw_min_fill + 1)` is negative there) and the certificate\n782\tloosens. Whether the *un*certified gap ≥ 2 instances also have `pw > tw` is\n783\topen; exact treewidth at these sizes would settle it.\n784\t### Clusters\n785\tk-means over the standardised size-free features of the 338 gap instances\n786\tchooses k = 2 by silhouette (0.370; 0.335–0.344 for k = 3–5), and the two\n787\tclusters are the two halves of instance space §2 already drew: a *sparse*\n788\tcluster of 201 (matrix density 0.079, MOSP-graph density 0.181, mean\n789\toptimum/n 0.336, mean gap 6.3, max 30; Chu & Stuckey 85, Faggioli–Bentivoglio\n790\t83, Harvey 23, the three Wilson, Challenge and SCOOP each, one Simonis) and a\n791\t*dense* cluster of 137 (density 0.238, graph density 0.536, optimum/n 0.614,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"85602c8d-df04-47a8-a103-12762ff7e492","uuid":"5d493d6b-8d73-4c34-af18-ead66bf0fba0","timestamp":"2026-09-26T19:23:18.143Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/85602c8d-df04-47a8-a103-12762ff7e492/tool-results/bqdzzn2cu.txt","content":"# Machine learning and the nature of MOSP — findings\n*Started 2026-09-25. The report `reports/ml_nature_plan.md` lays out the\nquestions; this file collects the answers, one section per Ralph-loop\niteration, appended and never rewritten. Where a later section contradicts an\nearlier one it says so and the earlier one stays. Every number here is\nregenerated by the command named in its section. Nothing in this file is a\nbound.*\n## 1. Isomorphism classes: how many instances does the corpus really hold? (§2.1a)\n*Iteration 1, 2026-09-25. Code: `learning/canonical.py`. Regenerate:*\n```bash\npip install pynauty                      # optional; without it only WL hashes are computed\npython -m learning.canonical --workers 16 --out reports/canonical_tables.md\n```\n*Under a second on 16 cores. Writes `learning/data/canonical.csv` (one row per\ninstance: matrix digest, bipartite and MOSP-graph nauty certificates, WL hash,\nautomorphism group order) and the tables below.*\n**Question.** The optimum is `pathwidth(MOSP graph) + 1`, so two instances with\nisomorphic MOSP graphs have the same optimum and are, for every question about\nthe optimum, one instance. How many distinct instances does the corpus of 6,376\ncertified optima actually contain, per collection and per size, and do\nisomorphic instances carry equal optima?\n**Method.** Three nested notions of \"the same instance\", each a canonical form:\n(1) the matrix byte for byte; (2) the matrix up to renaming customers and\nproducts, as a nauty certificate of the two-coloured bipartite graph\ncustomers–products; (3) the MOSP graph up to renaming customers, as a nauty\ncertificate of `M·Mᵀ > 0`. Alongside (3), the 3-iteration Weisfeiler–Lehman\nhash from networkx, which is cheap and what graph kernels and message-passing\nGNNs can see, but is not a complete invariant. Classes are counted per\ncollection, per `(n_customers, n_patterns)` and per size band, and within every\nlevel-3 class the optima are compared.\n**Baseline.** The count everything else in this repository has been using:\n6,376. And, for the WL hash, nauty's exact class count.\n### Corpus totals\n### Per collection\nWithin-collection counts hide the cross-collection duplicates, which the next\ntable shows: `MOSP_Instances/Challenge` is 46 distinct instances *on its own*\nand zero new ones for the corpus.\n### Classes shared across collections (level 2)\nAll 44 byte-identical duplicates are these: `MOSP_Instances/Challenge` is a copy\nof the Miller, Shaw and Wilson files of `ChallengeInstances2005`. The two\nChallenge instances not matched at level 2, `NWRS7_0` and `NWRS8_0`, are the\nWilson `NWRS7` and `NWRS8` with one all-zero product column removed\n(25×59 against 25×60); their MOSP graphs are isomorphic and their optima agree\n(10 and 16). So every one of the 46 Challenge instances is certified twice under\ntwo names, and the corpus count of 6,376 includes each of them twice. The\nremaining 19 level-2 duplicates are all inside Harvey (2,130 → 2,111), the only\ngenerator that re-emitted a matrix up to relabelling.\n### Per size band\n### Per (n_customers, n_patterns), sizes with at least 20 instances\n### Where the redundancy comes from: complete graphs\nThe four largest classes are the complete graphs K₁₀, K₁₅, K₃₀ and K₂₀, and\nthe next four are the same graphs minus one edge, optimum `n − 1`. A quarter of\nthe corpus is instances whose optimum is `n_customers` and whose MOSP graph is\ncomplete, which the trivial lower bound alone certifies if any single product is\nrequired by every customer, and which contraction degeneracy certifies\notherwise. Every one of them comes from Harvey or Simonis at densities that\nsaturate the graph; no other collection contains a single complete graph.\n### The WL hash against nauty\nAll five collisions are sparse Harvey instances with `edges = n` or `n − 1`,\nthat is, unions of paths and cycles, which 1-WL cannot tell apart when the\ncycle lengths differ. All 14 hidden classes have optimum 3. So on this corpus\n1-WL loses 0.4% of the classes and no information about the optimum, which\nbounds in advance what the §2.2 GNN check can lose to expressivity: nothing that\nshows in the optimum.\n### The audit: isomorphic instances have equal optima\n**Zero disagreements** over 3,667 classes, of which 203 have more than one\nmember and together hold 2,912 instances. Each redundant instance's certified\nvalue equals that of a class representative certified separately, 157 times\nacross different files and 46 times across different collections. This is not\nindependent of the solver, which produced both certificates, but it is\nindependent of the *run*: the Challenge copies were solved in different sweeps\nunder different names, and the giant Harvey + Simonis classes were certified in\nup to eleven files each. No instance needed re-certification, so none was\nre-solved and nothing was written to `solutions/`.\n**Finding.** The corpus holds 3,667 distinct MOSP graphs, not 6,376 instances:\n42.5% of it is isomorphic copies, and a quarter of it is complete graphs with\noptimum `n`. The redundancy is almost entirely a small-instance phenomenon,\n44.6% at `n ≤ 30` against 13.2% at `n > 30`, and is confined to Harvey and\nSimonis, whose generators at high density saturate the 10-to-30-customer graph,\nplus the 46 Challenge instances present under two names. Chu & Stuckey, SCOOP,\nShaw and Wilson contain no duplicates at any level. Two consequences for every\nstudy that follows. First, the effective sample size at `n ≤ 30` is 3,287, and\nat `n > 30` it is 380, of which 200 are Chu & Stuckey. Second, grouping by\n`source_file` does not isolate isomorphic siblings: 157 graph classes span more\nthan one file, and the largest spans eleven, so a \"grouped\" split still trains\non copies of its test instances. `learning/data/canonical.csv` now carries a\n`graph_cert` column, and the grouped protocol in `learning/study_optimum.py`\nshould group by it (or drop to one representative per class) before any\ngrouped number from `reports/learning.md` is quoted again; that is a change to\nthe split, not to any solver default, and is left for the study that next\ntouches the split. The 150 graph classes containing more than one matrix class,\n2,796 matrix classes in all, are free material for §2.9 (same graph, different\nclique cover). The audit found what it should: no pair of isomorphic instances\ncarries different certified optima.\n**Size range covered.** All 6,376 instances, 9 ≤ n ≤ 134. The redundancy\nfigures are dominated by `n ≤ 30` (5,938 instances); at `n > 30` they rest on\n438 instances and 380 classes.\n**Not a bound, not a solver change.** Nothing here touches `_lower_bound` or any\ndecision path; `learning.canonical` is analysis only.\n## 2. Generator fingerprint and the map of instance space (§2.1b)\n*Iteration 2, 2026-09-25. Code: `learning/fingerprint.py`. Regenerate:*\n```bash\npip install umap-learn                   # optional; without it the map falls back to PCA\npython -m learning.dataset               # if learning/data/instances.csv is stale, ~20 s\npython -m learning.canonical --workers 16 --csv learning/data/canonical.csv   # for the leak-free split\npython -m learning.fingerprint --out reports/fingerprint_tables.md\n```\n*About 50 s on 16 cores. Writes `reports/figures/instance_space.png`,\n`learning/data/instance_space.csv` (the 2-D coordinates, git-ignored) and the\nraw tables in `reports/fingerprint_tables.md`, from which every number below is\ncopied.*\n**Question.** Can a classifier tell which collection an instance came from\nusing only its structure? If it can, the benchmark families occupy different\nregions of instance space and a result measured on one says nothing about\nanother. Its accuracy is the finding; the classifier is used for nothing.\n**Method.** The label is the sub-collection, nine of them: Harvey, Simonis,\nShaw, Wilson and Miller from `ChallengeInstances2005`; Chu & Stuckey,\nFaggioli–Bentivoglio, SCOOP and the `Challenge` copies from `MOSP_Instances`.\nFeatures are the 28 structure-only columns of `learning.features` (matrix\nshape and degree statistics, MOSP-graph statistics; no bound), and, separately,\na 19-column *size-free* set in which every count is divided by the dimension it\nscales with (`col_cv = col_std / col_mean`, `g_degeneracy / (n − 1)` and so on)\nand `n`, `m`, `n_ones`, `g_nodes`, `g_edges` are dropped. The model is a random\nforest of 300 trees; gradient boosting was tried first and scored 0.81 on a\nrandom split where the forest and LightGBM both score 0.98, thrown by the\nclasses of one and twenty instances, so it was replaced. Three splits, all\nshuffled: grouped by `source_file` as the loop's rules require; grouped by the\nconnected components of files and MOSP-graph isomorphism classes from §1\n(512 groups against 617 files), which is the split that does not leak\nisomorphic copies; and a random split, printed only to show the leak. Plain\n`GroupKFold` had to be replaced by a shuffled `StratifiedGroupKFold`: on the\nChu & Stuckey files, which sort as `Random-n-m-d-1, -2, …`, its round-robin\nassignment held out exactly one seed index per fold and made the control below\nscore exactly zero.\n**Baselines.** The majority class (Simonis, 56.9%); the two size columns\n`(n_customers, n_patterns)` alone; and a ceiling: features are constant on a\nmatrix-isomorphism class, so where a class holds rows from two collections the\nminority rows are wrong under any model. From §1's classes the ceiling is\n**0.993** (the 46 Challenge copies of Miller, Shaw and Wilson instances).\n### The collection from structure\nSize alone is *worse than the majority class* under a grouped split, because\nevery file is one `(n, m)` cell: hold the file out and its size has never been\nseen with its label. Structure with the size removed does as well as structure\nwith it, so the fingerprint is not the size.\nThe balanced accuracy of about 0.5 is these last four rows. Shaw and Miller are\none file each, so under any grouped split they are never in the training set\nwhen they are tested; Wilson's five files are five different sizes. All 25 Shaw\ninstances and 18 of 20 Wilson are predicted \"Challenge\", which is where their\nbyte-identical copies sit in the training folds: that is the ceiling, and the\nmodel finds it. These four collections cannot be fingerprinted from this corpus\nbecause there is nothing to generalise from, not because they are\nindistinguishable.\nConfusion among the generators that can be learned (rows true, columns\npredicted, structure (all), grouped by file, 6,376 instances):\nThe Simonis errors are two files: `problem_30_30` and `problem_40_20` lose 74\ninstances to Chu & Stuckey, and `problem_10_20` loses 104 to\nFaggioli–Bentivoglio. The 13 Chu & Stuckey errors are all `Random-30-30` at\ndensities 6 to 10, the densest of their smallest size, predicted Simonis. SCOOP\nis the only collection of real industrial instances and the only one whose\nerrors scatter over three generators.\n### What the fingerprint is\nPermutation importance on held-out files, accuracy points: `col_std` 0.196,\n`row_std` 0.147, `density` 0.114, `col_max_frac` 0.113, then nothing above\n0.02. A depth-3 tree on the size-free features scores **0.940** grouped by file\nand reads:\n```\ncol_cv <= 0.017                         -> Harvey\ncol_cv >  0.017 and row_cv <= 0.035     -> Harvey\notherwise, col_max_frac <= 0.290        -> Faggioli–Bentivoglio\notherwise                               -> Simonis\n```\nChecked directly against the files: **every Harvey instance has constant\ncolumn sums, constant row sums, or both**, and no instance of any other\ngenerator has either. Harvey's three file families are exactly these\nconstraints: `wbo_*` (every product has the same number of customers,\n64.3% of Harvey), `wbp_*` (every customer has the same number of products) and\n`wbop_*` (both, a biregular bipartite graph). Faggioli–Bentivoglio's largest\nproduct covers at most half the customers (`col_max_frac` 0.10 to 0.50, mean\n0.22); Simonis's covers 0.23 to 0.95, mean 0.65, at a mean density of 0.455.\nChu & Stuckey are recognised by their sparsity at sizes where nothing else is\nsparse. So three of the four large collections are separated by two lines a\nperson can state, and the fourth by density and size.\n### Collections at the same (n, m)\nRestricted to the eleven `(n, m)` cells where at least two collections each\ncontribute ten or more instances, so that size cannot help. Grouped by file.\nAt equal size the generators are told apart on 98.8% of instances; the 20×20\ncell is the Shaw/Challenge ceiling again, and 30×30 is where dense Chu & Stuckey\nmeets Simonis.\n### Within Chu & Stuckey: `Random-n-m-d-k`\nThe data says what the name means: `col_mean` tracks `d` to within 0.3 at\nevery size (`d` is the mean number of customers per product), and `k` is a\nseed index. Grouped by file, which is one instance per file, 200 instances.\nThe density class is recovered from `col_mean` alone at 98%, and adding the\nother 27 features adds nothing. The seed index sits at chance, as it must; a\nclassifier that could predict `k` would be reading the file, not the instance.\n(The size-only rows below 0.2 are the stratified split's arithmetic on a\nfeature that carries no signal, not information.)\n### The map\n![instance space](figures/instance_space.png)\nUMAP (`n_neighbors` 30, `min_dist` 0.1) of the standardised structure features,\nleft coloured by collection, middle by number of customers, right the same for\nthe size-free features. The map is not a continuum: it is a lattice of streaks,\none per `(n, m)` cell (74 cells in the corpus), which the middle panel shows\ndirectly. Within a streak the generators separate; across streaks the layout is\nsize. In the size-free panel the cells merge and the collections form their own\nregions, with Chu & Stuckey at the sparse end of the Simonis band and\nFaggioli–Bentivoglio and Wilson beside them.\nNeighbourhood purity, the share of an instance's 10 nearest neighbours that\ncarry its collection:\nThe map keeps what the feature space has. The 24 SCOOP instances, the only\nreal-world data, have almost no SCOOP neighbours: they sit inside the sparse\nHarvey and Faggioli–Bentivoglio regions. Of the twelve k-means regions of the\nmap (`reports/fingerprint_tables.md`), one holds all 200 Chu & Stuckey\ninstances together with 431 Harvey, 161 Faggioli–Bentivoglio, 113 Simonis and 9 SCOOP at\nmean density 0.24 and mean `optimum / n` 0.60: the sparse, low-optimum corner is\nwhere the hard instances live and where the industrial ones fall too. The\nregions that are pure Simonis (mean density 0.45, `optimum / n` 0.8 to 0.94)\nare the complete and near-complete graphs of §1.\n**Finding.** The benchmark families are fingerprintable: 94.4% of instances are\nassigned to their collection from structure alone with the file held out\n(93.3% with every isomorphic copy held out too), and 98.8% at equal size, where\nthe baseline is 61.6%. The fingerprint is not size and is not subtle. Harvey is\na regularity constraint (constant column or row sums, present in all 2,130 of\nits instances and in no other generator's); Faggioli–Bentivoglio caps the\nlargest product at half the customers; Simonis is dense with large products;\nChu & Stuckey is sparse where nothing else is, and within it the density class\nis `col_mean` to within rounding while the seed index is at chance. What cannot\nbe fingerprinted is what has no siblings to learn from: Shaw, Miller and Wilson\nare one file or one size each and their copies under the Challenge name set a\nceiling of 0.993 that the model hits exactly. The consequence for every study\nthat follows is the one the plan anticipated: a result on Harvey is a result\nabout biregular-ish random bipartite graphs, a result on Simonis is about dense\nrandom matrices at 10 to 40 customers, and neither transfers to the sparse\nregion where Chu & Stuckey, the timeouts and the only industrial instances all\nsit. The instance-space map says the corpus is a lattice of 74 size cells with\nlarge empty regions between them, not a sample from a distribution over\ninstances; §2.4's generated ensembles are what fills it.\n**Size range covered.** All 6,376 instances, 9 ≤ n ≤ 134. The fingerprint\nnumbers rest on the four large collections (6,260 instances, 9 ≤ n ≤ 125); the\nsame-size test covers 10 ≤ n ≤ 40 only; the Chu & Stuckey result covers its\n30 ≤ n ≤ 125. Nothing here has been re-certified because no finding depends on\nany instance's optimum.\n**Not a bound, not a solver change.** The classifier and the embedding are\ndescriptive; nothing touches `_lower_bound` or any decision path, and nothing\nwas written to `solutions/`.\n## 3. Node counts into the ledger (§2.4 prerequisite)\n**Question.** Can the hardness of a decision call be recorded in a unit that\nsurvives a change of hardware, and what does that unit look like on the part of\nthe corpus where certification is cheap? §2.4 wants nodes visited by the\nrefutation at `optimum − 1`, not seconds; before this item the complete\ncustomer search counted its branch decisions and no driver stored them\n(`reports/ml_nature_plan.md` §0, \"node counts are printed, not stored\").\n**Method.** Instrumentation, not a study. The search itself is unchanged: its\n`Decision.nodes` and `Solution.nodes` were already returned by both the Python\nand the C path. What changed is every seam above them.\n- `satisfiability.mosp_solver.solve_mosp_exact` takes an optional `stats` dict\n  and fills it with `nodes`, `seconds`, `proof` and `procedure`. Its return\n  value is unchanged, so every existing caller keeps unpacking two values.\n  `nodes` is `None`, not 0, for a cached answer and for the SAT procedure, which\n  has none.\n- `benchmarks.compute.record` writes a fifth ledger column, `nodes`, and takes\n  rows of `(instance, seconds)` or `(instance, seconds, nodes)`; a driver with\n  no search behind it (`reheuristic`) leaves the cell empty. A new\n  `ensure_fields` migrates an older ledger in place once, appending the column\n  and leaving every existing row empty; `python -m benchmarks.compute\n  --migrate-ledger` runs it, and `record` runs it before appending.\n- `benchmarks.csearch`: the worker posts `result.nodes` as a sixth element of\n  its row and `sweep` records it; the first five fields are unchanged, which is\n  what `benchmarks.marathon` indexes.\n- `benchmarks.recertify`: already returned `nodes` from its worker and wrote it\n  into `recertify/results.json` and the solution file's `recertified` note; it\n  now also appends one ledger row per finished entry, as it lands, with the\n  seconds and nodes of the call, under `--ledger` (default the project's).\n- `learning/node_counts.py` checks the instrumentation end to end: it\n  summarises the ledger's `nodes` column per driver, then runs `decide` at\n  `optimum − 1` on every certified instance with at most 40 customers under\n  the two configurations whose counts reach the ledger (`decide` defaults, as\n  `solve_mosp_exact` runs; and the `csearch` driver's rule, Theorem 2 on sparse\n  instances with every candidate a dominator), recording status, nodes and\n  seconds. The status is a free audit: `sat` there would mean the stored\n  optimum is wrong.\n**Baseline.** The ledger before this item: 279 rows, 174 from `csearch` and 105\nfrom `reheuristic`, none carrying a node count. That is the row every later\n§2.4 run is compared against, and it is the first table below.\n### The compute ledger on the day the column landed\nThe 279 existing rows now read `\"\"` in `nodes`. The compute total is unchanged\nat 667 core-hours (`python -m benchmarks.compute --quote`), since nodes are not\ncompute and are not summed.\n### The audit: every stored optimum at n ≤ 40 refutes at optimum − 1\n12,270 decision calls, all `unsat`, none at the 10 s deadline; the whole run\ntakes about a second on 16 workers. This is a second independent refutation\nfor 6,135 of the 6,376 certified optima, under two configurations, and it\nagrees with the corpus everywhere. It does not touch the 241 instances above\n40 customers, which is where the two known false-refutation incidents were.\n### Nodes to refute optimum − 1, by size band\n### Nodes to refute optimum − 1, by collection\n### The two configurations, nodes per instance\n**Finding.** The node count now comes back through every path a decision call\ntakes and lands in the ledger from both drivers that make decision calls; the\nsearch itself was not touched and the corpus was not written to. What the\nfirst look shows is how little of the small corpus is a search problem at all:\n38.5% of the 6,135 refutations at `optimum − 1` visit **zero** nodes (53.7% at\nn ≤ 10, falling to 25% at 31–40), meaning the root is refuted by the search's\nown cost check before any branch, and 2,357 of those 2,360 are Challenge\ninstances, where §1 found 1,669 complete graphs. Where the search does branch,\nthe count grows about an order of magnitude per ten customers at the tail\n(p90: 4 → 23 → 108 → 1,764) while the median stays in single or double digits,\nand the single worst call, 29,138 nodes on `Random-40-40-2-2_0`, is at Chu &\nStuckey density 2: over the fifty `Random-30` and `Random-40` instances the\nmedian nodes fall monotonically with density (1,068 → 428 → 140 → 22 → 6 from\ndensity 2 to 10), which is the direction §2.4 predicts and the sign that the\nhardness peak, if there is one, sits at or below density 2 for these sizes.\nThe `csearch` driver's configuration never costs more than 1.1× the defaults\nand is cheaper on 1,302 instances, so mixing the two in one ledger is a small\ndistortion, and the `config` is recorded per row so it can be separated. None\nof this is the §2.4 study: density is confounded with collection here (§2),\nnothing is deduplicated by isomorphism class (§1 says 42.5% of the corpus is\nredundant), and 50 instances at two sizes is not a density sweep.\n**Size range covered.** The audit and the tables cover the 6,135 certified\ninstances with 2 ≤ optimum and n ≤ 40 (9 ≤ n ≤ 40), which is 96% of the corpus\nby count and none of the instances on which any run has ever taken more than a\nsecond. The ledger change covers every future row of every size. Nothing was\nre-certified because nothing disagreed; the run itself is a re-refutation of\nevery optimum it touched.\n**Not a bound, not a solver change.** No default changed; `_lower_bound` and\nevery decision path are untouched; nothing was written to `solutions/`. The\nrunning `benchmarks.recertify` process (PIDs under `pgrep -f\nbenchmarks.recertify`) predates these edits and was not restarted, so it will\nnot write `nodes` rows; its `recertify/results.json` carries the counts for\nthose calls.\n**Regenerate.** `python -m benchmarks.compute --migrate-ledger` (once;\nidempotent), then `python -m learning.node_counts --max-customers 40 --out\nreports/node_count_tables.md` (about 1 s on 16 workers; writes\n`learning/data/node_counts.csv`). The by-density line: group\n`learning/data/node_counts.csv` rows whose `instance_name` starts with\n`Random-` by the fourth dash-separated field. Tests: `python -m pytest\ntests/test_node_counts.py -q`.\n## 4. Pathwidth-adjacent invariants as features (§2.2a)\n*Iteration 4, 2026-09-25. Code: `learning/features.py` (group `invariants`),\n`learning/invariants_study.py`. Regenerate:*\n```bash\npython -m learning.dataset --workers 16                                   # 6,376 x 49 features, ~90 s\npython -m learning.invariants_study --workers 16 --out reports/invariants_tables.md   # ~140 s\npython -m pytest tests/test_invariants.py -q\n```\n*Writes `learning/data/instances.csv` (git-ignored) and the tables below.\n`learning.study_optimum` and `learning.fingerprint` leave the new group out by\ndefault, so `reports/learning.md` §1 and §2 above still regenerate from the\ncolumns they report.*\n**Question.** The optimum is `pathwidth(MOSP graph) + 1`, and §1 of\n`reports/learning.md` found that of the 36 features it is the MOSP graph's\nedge count and mean degree that carry the signal. Which of the invariants\nthat pathwidth is known to sit beside carry more of it, and do they explain\nthe part of the optimum the proved bound misses?\n**Method.** Thirteen new columns in a fourth feature group: the min-fill and\nmin-degree elimination widths (`tw_min_fill`, `tw_min_degree`, networkx's\ntreewidth heuristics; upper bounds on treewidth, which is at most pathwidth,\nso bounds on nothing here); the bandwidth of the reverse Cuthill–McKee order\n(`bw_rcm`; bandwidth is at least pathwidth, so `bw_rcm + 1` is a valid upper\nbound on the optimum and the corpus is checked against that); the adjacency\nspectral radius and the Laplacian Fiedler value, on the whole graph and on\nits largest component; two edge clique cover counts (`cc_products`, the\ndistinct products containing an edge, which is the upper bound the item asks\nfor, and `cc_greedy`, after dropping every product all of whose edges another\nproduct also covers); a balanced separator (`sep_size`, the smallest\none-sided boundary over the middle-third cuts of the Fiedler order and of the\nRCM order, and `sep_frac = sep_size / n`); and the random intersection graph\nquantities: with `p̂ = density` and `(n, m) = (n_customers, n_patterns)`\nalready in the matrix group, the pairwise edge probability\n`1 − (1 − p̂²)^m`, the expected degree `(n − 1)` times it, and the ratio of the\nobserved MOSP-graph density to that probability. The four greedy invariants\nrun on a copy relabelled by a label-free key (degree, neighbour degrees,\ntwo-hop degree sums) so tie-breaks do not depend on the file's row order, and\nthe residual order-dependence is measured by re-featuring 623 shuffled\ninstances (400 at random plus all 241 with `n ≥ 50`, minus overlap). The\nablation fits the same `HistGradientBoostingRegressor` as `learning.study_optimum`\non four feature sets under three splits: grouped by file, grouped by file ∪\nMOSP-graph isomorphism class (`learning.fingerprint.union_groups`), and\nrandom for contrast; on the optimum and on the residual `optimum − lb_best`.\nPermutation importance is on a held-out fifth of the file ∪ class groups.\n**Baseline.** The 36-feature model, grouped by file (`reports/learning.md`\n§1), and the solver's own `lb_best` and `ub_best` as point estimates. Note\nthat the table was rebuilt: `lb_best` now includes the expansion bound\n(higher on 1,329 rows than the table §1 of `reports/learning.md` was computed\nfrom) and `Random-100-100-2-2_0` carries its corrected optimum of 20, so the\nbaseline rows below are the current corpus, not the September 22 numbers.\n### Invariants as point estimates of the optimum\n`below`/`above` count instances where the estimate sits under/over the\noptimum. A valid lower bound has `above = 0`; a valid upper bound `below = 0`.\n### The leading estimates by size band\n### Ablation: the same model with and without the invariants\n`delta_mae` is against the same feature set without the group; negative is\nbetter; the plan's kill criterion is 0.02. `mae_gap_rows` restricts the\nscoring to the 1,467 instances where `ub_best > lb_best`.\nThe file ∪ class grouping is far harsher than the file grouping because it is\nfar more unbalanced: its largest group holds 1,620 instances (25.4% of the\ncorpus; the complete graphs of §1 chain Harvey and Simonis files together)\nand its five largest hold 88.0%, against 8.6% and 43.1% for the file\ngrouping. `GroupKFold` keeps a group whole, so the file ∪ class split is in\neffect a five-region hold-out, and its absolute numbers are extrapolation\nerrors between generators; the grouped-by-file rows are the ones comparable\nwith `reports/learning.md`.\n### By size band (grouped by file ∪ class, target optimum)\n### Permutation importance, all 49 features, target optimum\nMAE points on a held-out fifth of the file ∪ class groups; the top 20, then\nwhere every invariant landed.\nInvariants' ranks: `tw_min_fill` #1 (2.011), `tw_min_degree` #6 (0.064),\n`spectral_radius` #10 (0.025), `bw_rcm` #11 (0.024), `cc_greedy` #19\n(0.005), `sep_frac` #21 (0.005), `rig_deg_expected` #24 (0.002),\n`rig_density_ratio` #30 (0.001), `cc_products` #31 (0.000), `rig_edge_prob`\n#34 (0.000), `fiedler` #41 (−0.000), `sep_size` #47 (−0.002), `fiedler_lcc`\n#48 (−0.003).\nOn the residual `optimum − lb_best` the ranking is `bound_gap` 0.283,\n`bound_gap_frac` 0.229, `ub_cs_dfs` 0.020, then `bw_rcm` 0.017 as the first\ninvariant, `density` 0.016, `sep_size` 0.010, `tw_min_fill` 0.007 and\n`fiedler` 0.007: nothing in the group moves the residual by more than two\nhundredths of a stack.\n### Each invariant alone, with the two sizes (grouped by file ∪ class)\n### Order-dependence of the greedy invariants\n623 instances re-featured after shuffling customers and products.\n**Finding.** One invariant carries almost all of what the group adds, and it\nis the min-fill elimination width. `tw_min_fill + 1` is, on this corpus, a\ncloser point estimate of the optimum than either of the solver's own bounds:\nexact on 85.7% of the 6,376 instances against 84.6% for `ub_best` and 77.0%\nfor `lb_best`, at MAE 0.188 against 0.239 and 0.431, and it leads in every\nsize band, including the 120 instances with 61–134 customers where it is off\nby 2.6 stacks on average against 3.0 for the upper bound and 8.7 for the\nlower. It is a bound on nothing: it sits below the optimum on 481 instances\n(by up to 3) and above it on 428 (by up to 7), and treewidth being at most\npathwidth does not make a treewidth *heuristic* either. In the model it is\nthe single most important feature of all 49 at 2.01 MAE points, above\n`ub_cs_dfs` at 1.21 and `lb_best` at 0.43, and alone with the two sizes it\npredicts the optimum better under the harshest grouping (MAE 0.997, exact\n73.7%) than `lb_best` with the same sizes does (1.081, 68.6%). The plan's\nkill criterion of 0.02 MAE is cleared by an order of magnitude on the\nstructure-only set — grouped by file the MAE falls from 0.436 to 0.220 and\nthe exact rate rises from 71.2% to 87.1%, which is to say that structure plus\ninvariants, with no solver bound computed, now predicts the optimum as well as\nstructure plus the bounds did (0.191, 88.3%) — and cleared narrowly or not at\nall once the bounds are already present (−0.012 by file, −0.044 by file ∪\nclass). On the **residual over the proved bound the group adds nothing**:\n−0.004, −0.000 and −0.009 MAE, and no invariant moves that target by more than\n0.017 points. So the invariants know what the bound knows, expressed\ndifferently, and not what it is missing. Where the residual does correlate\nwith anything it is with the random-model sparsity: the pairwise edge\nprobability `1 − (1 − p̂²)^m` has Spearman −0.52 with the gap, the separator\nfraction −0.42 and the Fiedler value −0.32, so the bound falls short where the\ngraph is sparse and weakly connected, which is a direction for §2.3 rather\nthan an explanation. Of the rest: `tw_min_degree` (#6), the spectral radius\n(#10) and `bw_rcm` (#11) each add a few hundredths; the Fiedler value, the\nseparator, both clique cover counts and the random intersection parameters\ncontribute nothing once min-fill is present, and the random-model expected\ndegree predicts the optimum worse (MAE 2.13) than the observed mean degree\n(1.47), with the density ratio spanning 0.49–1.38, which says again what §2\nsaid: these graphs are not random intersection graphs at their own `(n, m,\np̂)`. `bw_rcm + 1` was never below the optimum on any of the 6,376 instances,\nas bandwidth ≥ pathwidth requires — a corpus-wide consistency check against a\ntheorem that cost nothing — and it is exact on 51.4% of them. The relabelling\nby a label-free key makes the greedy invariants order-independent in practice:\nover 623 shuffled instances the two treewidth heuristics and the separator\nnever changed, the RCM bandwidth changed once by one, and only `cc_greedy`\nmoved (26 instances, by at most 2), which is documented and harmless since it\ncarries no importance.\n**Size range covered.** 9–134 customers, 5,938 of 6,376 at n ≤ 30. The\nablation and importance tables are dominated by the small instances; at 31–60\ncustomers (318 instances) every model's grouped MAE is above 4 and at 61–134\n(120 instances) above 26, so no model here predicts the optimum at those\nsizes and the only claim that reaches them is the point-estimate comparison\nin the by-band table. The order-dependence check covers every instance with\nn ≥ 50.\n**Not a bound, not a solver change.** No default changed; `_lower_bound` and\nevery decision path are untouched; nothing was written to `solutions/`. No\nfinding rests on a handful of instances, so nothing was re-certified; the\n`bw_rcm + 1 ≥ optimum` check is a whole-corpus property and it passed.\n**For §2.2b.** The residual is the target the formula search should aim at,\nand this section says which columns to leave out of it: the treewidth\nheuristics track the bound, not the gap. The candidates with any signal on the\ngap are `rig_edge_prob`, `sep_frac`, `fiedler`, `cc_greedy` (ρ = 0.38) and\n`bw_rcm`.\n## 5. A formula for the residual, and for the optimum (§2.2b)\n*Iteration 7, 2026-09-25 (iteration 5 wrote the search and was cut short before\nits run finished; this iteration finished, fixed the run and wrote it up).\nCode: `learning/formula_search.py`. Regenerate:*\n```bash\npython -m learning.dataset --workers 16                                  # if the table is stale (~90 s)\npython -m learning.formula_search --workers 16 --out reports/formula_tables.md   # ~400 s; --no-pysr for the enumerated part alone (~7 min)\npython -m pytest tests/test_formula_search.py -q\n```\n*Writes `reports/formula_tables.md` (every table below, in full). The\nenumerated rows regenerate to the digit; the PySR rows do not, because\nmultithreaded PySR is not deterministic even with a seed, and see the note on\nJulia crashes under Method.*\n**Question.** §4 found that a boosting model on structure and the\npathwidth-adjacent invariants predicts the optimum as well as the solver's\nbounds do, and that no invariant explains the residual `optimum − lb_best`\nbeyond what the bound knows. A boosting model explains nothing. Is there a\n*closed-form* formula in structure-only features for the residual, and one for\nthe optimum, and is either clean enough to state as a conjecture?\n**Method.** Two feature pools, both without any `lb_*`, `ub_*` or `bound_*`\ncolumn: *structure* (38 columns: the matrix and MOSP-graph features minus\n`g_nodes`, which equals `n_customers`, plus the §4 invariants minus the two\ntreewidth heuristics) and *structure + treewidth heuristics* (40). Two\ntargets: `optimum − lb_best` (structure pool only, since §4 showed the\ntreewidth heuristics track the bound, not the gap) and `optimum` (both pools).\nTwo searches. (1) An **enumerated** search over every product and ratio of at\nmost three features, `f₁^e₁ · f₂^e₂ · f₃^e₃` with exponents in\n{−2, −1, −½, ½, 1, 2} for one feature, {−1, −½, ½, 1, 2} for two and {−1, 1}\nfor three, a negative exponent only on a strictly positive column: 58,221\nterms on the structure pool, 68,690 with the treewidth heuristics. Each term\n`t` is scored as the two-parameter fit `y ≈ a·t + b`, constants fitted on the\ntraining folds and error measured on the held-out folds of a five-fold grouped\nsplit: least squares as the first pass over every term, then least absolute\ndeviation (the metric is MAE and the target's tail pulls a least-squares line\noff the bulk) for the leading 150, ranked by that. A second stage fits\n`y ≈ a·t₁ + b·t₂ + c` over all pairs of those 150. Splits: grouped by\n`source_file`, grouped by file ∪ MOSP-graph isomorphism class\n(`learning.fingerprint.union_groups`, §1), and random for contrast. Because the\nsearch *selects* a term by its grouped error, that error is optimistic, so a\n**nested** protocol runs the whole search inside each outer training fold and\nscores its winner on the held-out files; those are the honest numbers.\n(2) **PySR** (`pip install pysr`, optional; Julia bootstraps on first use) with\n`+ − × ÷`, `sqrt`, `square`, `log`, L1 loss, maxsize 20, on every fifth file in\nsorted order held out (124 files, 719 instances), its whole Pareto front scored\non the held-out rows beside the enumerated winner and boosting fitted on the\nsame training rows. Finally the two invariants both searches keep returning\nare tabulated with their **constants fixed by hand** rather than fitted, so\nthere is nothing to hold out. *A note on the PySR runs:* PySR 2.5 on this\nmachine crashed inside Julia's garbage collector on six of seven fits at 16\nthreads and 10,000 iterations (segfault or abort, one of them on the\nwall-clock stop path), whichever process it ran in; at 4 threads and 2,000\niterations every fit finished. Each fit now runs in a child process with one\nretry, the tables above it are written to disk before PySR starts, and the\ndefaults are the ones that survive.\n**Baseline.** LightGBM on the same pool and the same grouped folds (the item's\nbaseline), the constant formula `residual = 0` (which is `lb_best` itself, MAE\n0.431), and `tw_min_fill + 1`, the best point estimate §4 found (MAE 0.188).\n**The searches** (pooled held-out MAE / exact rate of the LAD fit `y ≈ a·t + b`;\n`file` = grouped by file, `class` = grouped by file ∪ isomorphism class,\n`random` leaks isomorphic copies across folds):\nThe formulas do not care which split they are scored under, to three decimals;\nboosting does, badly, under the class grouping (which is a five-region\nhold-out in practice, §4). The LAD constants fitted on all rows are\n**Nested** (the formula selected on the training files only, scored on the\nheld-out files; the honest grouped MAE) and the winners per outer fold:\nSelection inflates the monomials' error by at most 0.001, so the grouped\nnumbers above are what a fresh file should expect.\n**Point estimates of the optimum**, grouped by file, constants refitted per\nfold, the residual formulas added to `lb_best`; `below`/`above` count rounded\nestimates under and over the optimum (a valid lower bound has `above = 0`):\nBy size band (MAE, grouped by file):\nBy collection the residual monomial beats boosting only on Harvey (0.266 vs\n0.274) and Simonis (0.172 vs 0.184), the two collections that are 90% of the\ncorpus and almost all at n ≤ 30; it loses on every other: Faggioli–Bentivoglio\n0.505 vs 0.426, Chu & Stuckey 2.430 vs 1.191, SCOOP 1.245 vs 0.519, Wilson\n1.329 vs 0.286.\n**The sandwich**, constants fixed by hand (nothing fitted, nothing held out).\nDegeneracy ≤ treewidth ≤ pathwidth and bandwidth ≥ pathwidth, so\n`g_degeneracy + 1` is a proved lower bound on the optimum and `bw_rcm + 1` a\nproved upper bound; the corpus is checked against both, and the optimum is\n`g_degeneracy + 1 + λ · (bw_rcm − g_degeneracy)` for some λ ∈ [0, 1]:\n**PySR** on the held-out fifth of the files (719 instances; the enumerated\nmonomial and pair selected by grouped CV on the training files only, boosting\nfitted on the same rows):\n\"Best on held-out\" picks among the ≤ 20 equations of a Pareto front by their\nscore on the test rows, so it is optimistic by that selection; PySR's own pick\nis chosen from training loss alone. The fronts themselves are in\n`reports/formula_tables.md`. For the residual the front reads, in order of\ncomplexity: `0`, `spectral_radius − g_deg_mean`, `(0.19 · g_deg_std)²`,\n`0.09 · (bw_rcm − g_degeneracy)`, `(0.07 · (bw_rcm − g_deg_mean))²`, then\n`g_deg_std` times a squared sparsity term from complexity 7 on — the degree\nspread, and the width of the sandwich, and nothing else.\n**Finding.** There is no clean formula for the residual. The best closed form\nin the structure features is `optimum − lb_best ≈ 0.01 · g_deg_std · sep_size /\ng_density − 0.02`, grouped MAE 0.303 (0.304 nested) against 0.431 for the\nconstant zero and 0.260 for LightGBM on the same pool: a formula recovers\nthree quarters of what boosting recovers, and its constants are not ones\nanybody would conjecture. What the term says is intelligible even so. Since\n`g_density = g_deg_mean / (n − 1)`, it is `(n − 1) · sep_size · σ_deg / μ_deg`:\nthe residual grows with the *dispersion of the degree sequence*, times the\nseparator size. That is the same fact §4 reached through correlations and\n`CLAUDE.md` records from the bound side, that the degree-based bounds saturate\nat the average degree, now with the degree spread as the explicit variable:\non the 1,721 instances where the term is zero the gap is zero on 99.8%, and\non the 1,705 degree-regular graphs it is zero on every one — but those are\ncomplete graphs, unions of cycles and one Miller graph, where a tight bound\nis a triviality rather than a finding, so no conjecture is stated for the\nresidual. For the *optimum*, the clean formula exists and is the same in both\nsearches: with the treewidth heuristics allowed it is `tw_min_fill + 1`, LAD\nconstants (1.000, 1.000), MAE 0.188, and nothing the enumerator or the pair\nstage adds improves it beyond 0.187; without them it is\n`1 + √(g_degeneracy · bw_rcm)`, again with constants exactly (1, 1), chosen in\nall five outer folds, MAE 0.672 and exact on 67.1% — the geometric mean of a\nproved lower bound and a proved upper bound on the optimum. That is the\nfinding worth keeping: the two invariants the searches converge on sit on\nopposite sides of the optimum, `degeneracy + 1 ≤ optimum ≤ bw_rcm + 1` holds\non all 6,376 instances (as the two theorems behind it require, a\nwhole-corpus consistency check that passed), they coincide and force the\noptimum on 2,823 of them, and where they differ the optimum sits at the\nmidpoint on median, with interquartile range ⅓–⅔, its position λ tracking\nsparsity (Spearman 0.40 with the separator fraction) and not size (0.05).\nPySR's whole Pareto front for the optimum is that same statement written out,\n`g_degeneracy + 1 + λ(·) · (bw_rcm − g_degeneracy)` with λ a function of\n`sep_frac`, `row_mean` or `n_ones`; in this run PySR's own pick for the optimum from structure alone is\n`distinct_row_frac + sqrt(g_degeneracy · bw_rcm)`, the enumerated formula\nfound by a second method, and with the treewidth heuristics allowed its pick\nis `tw_min_fill + 1`, held-out MAE 0.224 against boosting's 0.229. The one\nplace a formula beats boosting is the residual on the held-out files: PySR's\n`((g_components · rig_edge_prob · distinct_row_frac − 0.89)² · distinct_row_frac · g_deg_std)²`\nscores 0.235 against boosting's 0.276, selected on the test rows and so\noptimistic, and it is again the degree standard deviation times a sparsity\nterm; its own pick, `(0.07 · (bw_rcm − g_deg_mean))²` at 0.321, says the gap\ngrows with the square of how far the bandwidth runs above the mean degree. **None of this is a\nbound.** `1 + √(g_degeneracy · bw_rcm)` is below the optimum on 1,495 instances\nand above it on 600, by up to 34 and 9; the residual formula added to\n`lb_best` overshoots on 237; the geometric mean is *worse* than `lb_best` at\n61–134 customers (13.06 vs 8.71), because on the sparse Chu & Stuckey graphs\n`bw_rcm` runs far above the pathwidth. And the one clean lower bound in it,\n`g_degeneracy + 1`, is dominated by the contraction degeneracy + 1 that\n`_lower_bound` already computes (contraction degeneracy ≥ degeneracy), so\nthere is nothing here to hand to the bound work; a conjecture of the form\n\"pathwidth ≥ f(·)\" would have to come from a different family than degree\nstatistics, which is what §4's kill statement already said.\n**Size range covered.** 9–134 customers, 5,938 of 6,376 at n ≤ 30. Every\ngrouped MAE in the searches is dominated by that band. At 31–60 (318\ninstances) the residual formula is at 0.627 against boosting's 0.483 and\n`lb_best`'s 1.132; at 61–134 (120 instances) it is 3.88 against 1.64 and 8.71,\nand no formula here predicts the optimum at that size. The sandwich\ninequalities and the forced-optimum count are whole-corpus properties and\nhold at every size; the λ ≈ ½ statement rests on 3,169 instances at n ≤ 30,\n266 at 31–60 and 118 above, with the same median in each band to within 0.1.\n**Not a bound, not a solver change.** No default changed; `_lower_bound` and\nevery decision path are untouched; nothing was written to `solutions/`. No\nfinding rests on a handful of instances: the two inequalities and the forced\ncount are corpus-wide, and the 36 non-complete regular graphs are all\ncertified by their bound (`lb_best = optimum`), which is a proof independent\nof any search refutation, so nothing was re-certified.\n**For §2.3 and the next loop.** The residual's variable is degree dispersion,\nso the gap-vs-tight classifier of §2.3 should start from `g_deg_std / g_deg_mean`\nand `sep_size` beside `rig_edge_prob` and `sep_frac`. For §2.5 (does the optimum\nconcentrate on random instances) the sandwich is the natural frame: on\n`G(n, m, p)` the degeneracy and RCM bandwidth have their own concentration\nresults, and the question becomes whether λ concentrates at ½.\n## 6. Where the bounds fail (§2.3)\n*Iteration 8, 2026-09-25 (iteration 6 wrote `learning/bound_gap.py` and its\ntests and ran it, but ended before reading the run; its classifier tables\nscored 1.000 everywhere because the study's own label column `y` had been\nswept into the \"structure\" feature set by an every-numeric-column selector.\nThis iteration fixed the leak, added a guard and a test for it, added the\nsize-free feature set, the treewidth ceiling and the per-instance treewidth\nand simplicial counts, re-ran, and wrote this section.) Code:\n`learning/bound_gap.py`. Regenerate:*\n```bash\npython -m learning.dataset --workers 16                                    # if the table is stale (~90 s)\npython -m learning.canonical --workers 16                                  # the isomorphism classes (~1 s)\npython -m learning.bound_gap --workers 16 --out reports/bound_gap_tables.md   # ~120 s; --no-recertify skips the re-solve of the ten\npython -m pytest tests/test_bound_gap.py -q\n```\n*Writes `reports/bound_gap_tables.md` (every table below in full, plus the ten\ninstances drawn) and `learning/data/bound_gap.csv` (git-ignored, the 338 gap\ninstances with their cluster). Nothing is written to `solutions/`: the\nre-certification calls `solve_mosp_exact` with the corpus directory, which\nre-verifies the cached witness by simulation, and refutes `optimum − 1` through\n`learning.node_counts.refute`, which writes nothing.*\n**Question.** `lb_best` — the best of the trivial bound, the clique bound,\ncontraction degeneracy + 1 and the expansion bound — equals the optimum on\n77.0% of the corpus, misses by one on 17.7% and by two or more on 5.3% (338\ninstances). Do the instances it misses by two or more share a recognisable\nstructure, and which of the four component bounds is blind to it?\n**Method.** The label is `gap = optimum − lb_best` from the feature table,\nthree-way (tight, gap 1, gap ≥ 2); the classifier sees tight against gap ≥ 2\nwith the gap-1 rows left out, 5,247 rows and 338 positives. Three models — a\ndepth-3 decision tree (balanced class weight, 20 rows per leaf), an\nexplainable boosting machine (`interpret`, soft import, no interactions) and\n`HistGradientBoostingClassifier` as a ceiling — on five feature sets: the\nplan's baseline `n_customers`, `n_patterns`, `density`; the 28 matrix and\nMOSP-graph features; those plus the 13 invariants of §4; a 32-column\n*size-free* set (`learning.fingerprint.size_free_features` plus the invariants\ndivided by their dimension, so a tree can split on `g_deg_cv` or `bw_rcm / n`);\nand structure + invariants + the three lower bounds. No upper bound anywhere,\nsince `ub_best` equals the optimum on 85% of the corpus and `ub − lb` would be\nthe label in disguise. Out-of-fold probabilities under `GroupKFold` grouped by\nfile ∪ MOSP-graph isomorphism class (`learning.fingerprint.union_groups`, the\nhonest split) with the file-only grouping beside it; AUC, average precision\nand balanced accuracy, whole and per size band. Because gap ≥ 2 never occurs\nbelow 20 customers and is the norm above 60, the same models are scored again\non the 2,407 rows in the eleven `(n, m)` cells holding at least three of each\nclass, where size cannot be the answer. Then each size-free feature alone,\nz-scored within its cell, as a ranker of gap ≥ 2 against tight over those\ncells (AUC and Cohen's d); the EBM's term importances there; the depth-3\ntrees as text; k-means over the standardised size-free features of the 338\ngap instances with k by silhouette; and the ten smallest gap ≥ 2 instances\n(fewest customers, then fewest ones, one per isomorphism class), drawn as\nmatrices with their degree sequences, every bound recomputed from the\ninstance, the number of customers in exactly one product, and a treewidth\nupper bound from 200 randomised min-fill / min-degree eliminations. Each of\nthe ten is re-certified: the exact solver's value and an independent\nrefutation of `optimum − 1`.\nOne table not in the plan came out of a consistency check. The trivial bound\nis a clique, the clique bound is a clique, and contraction degeneracy is a\nlower bound on treewidth (Bodlaender, Koster & Wolle), so three of the four\ncomponents of `lb_best` are lower bounds on **treewidth + 1**, while\n`tw_min_fill + 1` from §4 is an upper bound on it. Hence\n`optimum > tw_min_fill + 1` certifies `pathwidth > treewidth` on that\ninstance, with no heuristic in the certificate (min-fill is an upper bound,\nthe optimum is exact). The \"treewidth ceiling\" table counts those\ncertificates per gap class and checks the two inequalities the theorems\nrequire; the module raises if either is violated.\n**Baseline.** Density and size alone, as the plan asks; the kill criterion\nis that structure must beat it. Iteration 6's leaked run is not a baseline\nfor anything and its numbers are not quoted.\n### Where the gap is\nBy collection: Chu & Stuckey 102 of 200 at gap ≥ 2 (51.0%, mean gap 5.4),\nFaggioli–Bentivoglio 83 of 300 (27.7%), Wilson 3 of 20, SCOOP 3 of 24,\nChallenge 3 of 46, Harvey 71 of 2,130 (3.3%), Simonis 73 of 3,630 (2.0%),\nShaw and Miller none. On the 338 gap ≥ 2 rows the component bounds fall\nshort by a mean of 4.8 (`lb_best`, median 2), 7.4 (`lb_contraction`, median\n3) and 14.4 (`lb_trivial`, median 7); on the 4,909 tight rows\n`lb_contraction` alone is tight on 84.8% and the trivial bound on 4.8%.\n### The classifier\nGrouped by file ∪ class, all 5,247 rows, 338 positives (file-only grouping\nin `reports/bound_gap_tables.md`; it is uniformly higher, by up to 0.07 AUC,\nfor the reason §2 gives — the collections are separable regions and a\nheld-out file has isomorphic neighbours in the training folds):\nWithin the eleven mixed `(n, m)` cells — 2,407 rows, 218 positives; the\ncells are 20×10 (13 positives of 515), 20×20 (4/433), 30×10 (86/478), 30×15\n(35/328), 30×30 (8/444), 40×20 (11/91), 40×40 (6/18), 50×50 (12/28), 50×100\n(5/20), 75×75 (17/22) and 100×100 (21/30):\nStructure beats density-and-size in every row of both tables, so the kill\ncriterion is not met — but the margin is in *average precision*, which\ndoubles (0.26 → 0.52 for the tree, 0.35 → 0.77 for the EBM, 0.54 → 0.81\nwithin cells), not in AUC, where the baseline is already 0.85–0.96 because a\ngap of two is first of all a large-and-sparse phenomenon. Adding the three\nlower bounds to the features changes nothing (structure + invariants 0.983\n→ 0.980 with them; tree identical), which is §4's finding again from the\nother side: the bounds carry no information about their own failure that\nthe structure does not. The 11–20 band is the hard one for every model (17\npositives among 2,508) and the one where the size-free set matters most for\nthe tree (0.688 → 0.908).\n### What the gap instances look like at fixed size\nEach size-free feature alone, z-scored within its `(n, m)` cell, ranking\ngap ≥ 2 against tight over the 2,407 rows; AUC below 0.5 means the gap\ninstances have *less* of it. The top of the table:\nAt fixed `(n, m)` the gap instances are the sparser ones — fewer edges, a\nsmaller degeneracy, a lower Fiedler value, lower clustering — with **more\ndispersed degrees** (`g_deg_cv` is the one feature the gap instances have\nmore of, d = +1.48, which is §5's residual variable found independently).\nAnd `rig_density_ratio`, the observed MOSP-graph density over what a random\nintersection graph of the same `(n, m, p̂)` would have, is at chance (0.505):\nthe gap instances are exactly as dense as the random model predicts for\ntheir matrix density. Nothing about the graph is anomalous given the\nmatrix; it is the sparsity itself, and the fringe of low-degree customers\nthat sparsity produces, that the bound cannot see. The EBM within the cells\nranks `g_deg_std` first (0.89), then `g_clustering` (0.71), `row_mean`\n(0.57) and `density` (0.52).\nThe depth-3 tree on the size-free features within the mixed cells reads:\n```\nrig_edge_prob ≤ 0.685 (sparse):\n    one component (g_components/n ≤ 0.042)                    → gap ≥ 2\n    several components: max degree > (n − 1)/2                 → gap ≥ 2, else tight\nrig_edge_prob > 0.685 (dense):\n    min degree ≤ 0.397 (n − 1) and one component               → gap ≥ 2\n    otherwise                                                  → tight\n```\nIn words: **a connected sparse MOSP graph, or a dense one with a low-degree\ncustomer**. Both branches say the same thing — the pathwidth is set by the\nconnected bulk and the degree-based bounds by the fringe — and the tree\nreaches AUC 0.931 within cells with that rule alone (0.849 for the\ndensity-and-size tree).\n### The treewidth ceiling\nBoth theorem checks pass on all 6,376 rows. **On 131 of the 338 gap ≥ 2\ninstances, and on 315 of the 1,129 gap-1 instances, pathwidth provably\nexceeds treewidth**, so no lower bound on treewidth — and three of the four\ncomponents of `lb_best` are exactly that — can ever be tight there. The 35\ntight instances above the ceiling are the 35 where `lb_best` exceeds\n`tw_min_fill + 1`: the expansion bound, the one component that argues about\nprefixes of an ordering rather than about degrees, certified an optimum\nstrictly above the treewidth. The fraction certified is a floor, since\nmin-fill is only an upper bound on treewidth; by band among the gap ≥ 2\ninstances it is 58.8% at ≤ 20 customers, 43.6% at 21–30, 57.4% at 31–60 and\n5.7% at 61–134, where min-fill runs far above the pathwidth (mean\n`optimum − (tw_min_fill + 1)` is negative there) and the certificate\nloosens. Whether the *un*certified gap ≥ 2 instances also have `pw > tw` is\nopen; exact treewidth at these sizes would settle it.\n### Clusters\nk-means over the standardised size-free features of the 338 gap instances\nchooses k = 2 by silhouette (0.370; 0.335–0.344 for k = 3–5), and the two\nclusters are the two halves of instance space §2 already drew: a *sparse*\ncluster of 201 (matrix density 0.079, MOSP-graph density 0.181, mean\noptimum/n 0.336, mean gap 6.3, max 30; Chu & Stuckey 85, Faggioli–Bentivoglio\n83, Harvey 23, the three Wilson, Challenge and SCOOP each, one Simonis) and a\n*dense* cluster of 137 (density 0.238, graph density 0.536, optimum/n 0.614,","numLines":791,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1208,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}}}
