{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J9DZ7DJdaqmiWSTsYwD7Be","type":"tool_result","content":"792\tmean gap 2.6, max 16; Simonis 72, Harvey 48, Chu & Stuckey 17). The\n793\tsilhouette is weak and the split is by collection, so the gap instances are\n794\tnot a family of their own in feature space; they are the sparse end of each\n795\tgenerator's range, which is what the within-cell contrast says with more\n796\tprecision.\n797\t### The ten smallest\n798\tAll ten have 20 customers and 10 products — six from Simonis's\n799\t`problem_20_10.dat` (densities 0.20–0.30) and four from Harvey (three\n800\t`wbo_20_10`, \"balanced orders, four customers per product\", and one\n801\t`wbp_20_10`, \"balanced products, three products per customer\"); the 17\n802\tgap ≥ 2 instances at n ≤ 20 all come from those two generators' 20×10 and\n803\t20×20 files. Every one has gap exactly 2, and each was re-certified: the\n804\texact solver returns the cached value and the customer search refutes\n805\t`optimum − 1` in 20–59 nodes. The matrices are in\n806\t`reports/bound_gap_tables.md`; the summary:\n807\tWhat they have in common, in words. Each is **one connected graph made of\n808\tten small cliques — the products, 4 to 10 customers each — glued together at\n809\ta few hub customers, with a fringe of customers that belong to one or two\n810\tproducts only.** The hubs are the customers in four to six products (one to\n811\tfour of the twenty); the fringe is the 2–11 customers in exactly one product,\n812\twhich are simplicial vertices whose whole neighbourhood is that product's\n813\tclique. The clique bound never exceeds the largest product (trivial = clique\n814\tin all ten), so no two products overlap enough to form a larger clique; the\n815\texpansion bound never exceeds the contraction bound except once, by one; and\n816\t`bw_rcm + 1` is 4 to 6 above the optimum, so the bandwidth heuristic is no\n817\thelp either. On eight of the ten the pathwidth provably exceeds the treewidth\n818\t— Warwick 871 is the cleanest: ten 4-cliques on twenty customers, eleven of\n819\tthem in a single product, treewidth exactly 3 (a K4 forces ≥ 3, min-fill\n820\tgives ≤ 3), pathwidth 5. That is the structure of a *tree of cliques with\n821\tbranching*: the treewidth of a tree of cliques is the largest clique minus\n822\tone, which every degree-based bound sees, while the pathwidth grows with how\n823\tmany branches must be held open at once when the tree is swept from one end\n824\tto the other, which none of them sees. The two not separated by the\n825\theuristic (HS 590 and Warwick 955, the only one with no fringe and degrees\n826\t10–16) may still have `pw > tw`; the 200-restart heuristic could not show\n827\tit. The gap of exactly 2 is the smallest a gap ≥ 2 instance can have; at\n828\tthis size the family is a one-in-fifty event (13 of 710 in the 20×10 cell,\n829\twhere tight instances have mean MOSP-graph density 0.86 and degree\n830\tcoefficient of variation 0.12 against 0.51 and 0.32 for the gap ≥ 2 ones).\n831\t**Finding.** The instances the proved bound misses by two or more are\n832\tsparse, connected, degree-dispersed MOSP graphs at the low-density end of\n833\teach generator's range — trees of cliques with branching, glued at hub\n834\tcustomers and fringed with single-product customers — and the reason the\n835\tbound misses them is structural, not statistical: three of its four\n836\tcomponents are lower bounds on treewidth, and on 131 of the 338 (and on 8 of\n837\tthe 10 smallest) the pathwidth is certifiably larger than the treewidth, so\n838\tthose components could not be tight under any tie-break or budget. A\n839\tdepth-3 tree on size-free features reads that description off the table\n840\t(\"connected and sparse, or dense with a low-degree customer\") and separates\n841\tgap ≥ 2 from tight at AUC 0.931 within fixed `(n, m)` cells against 0.849\n842\tfor density and size; the EBM reaches 0.971 with average precision 0.81\n843\tagainst 0.54. The lower bounds themselves add nothing to the classifier. The\n844\tonly component that can exceed the treewidth ceiling is the expansion bound,\n845\twhich is a pathwidth argument, and it did so on 36 instances; if the bound\n846\twork is ever resumed, that is the family to strengthen, and the clique and\n847\tdegree families are provably the wrong place (§5 said the same about\n848\tdegree statistics from the residual side). This does not contradict §4 or\n849\t§5; it explains them.\n850\t**Size range covered.** 9–134 customers, 5,938 of 6,376 at n ≤ 30. Gap ≥ 2\n851\tdoes not occur below 20 customers, so every statement about the gap\n852\tinstances covers 20–134 (17 instances at 20, 133 at 21–30, 101 at 31–60, 87\n853\tat 61–134). The within-cell tables rest on eleven cells from 20×10 to\n854\t100×100 and the per-band AUCs are in the tables above; the ten drawn\n855\tinstances are all 20×10, so the \"tree of cliques\" description is established\n856\tat n = 20 and *consistent* with, not certified at, larger sizes: the\n857\ttreewidth certificate covers 44–59% of the gap ≥ 2 instances at n ≤ 60 and\n858\t5.7% above, where min-fill is too loose to certify anything. The cluster\n859\tstatement covers all 338.\n860\t**Not a bound, not a solver change.** No default changed; `_lower_bound` and\n861\tevery decision path are untouched; nothing was written to `solutions/` (the\n862\tre-certification of the ten read the cache and re-verified it, and `git\n863\tstatus solutions/` is clean); the running `benchmarks.recertify` was not\n864\ttouched. The two theorem checks in the treewidth-ceiling table are the only\n865\t\"bound\" statements here and both are on record as consistency checks of\n866\tthe feature code, not as new bounds. The ten instances the description rests\n867\ton were each re-solved and independently refuted at `optimum − 1`.\n868\t**For the next loop.** §2.7 (extremal search) has its objective: maximise\n869\t`optimum − lb_best` at n ≤ 15 starting from trees of cliques, with\n870\t`optimum − (tw_ub + 1)` as a second objective, since a 12-customer instance\n871\twith `pw − tw ≥ 2` and a proof of it is exactly the kind of object one can\n872\treason about. §2.5 should record `tw_ub` alongside the optimum on\n873\t`G(n, m, p)` to measure how `pw − tw` scales with sparsity. And exact\n874\ttreewidth for the 207 uncertified gap ≥ 2 instances (82 of them at n ≤ 30,\n875\tcheap with a proper treewidth solver) would turn the 38.8% floor into a\n876\tnumber.\n877\t## 7. Distilling the closing policy (§2.6a)\n878\t*Iteration 9, 2026-09-25 (an earlier iteration wrote `learning/distil.py` and\n879\tended without running it; this iteration found that its worker pool deadlocked\n880\t— forking after LightGBM has run leaves the children asleep in libgomp — and\n881\tthat its MCN tie-break used the total product count where `least_cost_node`\n882\tuses the products not yet produced; fixed both, added the fidelity-fitted\n883\tmodels, the two named rules, the `cs-dfs` reference columns, the per-collection\n884\tand head-to-head tables, wrote the tests, ran it and wrote this section.)\n885\tCode: `learning/distil.py`. Regenerate:*\n886\t```bash\n887\tpython -m learning.distil --workers 16                 # ~4 min: fits 60 s, evaluation 140 s\n888\tpython -m learning.distil --workers 16 --lex-depth 2   # ~3 min, the 91-rule family only\n889\tpython -m pytest tests/test_distil.py -q\n890\t```\n891\t*Writes `reports/distil_tables.md` (every table below in full, the fitted\n892\tweights per fold and the trees of every fold) and `learning/data/distil.csv`\n893\t(git-ignored; one row per evaluated instance with every strategy's value).\n894\tNothing is written to `solutions/`, no solver file is touched.*\n895\t**Question.** `learning/policy.py` imitates the 2.24 M closing decisions of the\n896\tcertified witnesses with a LightGBM ranker over eight local features and, used\n897\tgreedily, cuts MCN's error over the optimum by two thirds. What rule did it\n898\tlearn? Does a depth-3 tree or a linear scorer keep most of the gain, does a\n899\tlexicographic rule a person can state, and do the two static hypotheses of the\n900\tplan — Fiedler-vector order, BFS layers from a minimum-degree root — hold?\n901\t**Method.** The protocol of `python -m learning.policy evaluate`: five folds\n902\tgrouped by source file with the same seed, up to 400 held-out instances per\n903\tfold (1,920 in all; fold 3's files hold 320), the ranker and every distilled\n904\tmodel fitted per fold on the training files' decision rows (1.6–2.0 M rows per\n905\tfold, 6,372 certified witnesses at run time). Every closing order is turned\n906\tinto a product order with `product_order_from_customers` and simulated on the\n907\toriginal instance with `max_open_stacks`; the statistics are over the optimum.\n908\tDistilled models: a depth-3 classification tree and a logistic scorer fitted\n909\tto the witness decisions (`tree`, `linear`, and `linear-r` with its weights\n910\trounded to two decimals), and a depth-3 regression tree and least squares\n911\tfitted to the *ranker's own raw scores* on the same rows (`tree-fid`,\n912\t`linear-fid`), which is distillation in the strict sense. Lexicographic rules:\n913\tall 570 orderings of up to three of the five per-candidate features\n914\t(`remaining_degree`, `newly_opened`, `already_open`, `total_degree`, products\n915\tnot yet produced), each key in either direction, MCN's own key — minimum\n916\tremaining degree, then fewest products left, then index — breaking every\n917\tremaining tie, so the empty rule is MCN itself (verified: it agrees with\n918\t`upper_bound(instance, \"mcn\")` on all 1,920). The rule is *selected* per fold on\n919\t400 training instances and scored held out (`lex-selected`); two rules are also\n920\tquoted by name, and their rows are descriptive because they were named after\n921\tseeing the pooled table. Static orders: Fiedler vector of each component\n922\t(sign fixed by the lowest-index customer) and its reverse; BFS from the\n923\tminimum-degree customer in FIFO order, in Cuthill–McKee order and reversed\n924\t(RCM); and minimum-degree elimination with fill-in, which is MCN made to\n925\tremember which open customers were opened together. The summary statistic is\n926\t`gain kept = (MAE_MCN − MAE_rule) / (MAE_MCN − MAE_ranker)`: 1 is the ranker, 0\n927\tis MCN. Reference columns: Chu & Stuckey's restricted DFS at 200,000 nodes\n928\tseeded with MCN (`cs-dfs`), with the ranker's order (`cs-dfs+lgbm`) and with\n929\tthe named rule's order (`cs-dfs+rule`).\n930\t**Baseline.** MCN, held out: MAE 1.616, exact 50.2%, worst +26. The ranker:\n931\t0.519, 75.0%, worst +34.\n932\t**Held-out constructions over the optimum** (1,920 instances, 9–134 customers).\n933\t**MAE by size band.**\n934\t**Head to head, held out** (instances where the first is below / equal / above the second).\n935\tWhere the rule loses to the ranker it loses by one on 82 of the 109 and by at\n936\tmost five; where it wins it wins by one on 181 of the 253 and by 20 or more on\n937\tfour. The ranker's +34 is GP8 (94 against an optimum of 60) and two of its\n938\t+20s are GP6 and GP7; the rule is exact on all three. That is the shape\n939\t`learning/policy.py`'s docstring already describes — a greedy with no search\n940\tbehind it — and the rule does not have it.\n941\t**Step-level agreement** with the witness (imitation) and with the ranker\n942\t(fidelity), over the 43,020 decisions with more than one candidate in the\n943\t1,920 held-out witnesses.\n944\t**What the fitted models say.** The logistic scorer is the same in all five\n945\tfolds to two decimals: `+1.00·already_open − 0.07·remaining_degree −\n946\t0.03..0.05·newly_opened + 0.01·total_degree` (per-step constants aside; `open_after\n947\t= open_now + newly_opened − 1`, so it has four effective weights). Read as a\n948\trule: *a customer whose stack is already open; then fewer unclosed neighbours;\n949\tthen fewer new stacks* — MCN's criterion with \"already open\" in front of it —\n950\tand it keeps 82% of the gain. The depth-3 tree splits on `open_after ≤ 6.5`,\n951\t`newly_opened ≤ 1.5`, `remaining_degree ≤ 2.5 / 10.5`, `already_open` and\n952\t`n_customers`, and its leaf probabilities fall with `newly_opened` and with\n953\t`remaining_degree`: *fewest new stacks, then smallest remaining degree*. With\n954\teight leaves it ties constantly, and the ties are what decide it: broken by\n955\tMCN's key it keeps 57%, broken by index it is worse than MCN. The two models\n956\tfitted to the ranker's scores rather than to the witness keep less (48%, 34%);\n957\t`linear-fid`'s `already_open` weight ranges from −2.3 to +8.9 across folds,\n958\tso the ranker's raw score is not close to linear in the features. And the\n959\tranker itself imitates the witness at only 45.3% of decisions, below MCN's\n960\t52.5%, while the one-key rule imitates at 62.3% and the tree at 58.5%: the\n961\timitation rate ranks these constructions almost inversely to their value,\n962\twhich is the first thing to know before building another imitation policy.\n963\t**The rule.** 64 of the 570 lexicographic rules beat the ranker's MAE: all 57\n964\twhose first key is `min newly_opened` (the worst of them 0.424) and 7 whose\n965\tfirst key is `max already_open`, and no others; the five per-fold selections\n966\tall carry `min newly_opened` first or second. The two named rules:\n967\t- *Open the fewest new stacks.* `newly_opened` is `|N[c] \\ opened|`, the\n968\t  stacks closing `c` would open for the first time; minimising it minimises the\n969\t  open stacks after the step. That is the cheapest-first order in which Chu &\n970\t  Stuckey's `ub_MOSP` expands candidates (`restricted_dfs`'s docstring), so\n971\t  followed greedily with MCN's tie-break it is that search's *first leaf*.\n972\t  Held out: 0.419, exact 79.1%, worst +11 — already better than the ranker\n973\t  on every statistic. (Pattern-level heuristics that open the fewest new\n974\t  stacks go back to Yuen; whether this customer-closing form with this\n975\t  tie-break is in the literature is not settled here.)\n976\t- *Open the fewest new stacks; on ties, the customer with the most unclosed\n977\t  neighbours.* The tie-break is the **reverse of MCN's**: MCN closes the\n978\t  lowest remaining degree, this closes the highest. Among candidates that open\n979\t  equally few stacks, the one with many unclosed neighbours is the one whose\n980\t  neighbourhood is already mostly open, so closing it retires the most of the\n981\t  current frontier. Held out: **0.348, exact 80.7%, worst +9**, 115% of the\n982\t  ranker's gain, better than the ranker on 253 instances and worse on 109,\n983\t  better than MCN on 896 and worse on 8, and better than the same rule with\n984\t  MCN's tie-break on 159 against 86. It is the ranker's equal or better in\n985\t  every size band and in every collection but Shaw (0.36 vs 0.32 on 25\n986\t  instances); on the 91 Chu & Stuckey instances it is 2.42 against the\n987\t  ranker's 3.35 and MCN's 9.44. It matches the 200,000-node DFS on exact\n988\t  solutions (80.7% vs 81.2%) though not on MAE (0.348 vs 0.311) or worst case\n989\t  (+9 vs +7). As the DFS's *incumbent* it is worth more than the learned\n990\t  order: `cs-dfs+rule` 0.127 / 92.7% against `cs-dfs+lgbm` 0.157 / 91.0%, 82\n991\t  instances better and 38 worse, and it never does worse than the MCN-seeded\n992\t  DFS on more than 2 of 1,920.\n993\tThe honest number is the per-fold selection, which does not see the held-out\n994\tinstances: 0.362, 79.2%, worst +9, 114% of the gain. The selected rules were\n995\t`min newly_opened, max already_open, max remaining_degree`; `min newly_opened,\n996\tmax remaining_degree, min already_open` (twice); `min newly_opened, max\n997\ttotal_degree, min patterns`; `max already_open, min newly_opened, min patterns`.\n998\tFold 3's choice generalised worst (train 0.265, held out 0.722, still below the\n999\tranker's 1.259 on those files).\n1000\t**The static hypotheses.** Fiedler order — sort the customers of each\n1001\tcomponent by the Fiedler vector, no state at all — keeps 66% of the gain (0.886,\n1002\t64.5%), more than the depth-3 tree and more than the logistic scorer's rounded\n1003\tform loses; reversed it keeps 52%. BFS from a minimum-degree root does not:\n1004\tFIFO order is worse than MCN, Cuthill–McKee keeps 9%, RCM 46%. So the plan's\n1005\tfirst hypothesis is partly right and its second is wrong, and both are far from\n1006\tthe best rule. Minimum-degree elimination with fill-in is worse than MCN\n1007\t(−34%): remembering that the open neighbours of a closed customer stay open\n1008\ttogether makes the greedy worse, not better.\n1009\t**Finding.** The kill criterion — no readable rule keeps more than half the\n1010\tranker's gain — is not met; it is exceeded, and in the direction the plan did\n1011\tnot anticipate. The ranker did not learn something a rule cannot express; it\n1012\tlearned, imperfectly, the cheapest-first order of Chu & Stuckey's own DFS, and\n1013\ta two-key rule a sentence long — *close the customer that opens the fewest new\n1014\tstacks, and among those the one with the most unclosed neighbours* — beats it\n1015\ton every statistic, at every size, in every collection but one, with no model,\n1016\tno training and no dependency. The distilled tree and scorer are worse than\n1017\tthe rule because imitation is the wrong objective: the models fitted to the\n1018\twitness reproduce MCN's minimum-degree preference, which the witnesses share\n1019\tat the level of individual decisions (imitation 52–62%) but which is the wrong\n1020\ttie-break for the construction as a whole (159 better / 86 worse for the\n1021\treverse). This is the third time in this report that a learned component has\n1022\tresolved into a finding about the solver rather than a component of it\n1023\t(`reports/learning_plan.md`): here the finding is a heuristic in the\n1024\tliterature's sense, and a better DFS seed than the imitation policy that\n1025\t`reports/learning.md` measured.\n1026\t**Size range covered.** 9–134 customers; 1,768 of the 1,920 held-out\n1027\tinstances have ≤ 30, 96 have 31–60, 56 have 61–134. The rule's lead over the\n1028\tranker holds in each band (0.207 vs 0.282; 1.010 vs 1.573; 3.679 vs 6.179),\n1029\tand grows with size, but above 60 customers it rests on 56 instances and the\n1030\trule's own error there is 3.7 stacks with a worst case of +9 (Chu & Stuckey\n1031\t100×50, `Random-100-50-4-3_0`, 38 against an optimum of 29); at that size\n1032\tneither it nor the ranker is close to the optimum without the search behind\n1033\tit. The imitation and fidelity rates are over all 1,920 witnesses and are\n1034\tdominated by the small instances.\n1035\t**Not a bound, not a solver change.** Nothing here touches `_lower_bound` or\n1036\tany decision path; every value in every table is an upper bound obtained by\n1037\tsimulation on the original instance; no default changed; nothing was written\n1038\tto `solutions/` (`git status solutions/` clean after the run); the running\n1039\t`benchmarks.recertify` (12 processes) was not touched. The rule is not\n1040\tregistered as a heuristic in `satisfiability/heuristics.py`: that would be a\n1041\tsolver change, and it is left for the loop's owner.\n1042\t**For the next loop.** (i) The rule costs what MCN costs and needs no model,\n1043\tso it is the natural replacement for `learned+cs-dfs` as the DFS seed:\n1044\t`learning.corpus_sweep` over all 6,376 with the rule seeding `restricted_dfs`,\n1045\tagainst the 709-better / 13-worse that `learned+cs-dfs` scored over `cs-dfs`,\n1046\twould settle whether the LightGBM dependency decision left open in\n1047\t`reports/learning.md` needs deciding at all. (ii) Any future imitation study\n1048\tshould select by construction value, never by step accuracy; §2.6b (item 08)\n1049\tshould measure how many optimal closing orders there are before anyone\n1050\timitates \"the\" witness again, because a 45%-imitation policy beating a\n1051\t62%-imitation rule says the witnesses disagree with each other. (iii) The\n1052\ttie-break's direction is a small, statable conjecture about the customer\n1053\tsearch: among cheapest candidates, expanding the highest remaining degree\n1054\tfirst should reach the optimum sooner; `restricted_dfs` orders its fan\n1055\tcheapest-first with ties by customer index (`scored.sort()` on `(cost,\n1056\tcustomer, ...)`), and `learning.node_counts.refute` is the primitive to\n1057\tmeasure it with.\n1058\t## 8. Degeneracy of the optimum: how many orderings are optimal? (§2.6b)\n1059\t*Iteration 10, 2026-09-25. Code: `learning/degeneracy.py`. Regenerate:*\n1060\t```bash\n1061\tpython -m learning.degeneracy --workers 16          # ~90 s; corpus n ≤ 15 and 256 generated\n1062\tpython -m learning.degeneracy --tables-only         # the tables again from learning/data/degeneracy.csv\n1063\tpython -m pytest tests/test_degeneracy.py -q\n1064\t```\n1065\t*Writes `reports/degeneracy_tables.md` (every table below in full) and\n1066\t`learning/data/degeneracy.csv` (git-ignored; one row per instance with every\n1067\tcount). The generated instances' witnesses are persisted under\n1068\t`learning/data/degeneracy_solutions/`, never under `solutions/`; no solver file\n1069\tis touched.*\n1070\t**Question.** `learning/policy.py` imitates \"the\" witness of each certified\n1071\tinstance, and §7 found a ranker imitating 45% of witness decisions beating a\n1072\trule imitating 62%. That is only possible if many closing orders are optimal\n1073\tand the one in `solutions/` is an arbitrary member. How many are there, how\n1074\tdoes the count depend on size and density, is the optimum ever unique up to\n1075\treversal, and what does that imply for imitation?\n1076\t**Method.** Three exact counts per instance, each a path count through the\n1077\tsubset lattice rather than an enumeration of permutations, so the counts are\n1078\texact where `n!` and `m!` are not enumerable: (i) **closing orders,\n1079\tconstruction value** — permutations of the customers whose product order,\n1080\tbuilt by `product_order_from_customers` exactly as `learning.policy` builds\n1081\tit, simulates on the original instance to the optimum; the cost of closing\n1082\t`c` from the closed set `S` depends on `(S, c)` only, by simulating the block\n1083\tof `c`'s unproduced products; (ii) **closing orders, search measure** — the\n1084\tsame under Chu & Stuckey's `max_i |O(S_i) − S_{i−1}|`, which the complete\n1085\tsearch branches on and which over-charges orders that place an already\n1086\tfinished customer late; (iii) **product orders** — permutations of the\n1087\tproducts with open-stack peak equal to the optimum, over the `2^m` lattice of\n1088\tproduced sets in numpy, for `m ≤ 20`. Each lattice's bottleneck minimum is a\n1089\tfree audit of the certified optimum. Reversal is a symmetry only of (iii): it\n1090\treverses the open-stack profile, so the count is even and \"unique up to\n1091\treversal\" means two orders after dividing by the factorials of identical\n1092\tcolumns; (i) is divided by the factorials of identical rows (twins), (ii) by\n1093\t`|Aut(G)|` from `learning/data/canonical.csv`, on which the automorphism group\n1094\tacts freely. Along every witness's induced closing order the module counts,\n1095\tat every step, the number of remaining customers that begin an optimal\n1096\tcompletion (`choices`); `mean(1 / choices)` is the **step-accuracy ceiling** of\n1097\ta policy that is optimal at every step but indifferent among optimal moves,\n1098\tand the same statistic is computed over all optimal orders weighted by how\n1099\tmany pass through each state. Brute-force permutation enumeration agrees with\n1100\tall three lattices on 40 random instances up to 7 × 7 (`tests/test_degeneracy.py`\n1101\tcarries six of them and a hand-checked path). Coverage: every corpus instance\n1102\twith `n ≤ 15` — 2,812 instances, of which 1,614 have `n ≤ 12` as the item\n1103\tasked (10 at n = 9, 1,604 at n = 10) and 1,198 have 13–15; 1,116 MOSP-graph\n1104\tclasses, 579 at n ≤ 12 — plus 256 generated `G(n, m, p)` instances at\n1105\tn ∈ {8, 10, 12, 15}, m ∈ {n, 2n}, p ∈ {0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5}, 8 seeds each,\n1106\tsolved with `solve_mosp_exact` into a separate solutions directory and checked\n1107\tagainst the lattice minimum. 93 s on 16 workers, 2.3 s for the slowest instance.\n1108\t**Baseline.** The trivial answer would be that the optimum is essentially\n1109\tunique — one ordering up to reversal and twins — which is what imitating a\n1110\tsingle witness implicitly assumes.\n1111\t### Audit\n1112\tEvery check passed on every instance: 2,812 of 2,812 construction minima and\n1113\tsearch-measure minima equal the certified optimum, 2,138 of 2,138 product-order\n1114\tminima (all instances with m ≤ 20), every witness re-simulates to its value,\n1115\tevery witness's induced closing order constructs to the optimum *and* is\n1116\toptimal under the search measure, the twin factor divides every construction\n1117\tcount, `|Aut(G)|` divides every search-measure count (the CSV stores 15! as\n1118\t1307674367999.9998, which is rounded before dividing), and on the 256\n1119\tgenerated instances the solver's optimum equals the lattice minimum every\n1120\ttime. Nothing needed re-certification.\n1121\t### How many optimal orders (corpus, complete graphs excluded)\n1122\t**No instance has a unique optimum.** Zero of 2,812 have a unique optimal\n1123\tclosing order even after dividing out twins, and zero of 2,138 have a unique\n1124\toptimal product order up to identical columns and reversal. The least\n1125\tdegenerate instance in the corpus is `Warwick 128` (10 × 20, optimum 3): 156\n1126\tof its 3,628,800 closing orders are optimal — 24 under the search measure —\n1127\tand 4,320 product orders up to symmetry. The next five least degenerate all\n1128\thave 180–648 optimal closing orders and are the sparsest instances of their\n1129\tsize (Warwick \"2 orders per product\", HS at density 0.20, NWRS2). At the other\n1130\tend, four Faggioli–Bentivoglio 10 × 40 instances with optimum 9 have every one\n1131\tof the 10! closing orders optimal without being complete graphs, and the 1,090\n1132\tcomplete graphs (40% of the n = 10 instances, 37% of the n = 15) trivially\n1133\thave every order optimal. Among 1,116 graph classes the picture is the same\n1134\t(median 1.8 × 10⁵ orders at n = 10, 8.6 × 10⁹ at n = 15), so it is not the\n1135\tduplicated files of §1 speaking.\n1136\t**The share of optimal orders tracks how close the optimum is to n.** Over the\n1137\t1,722 non-complete instances, Spearman of the log share of optimal closing\n1138\torders with `optimum / n` is +0.70 (+0.73 within n = 10, +0.87 within n = 15),\n1139\twith matrix density +0.60, with degree dispersion `g_deg_std` −0.43, with n\n1140\t−0.33, and with the bound gap `optimum − lb_best` +0.02: how degenerate the\n1141\toptimum is has nothing to do with how hard it is to prove. By density band the\n1142\tmedian share of optimal closing orders is 10⁻³·⁷ below 0.15, 10⁻¹·⁹ at\n1143\t0.15–0.30, 10⁻⁰·⁹ at 0.30–0.50 and 10⁻⁰·⁷ above 0.5. The *count* grows with n\n1144\tfaster than the share shrinks: at p = 0.1 the generated instances go from a\n1145\t45% share at n = 8 to 0.3% at n = 15 while the count goes from 1.8 × 10⁴ to\n1146\t3.8 × 10⁹. Twice as many products as customers roughly quarters the share\n1147\t(median 3.7% against 13.7%) and p = 0.5 gives complete graphs on 56–81% of the\n1148\tgenerated instances at n ≥ 10, so `G(n, m, p)` at that density is a poor test\n1149\tof anything.\n1150\t**The search measure sees fewer of them.** Under Chu & Stuckey's measure the\n1151\tcount is smaller on 1,069 of the 1,722 non-complete instances, median ratio\n1152\t0.75 and a tenth of instances below 0.048: the complete search over-charges up\n1153\tto 95% of the optimal closing orders, all of which are still reachable through\n1154\tsome order it does not over-charge (the minimum is right every time). Reversal\n1155\tis not a symmetry of closing orders: the reversed witness closing order is\n1156\toptimal on 16.7% of the non-complete instances (22% at n = 10, 10% at n = 15),\n1157\tagainst 100% in the product space where it is a theorem.\n1158\t### Imitation\n1159\tAt a typical step of a witness closing order at n = 15, six of the remaining\n1160\tcustomers can be closed next and still finish optimally; only one step in ten\n1161\tis forced. A policy that is optimal at every step and indifferent among\n1162\toptimal moves therefore agrees with the stored witness at 30–38% of steps, and\n1163\tthe witness path is no more constrained than a typical optimal order (the\n1164\tall-orders ceiling is 0.02 lower). Joined with §7's per-instance rates on the\n1165\t774 held-out instances the two studies share at n ≤ 15:\n1166\tEvery policy §7 measured agrees with the witness far *more* often than an\n1167\toptimal-but-indifferent policy could. That excess is not optimality — it is\n1168\tagreement with the tie-breaks of the procedure that produced the witness,\n1169\twhich is a cheapest-first DFS seeded by MCN with index ties. MCN, which\n1170\tconstructs worst, imitates second best; the ranker, trained on step agreement,\n1171\timitates worst of the four and constructs better than MCN because it learned\n1172\tthe witness's *value* structure imperfectly and its tie-breaks not at all.\n1173\tThis is the mechanism behind §7's inverse relation between imitation rate and\n1174\tconstruction value.\n1175\t**Finding.** The optimum of MOSP is massively degenerate at every size and\n1176\tdensity measured: never unique, at least 156 optimal closing orders on every\n1177\tcorpus instance up to 15 customers, typically 10⁵ at n = 10 and 10¹⁰ at\n1178\tn = 15, and never a unique product order up to reversal. Degeneracy grows with\n1179\tdensity and with `optimum / n`, reaching all `n!` orders at complete graphs\n1180\tand on some instances with optimum n − 1, and is unrelated to how far the\n1181\tproved bounds sit from the optimum. For imitation this settles the question\n1182\t§7 raised: a saved witness is one of 10⁵–10¹¹ equally good orders, step-level\n1183\tagreement with it has a ceiling near 0.3 for any policy that does not know the\n1184\tsolver's tie-breaks, and every rate above that measures reproduction of the\n1185\tseeding heuristic's order rather than of optimality. An imitation objective\n1186\thas to be set-valued — a step is right if it lands in the optimal-continuation\n1187\tset, which this module computes exactly for n ≤ 15 (`lattice_counts` gives the\n1188\tbackward counts, `optimal_choices` the set) — or has to be the construction\n1189\tvalue itself, as §7 already concluded from the other direction. The 2.27 M\n1190\tdecisions `learning/policy.py` trains on are therefore about 90% arbitrary\n1191\tchoices among several optimal ones, labelled as if the solver's pick were the\n1192\tonly right answer.\n1193\t**Size range covered.** Corpus 9–15 customers (1,614 instances at n ≤ 12,\n1194\t1,198 at 13–15; product-order counts where m ≤ 20, which excludes the n = 15\n1195\tinstances with 25 or 30 products), generated 8–15. Nothing above 15: the\n1196\t`2^n` lattice with block simulation is pure Python and 15 is where it stops\n1197\tbeing cheap; the search-measure lattice vectorises and would reach the 1,298\n1198\tinstances at n = 20 in numpy, which is the natural next step if the trend\n1199\twith n needs a fourth point. Whether the degeneracy persists at 50–125\n1200\tcustomers is not measured and the counts there are not enumerable; the\n1201\twitness-path choices are, by the backward count restricted to the witness's\n1202\town prefixes, and that is the measurement to make.\n1203\t**Not a bound, not a solver change.** Nothing here touches `_lower_bound` or\n1204\tany decision path; no default changed; nothing was written to `solutions/`\n1205\t(`git status solutions/` clean after every run); the running\n1206\t`benchmarks.recertify` was not touched.\n1207\t## 9. The generated-ensemble campaign: infrastructure and pilot (§3 phase 3, item 01)\n1208\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"85602c8d-df04-47a8-a103-12762ff7e492","uuid":"06977441-3f2d-48b2-88cb-81526fa1ce67","timestamp":"2026-09-26T19:23:32.849Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/85602c8d-df04-47a8-a103-12762ff7e492/tool-results/bqdzzn2cu.txt","content":"mean gap 2.6, max 16; Simonis 72, Harvey 48, Chu & Stuckey 17). The\nsilhouette is weak and the split is by collection, so the gap instances are\nnot a family of their own in feature space; they are the sparse end of each\ngenerator's range, which is what the within-cell contrast says with more\nprecision.\n### The ten smallest\nAll ten have 20 customers and 10 products — six from Simonis's\n`problem_20_10.dat` (densities 0.20–0.30) and four from Harvey (three\n`wbo_20_10`, \"balanced orders, four customers per product\", and one\n`wbp_20_10`, \"balanced products, three products per customer\"); the 17\ngap ≥ 2 instances at n ≤ 20 all come from those two generators' 20×10 and\n20×20 files. Every one has gap exactly 2, and each was re-certified: the\nexact solver returns the cached value and the customer search refutes\n`optimum − 1` in 20–59 nodes. The matrices are in\n`reports/bound_gap_tables.md`; the summary:\nWhat they have in common, in words. Each is **one connected graph made of\nten small cliques — the products, 4 to 10 customers each — glued together at\na few hub customers, with a fringe of customers that belong to one or two\nproducts only.** The hubs are the customers in four to six products (one to\nfour of the twenty); the fringe is the 2–11 customers in exactly one product,\nwhich are simplicial vertices whose whole neighbourhood is that product's\nclique. The clique bound never exceeds the largest product (trivial = clique\nin all ten), so no two products overlap enough to form a larger clique; the\nexpansion bound never exceeds the contraction bound except once, by one; and\n`bw_rcm + 1` is 4 to 6 above the optimum, so the bandwidth heuristic is no\nhelp either. On eight of the ten the pathwidth provably exceeds the treewidth\n— Warwick 871 is the cleanest: ten 4-cliques on twenty customers, eleven of\nthem in a single product, treewidth exactly 3 (a K4 forces ≥ 3, min-fill\ngives ≤ 3), pathwidth 5. That is the structure of a *tree of cliques with\nbranching*: the treewidth of a tree of cliques is the largest clique minus\none, which every degree-based bound sees, while the pathwidth grows with how\nmany branches must be held open at once when the tree is swept from one end\nto the other, which none of them sees. The two not separated by the\nheuristic (HS 590 and Warwick 955, the only one with no fringe and degrees\n10–16) may still have `pw > tw`; the 200-restart heuristic could not show\nit. The gap of exactly 2 is the smallest a gap ≥ 2 instance can have; at\nthis size the family is a one-in-fifty event (13 of 710 in the 20×10 cell,\nwhere tight instances have mean MOSP-graph density 0.86 and degree\ncoefficient of variation 0.12 against 0.51 and 0.32 for the gap ≥ 2 ones).\n**Finding.** The instances the proved bound misses by two or more are\nsparse, connected, degree-dispersed MOSP graphs at the low-density end of\neach generator's range — trees of cliques with branching, glued at hub\ncustomers and fringed with single-product customers — and the reason the\nbound misses them is structural, not statistical: three of its four\ncomponents are lower bounds on treewidth, and on 131 of the 338 (and on 8 of\nthe 10 smallest) the pathwidth is certifiably larger than the treewidth, so\nthose components could not be tight under any tie-break or budget. A\ndepth-3 tree on size-free features reads that description off the table\n(\"connected and sparse, or dense with a low-degree customer\") and separates\ngap ≥ 2 from tight at AUC 0.931 within fixed `(n, m)` cells against 0.849\nfor density and size; the EBM reaches 0.971 with average precision 0.81\nagainst 0.54. The lower bounds themselves add nothing to the classifier. The\nonly component that can exceed the treewidth ceiling is the expansion bound,\nwhich is a pathwidth argument, and it did so on 36 instances; if the bound\nwork is ever resumed, that is the family to strengthen, and the clique and\ndegree families are provably the wrong place (§5 said the same about\ndegree statistics from the residual side). This does not contradict §4 or\n§5; it explains them.\n**Size range covered.** 9–134 customers, 5,938 of 6,376 at n ≤ 30. Gap ≥ 2\ndoes not occur below 20 customers, so every statement about the gap\ninstances covers 20–134 (17 instances at 20, 133 at 21–30, 101 at 31–60, 87\nat 61–134). The within-cell tables rest on eleven cells from 20×10 to\n100×100 and the per-band AUCs are in the tables above; the ten drawn\ninstances are all 20×10, so the \"tree of cliques\" description is established\nat n = 20 and *consistent* with, not certified at, larger sizes: the\ntreewidth certificate covers 44–59% of the gap ≥ 2 instances at n ≤ 60 and\n5.7% above, where min-fill is too loose to certify anything. The cluster\nstatement covers all 338.\n**Not a bound, not a solver change.** No default changed; `_lower_bound` and\nevery decision path are untouched; nothing was written to `solutions/` (the\nre-certification of the ten read the cache and re-verified it, and `git\nstatus solutions/` is clean); the running `benchmarks.recertify` was not\ntouched. The two theorem checks in the treewidth-ceiling table are the only\n\"bound\" statements here and both are on record as consistency checks of\nthe feature code, not as new bounds. The ten instances the description rests\non were each re-solved and independently refuted at `optimum − 1`.\n**For the next loop.** §2.7 (extremal search) has its objective: maximise\n`optimum − lb_best` at n ≤ 15 starting from trees of cliques, with\n`optimum − (tw_ub + 1)` as a second objective, since a 12-customer instance\nwith `pw − tw ≥ 2` and a proof of it is exactly the kind of object one can\nreason about. §2.5 should record `tw_ub` alongside the optimum on\n`G(n, m, p)` to measure how `pw − tw` scales with sparsity. And exact\ntreewidth for the 207 uncertified gap ≥ 2 instances (82 of them at n ≤ 30,\ncheap with a proper treewidth solver) would turn the 38.8% floor into a\nnumber.\n## 7. Distilling the closing policy (§2.6a)\n*Iteration 9, 2026-09-25 (an earlier iteration wrote `learning/distil.py` and\nended without running it; this iteration found that its worker pool deadlocked\n— forking after LightGBM has run leaves the children asleep in libgomp — and\nthat its MCN tie-break used the total product count where `least_cost_node`\nuses the products not yet produced; fixed both, added the fidelity-fitted\nmodels, the two named rules, the `cs-dfs` reference columns, the per-collection\nand head-to-head tables, wrote the tests, ran it and wrote this section.)\nCode: `learning/distil.py`. Regenerate:*\n```bash\npython -m learning.distil --workers 16                 # ~4 min: fits 60 s, evaluation 140 s\npython -m learning.distil --workers 16 --lex-depth 2   # ~3 min, the 91-rule family only\npython -m pytest tests/test_distil.py -q\n```\n*Writes `reports/distil_tables.md` (every table below in full, the fitted\nweights per fold and the trees of every fold) and `learning/data/distil.csv`\n(git-ignored; one row per evaluated instance with every strategy's value).\nNothing is written to `solutions/`, no solver file is touched.*\n**Question.** `learning/policy.py` imitates the 2.24 M closing decisions of the\ncertified witnesses with a LightGBM ranker over eight local features and, used\ngreedily, cuts MCN's error over the optimum by two thirds. What rule did it\nlearn? Does a depth-3 tree or a linear scorer keep most of the gain, does a\nlexicographic rule a person can state, and do the two static hypotheses of the\nplan — Fiedler-vector order, BFS layers from a minimum-degree root — hold?\n**Method.** The protocol of `python -m learning.policy evaluate`: five folds\ngrouped by source file with the same seed, up to 400 held-out instances per\nfold (1,920 in all; fold 3's files hold 320), the ranker and every distilled\nmodel fitted per fold on the training files' decision rows (1.6–2.0 M rows per\nfold, 6,372 certified witnesses at run time). Every closing order is turned\ninto a product order with `product_order_from_customers` and simulated on the\noriginal instance with `max_open_stacks`; the statistics are over the optimum.\nDistilled models: a depth-3 classification tree and a logistic scorer fitted\nto the witness decisions (`tree`, `linear`, and `linear-r` with its weights\nrounded to two decimals), and a depth-3 regression tree and least squares\nfitted to the *ranker's own raw scores* on the same rows (`tree-fid`,\n`linear-fid`), which is distillation in the strict sense. Lexicographic rules:\nall 570 orderings of up to three of the five per-candidate features\n(`remaining_degree`, `newly_opened`, `already_open`, `total_degree`, products\nnot yet produced), each key in either direction, MCN's own key — minimum\nremaining degree, then fewest products left, then index — breaking every\nremaining tie, so the empty rule is MCN itself (verified: it agrees with\n`upper_bound(instance, \"mcn\")` on all 1,920). The rule is *selected* per fold on\n400 training instances and scored held out (`lex-selected`); two rules are also\nquoted by name, and their rows are descriptive because they were named after\nseeing the pooled table. Static orders: Fiedler vector of each component\n(sign fixed by the lowest-index customer) and its reverse; BFS from the\nminimum-degree customer in FIFO order, in Cuthill–McKee order and reversed\n(RCM); and minimum-degree elimination with fill-in, which is MCN made to\nremember which open customers were opened together. The summary statistic is\n`gain kept = (MAE_MCN − MAE_rule) / (MAE_MCN − MAE_ranker)`: 1 is the ranker, 0\nis MCN. Reference columns: Chu & Stuckey's restricted DFS at 200,000 nodes\nseeded with MCN (`cs-dfs`), with the ranker's order (`cs-dfs+lgbm`) and with\nthe named rule's order (`cs-dfs+rule`).\n**Baseline.** MCN, held out: MAE 1.616, exact 50.2%, worst +26. The ranker:\n0.519, 75.0%, worst +34.\n**Held-out constructions over the optimum** (1,920 instances, 9–134 customers).\n**MAE by size band.**\n**Head to head, held out** (instances where the first is below / equal / above the second).\nWhere the rule loses to the ranker it loses by one on 82 of the 109 and by at\nmost five; where it wins it wins by one on 181 of the 253 and by 20 or more on\nfour. The ranker's +34 is GP8 (94 against an optimum of 60) and two of its\n+20s are GP6 and GP7; the rule is exact on all three. That is the shape\n`learning/policy.py`'s docstring already describes — a greedy with no search\nbehind it — and the rule does not have it.\n**Step-level agreement** with the witness (imitation) and with the ranker\n(fidelity), over the 43,020 decisions with more than one candidate in the\n1,920 held-out witnesses.\n**What the fitted models say.** The logistic scorer is the same in all five\nfolds to two decimals: `+1.00·already_open − 0.07·remaining_degree −\n0.03..0.05·newly_opened + 0.01·total_degree` (per-step constants aside; `open_after\n= open_now + newly_opened − 1`, so it has four effective weights). Read as a\nrule: *a customer whose stack is already open; then fewer unclosed neighbours;\nthen fewer new stacks* — MCN's criterion with \"already open\" in front of it —\nand it keeps 82% of the gain. The depth-3 tree splits on `open_after ≤ 6.5`,\n`newly_opened ≤ 1.5`, `remaining_degree ≤ 2.5 / 10.5`, `already_open` and\n`n_customers`, and its leaf probabilities fall with `newly_opened` and with\n`remaining_degree`: *fewest new stacks, then smallest remaining degree*. With\neight leaves it ties constantly, and the ties are what decide it: broken by\nMCN's key it keeps 57%, broken by index it is worse than MCN. The two models\nfitted to the ranker's scores rather than to the witness keep less (48%, 34%);\n`linear-fid`'s `already_open` weight ranges from −2.3 to +8.9 across folds,\nso the ranker's raw score is not close to linear in the features. And the\nranker itself imitates the witness at only 45.3% of decisions, below MCN's\n52.5%, while the one-key rule imitates at 62.3% and the tree at 58.5%: the\nimitation rate ranks these constructions almost inversely to their value,\nwhich is the first thing to know before building another imitation policy.\n**The rule.** 64 of the 570 lexicographic rules beat the ranker's MAE: all 57\nwhose first key is `min newly_opened` (the worst of them 0.424) and 7 whose\nfirst key is `max already_open`, and no others; the five per-fold selections\nall carry `min newly_opened` first or second. The two named rules:\n- *Open the fewest new stacks.* `newly_opened` is `|N[c] \\ opened|`, the\n  stacks closing `c` would open for the first time; minimising it minimises the\n  open stacks after the step. That is the cheapest-first order in which Chu &\n  Stuckey's `ub_MOSP` expands candidates (`restricted_dfs`'s docstring), so\n  followed greedily with MCN's tie-break it is that search's *first leaf*.\n  Held out: 0.419, exact 79.1%, worst +11 — already better than the ranker\n  on every statistic. (Pattern-level heuristics that open the fewest new\n  stacks go back to Yuen; whether this customer-closing form with this\n  tie-break is in the literature is not settled here.)\n- *Open the fewest new stacks; on ties, the customer with the most unclosed\n  neighbours.* The tie-break is the **reverse of MCN's**: MCN closes the\n  lowest remaining degree, this closes the highest. Among candidates that open\n  equally few stacks, the one with many unclosed neighbours is the one whose\n  neighbourhood is already mostly open, so closing it retires the most of the\n  current frontier. Held out: **0.348, exact 80.7%, worst +9**, 115% of the\n  ranker's gain, better than the ranker on 253 instances and worse on 109,\n  better than MCN on 896 and worse on 8, and better than the same rule with\n  MCN's tie-break on 159 against 86. It is the ranker's equal or better in\n  every size band and in every collection but Shaw (0.36 vs 0.32 on 25\n  instances); on the 91 Chu & Stuckey instances it is 2.42 against the\n  ranker's 3.35 and MCN's 9.44. It matches the 200,000-node DFS on exact\n  solutions (80.7% vs 81.2%) though not on MAE (0.348 vs 0.311) or worst case\n  (+9 vs +7). As the DFS's *incumbent* it is worth more than the learned\n  order: `cs-dfs+rule` 0.127 / 92.7% against `cs-dfs+lgbm` 0.157 / 91.0%, 82\n  instances better and 38 worse, and it never does worse than the MCN-seeded\n  DFS on more than 2 of 1,920.\nThe honest number is the per-fold selection, which does not see the held-out\ninstances: 0.362, 79.2%, worst +9, 114% of the gain. The selected rules were\n`min newly_opened, max already_open, max remaining_degree`; `min newly_opened,\nmax remaining_degree, min already_open` (twice); `min newly_opened, max\ntotal_degree, min patterns`; `max already_open, min newly_opened, min patterns`.\nFold 3's choice generalised worst (train 0.265, held out 0.722, still below the\nranker's 1.259 on those files).\n**The static hypotheses.** Fiedler order — sort the customers of each\ncomponent by the Fiedler vector, no state at all — keeps 66% of the gain (0.886,\n64.5%), more than the depth-3 tree and more than the logistic scorer's rounded\nform loses; reversed it keeps 52%. BFS from a minimum-degree root does not:\nFIFO order is worse than MCN, Cuthill–McKee keeps 9%, RCM 46%. So the plan's\nfirst hypothesis is partly right and its second is wrong, and both are far from\nthe best rule. Minimum-degree elimination with fill-in is worse than MCN\n(−34%): remembering that the open neighbours of a closed customer stay open\ntogether makes the greedy worse, not better.\n**Finding.** The kill criterion — no readable rule keeps more than half the\nranker's gain — is not met; it is exceeded, and in the direction the plan did\nnot anticipate. The ranker did not learn something a rule cannot express; it\nlearned, imperfectly, the cheapest-first order of Chu & Stuckey's own DFS, and\na two-key rule a sentence long — *close the customer that opens the fewest new\nstacks, and among those the one with the most unclosed neighbours* — beats it\non every statistic, at every size, in every collection but one, with no model,\nno training and no dependency. The distilled tree and scorer are worse than\nthe rule because imitation is the wrong objective: the models fitted to the\nwitness reproduce MCN's minimum-degree preference, which the witnesses share\nat the level of individual decisions (imitation 52–62%) but which is the wrong\ntie-break for the construction as a whole (159 better / 86 worse for the\nreverse). This is the third time in this report that a learned component has\nresolved into a finding about the solver rather than a component of it\n(`reports/learning_plan.md`): here the finding is a heuristic in the\nliterature's sense, and a better DFS seed than the imitation policy that\n`reports/learning.md` measured.\n**Size range covered.** 9–134 customers; 1,768 of the 1,920 held-out\ninstances have ≤ 30, 96 have 31–60, 56 have 61–134. The rule's lead over the\nranker holds in each band (0.207 vs 0.282; 1.010 vs 1.573; 3.679 vs 6.179),\nand grows with size, but above 60 customers it rests on 56 instances and the\nrule's own error there is 3.7 stacks with a worst case of +9 (Chu & Stuckey\n100×50, `Random-100-50-4-3_0`, 38 against an optimum of 29); at that size\nneither it nor the ranker is close to the optimum without the search behind\nit. The imitation and fidelity rates are over all 1,920 witnesses and are\ndominated by the small instances.\n**Not a bound, not a solver change.** Nothing here touches `_lower_bound` or\nany decision path; every value in every table is an upper bound obtained by\nsimulation on the original instance; no default changed; nothing was written\nto `solutions/` (`git status solutions/` clean after the run); the running\n`benchmarks.recertify` (12 processes) was not touched. The rule is not\nregistered as a heuristic in `satisfiability/heuristics.py`: that would be a\nsolver change, and it is left for the loop's owner.\n**For the next loop.** (i) The rule costs what MCN costs and needs no model,\nso it is the natural replacement for `learned+cs-dfs` as the DFS seed:\n`learning.corpus_sweep` over all 6,376 with the rule seeding `restricted_dfs`,\nagainst the 709-better / 13-worse that `learned+cs-dfs` scored over `cs-dfs`,\nwould settle whether the LightGBM dependency decision left open in\n`reports/learning.md` needs deciding at all. (ii) Any future imitation study\nshould select by construction value, never by step accuracy; §2.6b (item 08)\nshould measure how many optimal closing orders there are before anyone\nimitates \"the\" witness again, because a 45%-imitation policy beating a\n62%-imitation rule says the witnesses disagree with each other. (iii) The\ntie-break's direction is a small, statable conjecture about the customer\nsearch: among cheapest candidates, expanding the highest remaining degree\nfirst should reach the optimum sooner; `restricted_dfs` orders its fan\ncheapest-first with ties by customer index (`scored.sort()` on `(cost,\ncustomer, ...)`), and `learning.node_counts.refute` is the primitive to\nmeasure it with.\n## 8. Degeneracy of the optimum: how many orderings are optimal? (§2.6b)\n*Iteration 10, 2026-09-25. Code: `learning/degeneracy.py`. Regenerate:*\n```bash\npython -m learning.degeneracy --workers 16          # ~90 s; corpus n ≤ 15 and 256 generated\npython -m learning.degeneracy --tables-only         # the tables again from learning/data/degeneracy.csv\npython -m pytest tests/test_degeneracy.py -q\n```\n*Writes `reports/degeneracy_tables.md` (every table below in full) and\n`learning/data/degeneracy.csv` (git-ignored; one row per instance with every\ncount). The generated instances' witnesses are persisted under\n`learning/data/degeneracy_solutions/`, never under `solutions/`; no solver file\nis touched.*\n**Question.** `learning/policy.py` imitates \"the\" witness of each certified\ninstance, and §7 found a ranker imitating 45% of witness decisions beating a\nrule imitating 62%. That is only possible if many closing orders are optimal\nand the one in `solutions/` is an arbitrary member. How many are there, how\ndoes the count depend on size and density, is the optimum ever unique up to\nreversal, and what does that imply for imitation?\n**Method.** Three exact counts per instance, each a path count through the\nsubset lattice rather than an enumeration of permutations, so the counts are\nexact where `n!` and `m!` are not enumerable: (i) **closing orders,\nconstruction value** — permutations of the customers whose product order,\nbuilt by `product_order_from_customers` exactly as `learning.policy` builds\nit, simulates on the original instance to the optimum; the cost of closing\n`c` from the closed set `S` depends on `(S, c)` only, by simulating the block\nof `c`'s unproduced products; (ii) **closing orders, search measure** — the\nsame under Chu & Stuckey's `max_i |O(S_i) − S_{i−1}|`, which the complete\nsearch branches on and which over-charges orders that place an already\nfinished customer late; (iii) **product orders** — permutations of the\nproducts with open-stack peak equal to the optimum, over the `2^m` lattice of\nproduced sets in numpy, for `m ≤ 20`. Each lattice's bottleneck minimum is a\nfree audit of the certified optimum. Reversal is a symmetry only of (iii): it\nreverses the open-stack profile, so the count is even and \"unique up to\nreversal\" means two orders after dividing by the factorials of identical\ncolumns; (i) is divided by the factorials of identical rows (twins), (ii) by\n`|Aut(G)|` from `learning/data/canonical.csv`, on which the automorphism group\nacts freely. Along every witness's induced closing order the module counts,\nat every step, the number of remaining customers that begin an optimal\ncompletion (`choices`); `mean(1 / choices)` is the **step-accuracy ceiling** of\na policy that is optimal at every step but indifferent among optimal moves,\nand the same statistic is computed over all optimal orders weighted by how\nmany pass through each state. Brute-force permutation enumeration agrees with\nall three lattices on 40 random instances up to 7 × 7 (`tests/test_degeneracy.py`\ncarries six of them and a hand-checked path). Coverage: every corpus instance\nwith `n ≤ 15` — 2,812 instances, of which 1,614 have `n ≤ 12` as the item\nasked (10 at n = 9, 1,604 at n = 10) and 1,198 have 13–15; 1,116 MOSP-graph\nclasses, 579 at n ≤ 12 — plus 256 generated `G(n, m, p)` instances at\nn ∈ {8, 10, 12, 15}, m ∈ {n, 2n}, p ∈ {0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5}, 8 seeds each,\nsolved with `solve_mosp_exact` into a separate solutions directory and checked\nagainst the lattice minimum. 93 s on 16 workers, 2.3 s for the slowest instance.\n**Baseline.** The trivial answer would be that the optimum is essentially\nunique — one ordering up to reversal and twins — which is what imitating a\nsingle witness implicitly assumes.\n### Audit\nEvery check passed on every instance: 2,812 of 2,812 construction minima and\nsearch-measure minima equal the certified optimum, 2,138 of 2,138 product-order\nminima (all instances with m ≤ 20), every witness re-simulates to its value,\nevery witness's induced closing order constructs to the optimum *and* is\noptimal under the search measure, the twin factor divides every construction\ncount, `|Aut(G)|` divides every search-measure count (the CSV stores 15! as\n1307674367999.9998, which is rounded before dividing), and on the 256\ngenerated instances the solver's optimum equals the lattice minimum every\ntime. Nothing needed re-certification.\n### How many optimal orders (corpus, complete graphs excluded)\n**No instance has a unique optimum.** Zero of 2,812 have a unique optimal\nclosing order even after dividing out twins, and zero of 2,138 have a unique\noptimal product order up to identical columns and reversal. The least\ndegenerate instance in the corpus is `Warwick 128` (10 × 20, optimum 3): 156\nof its 3,628,800 closing orders are optimal — 24 under the search measure —\nand 4,320 product orders up to symmetry. The next five least degenerate all\nhave 180–648 optimal closing orders and are the sparsest instances of their\nsize (Warwick \"2 orders per product\", HS at density 0.20, NWRS2). At the other\nend, four Faggioli–Bentivoglio 10 × 40 instances with optimum 9 have every one\nof the 10! closing orders optimal without being complete graphs, and the 1,090\ncomplete graphs (40% of the n = 10 instances, 37% of the n = 15) trivially\nhave every order optimal. Among 1,116 graph classes the picture is the same\n(median 1.8 × 10⁵ orders at n = 10, 8.6 × 10⁹ at n = 15), so it is not the\nduplicated files of §1 speaking.\n**The share of optimal orders tracks how close the optimum is to n.** Over the\n1,722 non-complete instances, Spearman of the log share of optimal closing\norders with `optimum / n` is +0.70 (+0.73 within n = 10, +0.87 within n = 15),\nwith matrix density +0.60, with degree dispersion `g_deg_std` −0.43, with n\n−0.33, and with the bound gap `optimum − lb_best` +0.02: how degenerate the\noptimum is has nothing to do with how hard it is to prove. By density band the\nmedian share of optimal closing orders is 10⁻³·⁷ below 0.15, 10⁻¹·⁹ at\n0.15–0.30, 10⁻⁰·⁹ at 0.30–0.50 and 10⁻⁰·⁷ above 0.5. The *count* grows with n\nfaster than the share shrinks: at p = 0.1 the generated instances go from a\n45% share at n = 8 to 0.3% at n = 15 while the count goes from 1.8 × 10⁴ to\n3.8 × 10⁹. Twice as many products as customers roughly quarters the share\n(median 3.7% against 13.7%) and p = 0.5 gives complete graphs on 56–81% of the\ngenerated instances at n ≥ 10, so `G(n, m, p)` at that density is a poor test\nof anything.\n**The search measure sees fewer of them.** Under Chu & Stuckey's measure the\ncount is smaller on 1,069 of the 1,722 non-complete instances, median ratio\n0.75 and a tenth of instances below 0.048: the complete search over-charges up\nto 95% of the optimal closing orders, all of which are still reachable through\nsome order it does not over-charge (the minimum is right every time). Reversal\nis not a symmetry of closing orders: the reversed witness closing order is\noptimal on 16.7% of the non-complete instances (22% at n = 10, 10% at n = 15),\nagainst 100% in the product space where it is a theorem.\n### Imitation\nAt a typical step of a witness closing order at n = 15, six of the remaining\ncustomers can be closed next and still finish optimally; only one step in ten\nis forced. A policy that is optimal at every step and indifferent among\noptimal moves therefore agrees with the stored witness at 30–38% of steps, and\nthe witness path is no more constrained than a typical optimal order (the\nall-orders ceiling is 0.02 lower). Joined with §7's per-instance rates on the\n774 held-out instances the two studies share at n ≤ 15:\nEvery policy §7 measured agrees with the witness far *more* often than an\noptimal-but-indifferent policy could. That excess is not optimality — it is\nagreement with the tie-breaks of the procedure that produced the witness,\nwhich is a cheapest-first DFS seeded by MCN with index ties. MCN, which\nconstructs worst, imitates second best; the ranker, trained on step agreement,\nimitates worst of the four and constructs better than MCN because it learned\nthe witness's *value* structure imperfectly and its tie-breaks not at all.\nThis is the mechanism behind §7's inverse relation between imitation rate and\nconstruction value.\n**Finding.** The optimum of MOSP is massively degenerate at every size and\ndensity measured: never unique, at least 156 optimal closing orders on every\ncorpus instance up to 15 customers, typically 10⁵ at n = 10 and 10¹⁰ at\nn = 15, and never a unique product order up to reversal. Degeneracy grows with\ndensity and with `optimum / n`, reaching all `n!` orders at complete graphs\nand on some instances with optimum n − 1, and is unrelated to how far the\nproved bounds sit from the optimum. For imitation this settles the question\n§7 raised: a saved witness is one of 10⁵–10¹¹ equally good orders, step-level\nagreement with it has a ceiling near 0.3 for any policy that does not know the\nsolver's tie-breaks, and every rate above that measures reproduction of the\nseeding heuristic's order rather than of optimality. An imitation objective\nhas to be set-valued — a step is right if it lands in the optimal-continuation\nset, which this module computes exactly for n ≤ 15 (`lattice_counts` gives the\nbackward counts, `optimal_choices` the set) — or has to be the construction\nvalue itself, as §7 already concluded from the other direction. The 2.27 M\ndecisions `learning/policy.py` trains on are therefore about 90% arbitrary\nchoices among several optimal ones, labelled as if the solver's pick were the\nonly right answer.\n**Size range covered.** Corpus 9–15 customers (1,614 instances at n ≤ 12,\n1,198 at 13–15; product-order counts where m ≤ 20, which excludes the n = 15\ninstances with 25 or 30 products), generated 8–15. Nothing above 15: the\n`2^n` lattice with block simulation is pure Python and 15 is where it stops\nbeing cheap; the search-measure lattice vectorises and would reach the 1,298\ninstances at n = 20 in numpy, which is the natural next step if the trend\nwith n needs a fourth point. Whether the degeneracy persists at 50–125\ncustomers is not measured and the counts there are not enumerable; the\nwitness-path choices are, by the backward count restricted to the witness's\nown prefixes, and that is the measurement to make.\n**Not a bound, not a solver change.** Nothing here touches `_lower_bound` or\nany decision path; no default changed; nothing was written to `solutions/`\n(`git status solutions/` clean after every run); the running\n`benchmarks.recertify` was not touched.\n## 9. The generated-ensemble campaign: infrastructure and pilot (§3 phase 3, item 01)\n","numLines":417,"startLine":792,"totalLines":1208}}}
