{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EisjL59aSyFRWSVdcCE2fZ","type":"tool_result","content":"731\tits row's `(base_name, method, k)` through `recover`, the base from the\n732\tcampaign manifest), the re-covered witnesses under\n733\t`learning/data/ensemble/recover_solutions/` (64 MB, git-ignored, re-solved in\n734\tthree minutes), and every table below in full in\n735\t`reports/graph_story_tables.md`. Nothing was written to `solutions/`.*\n736\t**Question.** The optimum is `pathwidth(MOSP graph) + 1`, a function of the\n737\tgraph alone. Is the *hardness* — the nodes the complete customer search\n738\tvisits to refute `optimum − 1` — a function of the graph too, or does the\n739\tmatrix (which cliques cover which edges) carry information the reduction\n740\tdiscards? And §8's two counts: is the number of optimal closing orders under\n741\tthe search measure (`count_search`) equal on two instances with the same\n742\tgraph, and is the number under the construction value (`count_closing`) free\n743\tto differ? **Kill**: graph-only features predict nodes as well as the full set.\n744\t**What the code says before any measurement.** `customer_search.decide` and\n745\tits C port consume `_neighbour_masks(instance)` — the self-inclusive\n746\tneighbourhoods `N[c]` of the MOSP graph, indexed by customer — and nothing\n747\telse of the matrix. So under the default configuration the node count is a\n748\tfunction of the *labelled* graph, and the measurement below is a test of the\n749\timplementation rather than of the problem. The matrix enters in exactly one\n750\tplace: the `csearch` configuration turns Theorem 2 (`better_move`) on when the\n751\tmean number of products per customer is at most 5\n752\t(`sparse_enough_for_better_move`), and a re-covering can move that mean across\n753\tthe threshold. Everything else that differs between two matrices with one\n754\tgraph — the upper-bound heuristics simulate the *constructed* product order,\n755\t`lb_trivial` is a column sum, the SAT encoding has a position variable per\n756\tproduct — is outside the refutation being counted. The study therefore\n757\tseparates three things and says which is which.\n758\t**Method.** Three sources of pairs with the same graph and different products.\n759\t(1) *Re-coverings with fixed labels*, the item's construction: 1,400 base\n760\tinstances from the campaign, 200 per `n ∈ {10, 15, …, 40}`, one per\n761\tisomorphism class, non-complete, half drawn uniformly and half from the\n762\thardest decile by nodes so the ratios have room to move (base nodes median 9\n763\tat n = 10 → 3,850 at n = 40, max 30,755). For each base, four re-coverings by\n764\teach of three methods: **split** one product `P` into `A ⊂ P` and `B`, where\n765\t`B` is `P \\ A` plus every vertex of `A` whose edge into `P \\ A` no other\n766\tproduct covers (so the cut's crossing edges are kept where needed and left to\n767\tthe other products where they can be); **merge** two overlapping products\n768\twhose union is a clique of the graph, preferring a pair neither of which\n769\tcontains the other; and a fresh **greedy edge-clique cover**, maximal cliques\n770\tgrown from uncovered edges in a shuffled order, with a singleton product kept\n771\tfor every customer that had products but no neighbours, so the set of\n772\tcustomers that ever open is unchanged. Each re-covering is seeded from\n773\t`(base_name, method, k)`, checked to have the same neighbour masks and nauty\n774\tcertificate as its base, **solved independently** with `solve_mosp_exact`\n775\tinto its own solutions directory, its witness re-simulated, and `optimum − 1`\n776\trefuted under both `node_counts` configurations; the base is re-refuted in\n777\tthe same process and compared with the campaign CSV. (2) *Relabellings*: 20\n778\trandom row-and-column permutations of each base, refuted under both\n779\tconfigurations. Every corpus pair with one graph and different products also\n780\tdiffers in labelling, so this is the variance no graph-invariant can see and\n781\tthe floor against which the kill test has to be read. (3) *The corpus*: §1's\n782\t150 graph classes at n ≤ 40 holding several matrix classes, with their node\n783\tcounts from `learning/data/node_counts.csv`. For §8's counts,\n784\t`learning.degeneracy.analyse` on every re-covered pair at n ≤ 15 (the `2^n`\n785\tlattice is pure Python). The kill test predicts `log10(1 + nodes)` on the\n786\tdeduplicated campaign (27,321 rows, one per class per cell) with LightGBM\n787\t(400 trees) under `GroupKFold(5)` by MOSP-graph class, from three feature\n788\tsets built from `learning.features` group names: **graph-only** (the graph\n789\tgroup, the label-free invariants `tw_min_fill`, `tw_min_degree`, `bw_rcm`,\n790\t`spectral_radius`, `fiedler`, `fiedler_lcc`, `sep_size`, `sep_frac`,\n791\t`lb_contraction`, and the optimum, itself a graph invariant: 21 features),\n792\t**matrix-only** (the matrix group and the optimum: 18) and **full** (all 49\n793\tand the optimum: 50); variants without the optimum and with `nodes_csearch`\n794\tas target. Paired statistics are on `log10(1 + nodes)`; Wilcoxon on the\n795\tnon-zero differences.\n796\t**Baseline.** The hypothesis the plan states: two matrices with the same\n797\tgraph cost the search differently, so the matrix carries information the\n798\treduction discards. For the kill test, the per-size median (what size alone\n799\tknows) and the matrix-only set.\n800\t### Re-coverings with fixed labels: 15,900 pairs, every default node count equal\n801\t15,900 applicable re-coverings (merge needs two overlapping products whose\n802\tunion is a clique, split a product of three or more customers; 900 attempts\n803\tfound none), 14,280 distinct matrices in 12,031 matrix classes, all 15,900\n804\twith the base's neighbour masks and nauty certificate. All 15,900 solved\n805\tindependently to the base's optimum with a witness that re-simulates — 15,900\n806\tfree audits of Yanasse's equality, none failed. **Under the default\n807\tconfiguration the node count is equal to the base's in 15,900 of 15,900\n808\tpairs**: median and maximum `|log10 ratio|` are 0, the Wilcoxon test has no\n809\tnon-zero differences to run on. All 1,400 base re-refutations reproduce the\n810\tcampaign CSV exactly.\n811\tUnder `csearch` the count differs in 394 pairs, and **every one of the 394 is\n812\tamong the 622 pairs where the re-covering moved the mean products per\n813\tcustomer across the `better_move` threshold** (0 differ outside them). That\n814\tis the one matrix effect, and it is an effect of the configuration rule, not\n815\tof the search. Read as Theorem 2 on against off on the same labelled graph:\n816\tTheorem 2 saves 10–13% of the nodes at the median for n ≥ 25, up to 20–25% at\n817\tthe tenth percentile, and never costs a node (on > off: 0 of 622). The\n818\tre-coverings that flip it are mostly the greedy cover (536 of 622): maximal\n819\tcliques are fewer and larger than the generator's products, and the mean\n820\tproducts per customer moves by −2.0 to +1.7 (interquartile), either way\n821\tacross 5.\n822\t### Relabellings of the same matrix: the label floor\n823\t29,400 refutations (20 relabellings and the base, 1,400 bases), all `unsat`.\n824\tRenaming the customers — the same matrix, the same graph — changes the\n825\tdefault node count on 588 of the 800 bases at n ≥ 25, by 2–6% at the median\n826\tover 20 relabellings and 14–18% at the ninetieth percentile, at most 2.26×;\n827\tunder `csearch` the spread is three to four times wider (p90 1.4–1.7×, max\n828\t8.1×), because Theorem 2's dominance depends on which of several tied\n829\tcandidates the index order puts first. **The labelling moves the count; the\n830\tmatrix, with labels fixed, does not.** The mean absolute deviation of\n831\t`log10(1 + nodes)` within a base, 0.005–0.008 (default) and 0.012–0.026\n832\t(`csearch`), is the floor below which no label-free predictor can go.\n833\t### §8's counts within re-covered pairs (n = 10 and 15, 4,516 pairs)\n834\tAs §8 predicted: **`count_search` is equal in 4,516 of 4,516 pairs** (it is a\n835\tgraph invariant, and the lattice minimum equals the optimum on every one),\n836\tand **`count_closing` is not** — a greedy re-cover leaves it unchanged on 19\n837\tof 1,600 pairs and multiplies it by 2.9 (n = 10) and 4.5 (n = 15) at the\n838\tmedian, by 13–40× at the ninetieth percentile and by up to 1,540×. The\n839\tdirection is systematic: covering the same edges with fewer, larger products\n840\tmakes more closing orders construct to the optimum; splitting a product makes\n841\tfewer (median ratio 0.94–0.97, minimum 0.11). So the number of optimal\n842\tclosing orders *under the search measure* is a property of the graph and the\n843\tnumber *under the construction value* is a property of the matrix, by up to\n844\tthree orders of magnitude at 15 customers.\n845\t### The corpus pairs are uninformative\n846\t§1's 150 graph classes at n ≤ 40 with several matrix classes hold 2,796\n847\tmatrix classes and instances (Simonis, Harvey, Faggioli–Bentivoglio, and the\n848\ttwo Wilson/Challenge `NWRS7`/`NWRS8` pairs). Node counts never differ within\n849\ta class under either configuration — but they could not: the classes are\n850\tnear-complete graphs (optimum n − 1 or n − 2 at n = 10, 15, 20, 30; 28–29 at\n851\tn = 30) refuted in 0–3 nodes, and the maximum in the whole set is 13, the two\n852\t`NWRS` pairs at n = 25 which are the same matrix with one all-zero column\n853\tremoved. The corpus offers no pair on which the question has room to move;\n854\tthe generated pairs above are the evidence.\n855\t### The kill test: graph-only features predict nodes as well as the full set\n856\t`log10(1 + nodes_default)`, deduplicated campaign, `GroupKFold(5)` by graph\n857\tclass:\n858\tGraph-only beats the full set by 0.001–0.002 MAE on both targets and both\n859\trow sets, and is within 0.003 of it without the optimum; by size it is ahead\n860\tat n ≥ 25 and behind by at most 0.001 at n ≤ 20 (table in\n861\t`reports/graph_story_tables.md`). Matrix-only is 25–30% worse everywhere.\n862\tBoth are an order of magnitude above the relabelling floor (0.005–0.008), so\n863\twhat remains is model and feature error, not label noise. **Kill criterion\n864\tmet**: the 29 matrix, bound and matrix-derived invariant features add nothing\n865\tto the prediction of hardness that the 21 graph features do not already carry.\n866\t**Finding.** **No: the matrix is not part of the story for hardness, as\n867\thardness is measured here.** With the customer labels fixed, re-covering the\n868\tsame edge set with different cliques — 15,900 re-coverings of 1,400\n869\tinstances, 12,031 matrix classes, products removed or added by up to 30 —\n870\tleaves the default node count to refute `optimum − 1` *exactly* unchanged in\n871\tevery pair, and the kill test agrees from the other direction: graph-only\n872\tfeatures predict nodes as well as graph-plus-matrix features (MAE 0.095\n873\tagainst 0.097 in log10), and matrix-only features are 25–30% worse. Two\n874\tthings do move the count, and neither is the clique cover. The **labelling**\n875\tdoes — renaming the customers of the same matrix changes the default count\n876\ton three bases in four at n ≥ 25, by 2–6% at the median and up to 2.3×, and\n877\tby up to 8× under `csearch` — which is a fact about the search's tie-breaks,\n878\tinvisible to every graph invariant and the floor for any label-free\n879\tpredictor. And the **configuration rule** does: `csearch` turns Theorem 2 on\n880\tby a matrix statistic, mean products per customer ≤ 5, and a re-covering that\n881\tcrosses it changes the count by 10–13% at the median, up to 25%, always in\n882\tTheorem 2's favour; that is the only channel through which the matrix reaches\n883\tthe refutation, and it is a design choice of `sparse_enough_for_better_move`,\n884\tnot a property of the problem. Where the matrix *does* carry information is\n885\texactly where §8 said: the number of optimal closing orders under the\n886\tconstruction value, which the same graph's re-coverings multiply or divide by\n887\tup to three orders of magnitude at n = 15 while the count under the search\n888\tmeasure is identical in all 4,516 pairs. Part of this finding is a theorem\n889\tabout the code — the search reads neighbour masks and nothing else — and the\n890\tmeasurement's value is that it confirms the C port and the Python behave as\n891\tthe code says on 15,900 pairs, quantifies the two exceptions, and hands §2.8\n892\ta clean statement: hardness is a function of the labelled graph, so a\n893\tpredictor of nodes at 125 × 125 needs graph features and a label-noise term,\n894\tand no matrix feature. For the SAT encoding, whose formula has a position\n895\tvariable per product, the story would be different; it is not the hardness\n896\tmeasured here.\n897\t**Size range covered.** Re-coverings and relabellings at n ∈ {10, 15, 20, 25,\n898\t30, 35, 40}, m ∈ {n, 2n} before re-covering, 200 bases per size with base\n899\tnode counts up to 30,755; the §8 counts at n = 10 and 15 only (4,516 pairs);\n900\tthe corpus pairs at n ≤ 40; the kill test on the whole deduplicated campaign\n901\t(10 ≤ n ≤ 40). The exact-equality result is structural and does not depend\n902\ton size; the sizes of the two exceptions (label spread, Theorem 2's saving)\n903\tare measured to n = 40 and nothing here says how they scale to 125.\n904\t**Kill criterion.** Met: graph-only MAE 0.0948 against full 0.0968 on all\n905\trows, 0.0937 against 0.0952 on non-complete rows (tolerance 0.01); met again\n906\ton `nodes_csearch` and within 0.003 without the optimum.\n907\t**Not a bound, not a solver change.** No default changed; `_lower_bound` and\n908\tevery decision path are untouched; nothing was written to `solutions/`. No\n909\tfinding rests on a handful of instances: every re-covered instance was solved\n910\tindependently and matched its base's optimum (15,900 of 15,900), every\n911\trefutation returned `unsat` (15,900 + 29,400), and the lattice minimum equals\n912\tthe optimum on all 4,916 instances it was computed for.\n913\t**Method notes.** *The itertuples row of a DataFrame does not pickle* across a\n914\tpool; pass `to_dict(\"records\")` and wrap. *tabulate prints integers above\n915\t1,000 as `1.09e+03` under `floatfmt`*; format the frame to strings first.\n916\t*Greedy re-covering can return the base's own matrix class* — 44 of 5,600 were\n917\ta column permutation of the base — so \"matrix differs\" and \"matrix class\n918\tdiffers\" are both reported. *Which way the flip goes matters for nothing\n919\there*: 247 re-coverings became sparse enough for Theorem 2 and 375 too dense,\n920\tand the table above is folded to on-against-off. *The witnesses are the\n921\texpensive-looking part of the artifact and the cheapest to regenerate*: 64 MB\n922\tfor 15,900 instances that re-solve in 170 s, so they are git-ignored and the\n923\tCSVs — 2 MB gzipped, from which every instance regenerates — are committed.\n924\t## 14. Does it hold at 125 × 125? The n ≤ 40 laws against the Chu & Stuckey corpus (§2.8, scaling only, item 06)\n925\t*loop0002 iteration 6, 2026-09-26. Code: `learning/scale_test.py`. Regenerate:*\n926\t```bash\n927\tpython -m learning.scale_test --refute --workers 16 --deadline 1500 --wall-budget 4200   # the 50-125 node counts -> learning/data/ensemble/scale_nodes.csv (resumable)\n928\tpython -m learning.scale_test                                                             # reports/scale_test_tables.md + reports/figures/scale_test.png, ~15 s\n929\tpython -m pytest tests/test_scale_test.py -q                                              # 8 tests on hand-checkable data\n930\t```\n931\t*Reads the campaign (`learning/data/ensemble/results.csv`, §10), the corpus\n932\tfeature table (`learning/data/instances.csv`), the n = 30 and 40 node counts of\n933\t§3 (`learning/data/node_counts.csv`), `recertify/results.json`, and the node\n934\tcounts this item refuted itself (`learning/data/ensemble/scale_nodes.csv`,\n935\tcommitted with the code). Writes nothing under `solutions/`; no solver default\n936\tchanged; `_lower_bound` untouched. Every table below is in full in\n937\t`reports/scale_test_tables.md`.*\n938\t**Question.** Items 03 and 04 fitted two laws on generated instances with at\n939\tmost 40 customers: the refutation node count grows exponentially with `n` at\n940\tfixed density (§11, doubling every 2.9–3.5 customers on the ridge, every\n941\t4.5–4.9 off it), and the mean optimum is `E[opt] ≈ a(1 − q) + n[1 − √(1 − q)·c/(D + c)]`\n942\tin the random-intersection-graph parameters `q = 1 − (1 − p²)^m`,\n943\t`D = (n − 1)q` (§12). Taken exactly as fitted, do they predict (a) the certified\n944\toptimum and (b) the nodes to refute `optimum − 1` for Chu & Stuckey's 200\n945\t`Random-n-m-d` corpus instances at `n = 30, 40, 50, 75, 100, 125`? At what\n946\tsize does each prediction leave a conformal interval calibrated on `n ≤ 40`?\n947\tThe plan's kill: if coverage collapses across the size boundary, the intervals\n948\tsay nothing at large sizes and must not be quoted there.\n949\t**Data, and what had to be computed.** The item's brief said the 50–125 node\n950\tcounts were in the compute ledger. **They are not**: `compute_ledger.csv` has\n951\ta `nodes` column and no row carries a value (the column post-dates the runs\n952\tthat filled the corpus). The only large counts on record were the five\n953\t125 × 125 refutations of `recertify/results.json`. So this item refuted\n954\t`optimum − 1` itself for the 140 corpus instances at `n ≥ 50` outside the two\n955\tday-long classes (`Random-125-125-2` and `-4`, where the recertify counts stand\n956\tin), under both `learning.node_counts` configurations, 16 workers, a 1,500 s\n957\tdeadline per call. A call that hits the deadline is **censored**: its count is\n958\ta lower bound and enters coverage only when that lower bound already exceeds\n959\tthe interval, otherwise as \"undetermined\". The recertify configuration\n960\t(`better_move=True, better_move_dominators=0, memo=True`) is exactly the\n961\t`csearch` configuration on instances with at most five products per customer\n962\t(`sparse_enough_for_better_move` is true for all five), so those five counts\n963\tare `csearch` observations, and not `default` ones.\n964\t**Method.** Fits use campaign `n ≤ 30`; conformal radii are the\n965\t⌈(N + 1)·0.9⌉/N quantile of |residual| on the campaign's `n = 35` and `40`\n966\tinstances (split conformal, so an \"`n ≤ 40`\" interval is honest about the\n967\t5–10 customers of extrapolation it already contains); coverage is then read on\n968\tthe corpus by band (30–40, 50, 75, 100, 125; 50 instances in the first, 25 in\n969\teach other, of which the 125 band has 15 `default` and 20 `csearch`\n970\tobservations). Two radii per estimate: an **absolute** one, and one **scaled\n971\twith `n`** (the campaign's residual spread grows roughly linearly in `n` for\n972\tthe optimum, §12), whose width at 50 and 125 is printed beside it, because a\n973\twide enough interval \"holds\" trivially. A 90% interval is said to hold on a\n974\tband if at least 80% of the determined observations fall inside it (the\n975\tbinomial slack of 25 draws), and the deliverable is the first band where it\n976\tdoes not. For the optimum: the §12 form with constants refit on `n ≤ 30`\n977\t(nominal `p = d/n`, and realised `p` = matrix density), the §12 constants\n978\tthemselves, and the baselines `tw_min_fill + 1` (§4's best point estimate),\n979\tthe §5 sandwich midpoint, `lb_best`, `ub_best`, and a LightGBM on\n980\t**scale-free** graph features predicting `optimum / n` (trees cannot\n981\textrapolate in `n`, so every feature is a ratio). For the nodes: §11's cell\n982\tlaw `log10(median) = a_d + b_d·n` per fixed `m = n` density, interpolated in\n983\t`log10 col_mean` between adjacent densities (Chu & Stuckey's \"d = 2\" has\n984\t`col_mean` 2.7–2.8, §11), the same fitted on 15–40 as §11 did, §11's\n985\tpre-registered ridge law (`d = 3`), and a linear **surface** in `n`,\n986\t`log10 col_mean`, `optimum / n`, mean degree and their products, fitted on all\n987\tfour campaign series at `n ≤ 30` (linear in `n`, hence extrapolable; §13\n988\tlicenses graph-only features). Then the laws are read off the corpus itself:\n989\tper density class, the class-median nodes at each `n` where all five counts\n990\tare uncensored, fitted exponential against power law with `fit_scaling` over\n991\t30–125, and the class-mean optimum against `n` as a line.\n992\t**Baseline.** For the optimum, `tw_min_fill + 1`. For the nodes, §11's own\n993\tfits extrapolated, which is what a reader of §11 would do.\n994\t### (a) The optimum: the line holds to 125, the formula's constants do not, and no interval survives 100\n995\tConstants refit on the campaign's `n ≤ 30` cell means: `a = 2.26`, `c = 27.3`\n996\t(§12 on `n ≤ 40`: 2.1, 27). Error in stacks over the 200 corpus instances, and\n997\tthe share inside the 90% interval calibrated on campaign `n = 35, 40`\n998\t(absolute radius, and the `n`-scaled radius whose width at 125 is in the\n999\tlast column):\n1000\tEvery absolute interval that covers at 30–40 has stopped covering by 100,\n1001\twhere the best of them (the realised-`p` formula) holds 40% of the instances\n1002\tinside a ±2.9-stack band; `tw_min_fill + 1`, exact on 85.7% of the corpus\n1003\t(§4), is off by 4.2 stacks on average at 125 and inside its ±1 band on 2 of\n1004\t25. The `n`-scaled intervals \"hold\" for the formula and for `ub_best`, and\n1005\tsay nothing: at 125 they are ±9.8 and ±6.3 stacks wide on optima of 23–103.\n1006\t**Kill criterion met for the optimum**: the coverage of every interval\n1007\tcalibrated on `n ≤ 40` collapses between 50 and 100, and no interval from\n1008\tthis section is to be quoted at `n ≥ 75`.\n1009\tWhere the formula goes wrong is specific. Per class (mean signed error,\n1010\trealised `p`): `d = 2` +1.6 at 30, +1.8 at 40, +2.4 at 50, +5.1 at 75, +5.6\n1011\tat 100, +8.1 at 125 — a bias growing linearly in `n`; `d = 4` within ±1.5 at\n1012\tevery size; `d = 6, 8, 10` within −1.9 … −0.2 at every size from 30 to 125. The\n1013\t`m ≠ n` classes are worse (`Random-100-50-2`: +10.6; `Random-50-100-2`: −0.2\n1014\twith realised `p` but −5.8 nominal), which is the `m` in `q = 1 − (1 − p²)^m`\n1015\tbeing carried through without ever having been tested off `m ∈ {n, 2n}`.\n1016\tWhat the corpus says about `E[opt]` itself is cleaner than the formula. The\n1017\tclass-mean optimum against `n` at fixed density, `m = n`, six sizes from 30 to\n1018\t125:\n1019\t**§12's linearity holds through 125** — `E[opt] = α(density)·n + β` with\n1020\tr² ≥ 0.986 in every class and no class mean more than 1.2 stacks off its\n1021\tline. What fails is the constants: the intercept is **4–5 stacks, not the\n1022\tformula's `a(1 − q) ≈ 2.2`**, and the slope at `col_mean` 2.8 (`D ≈ 8`) is\n1023\t0.146 against the formula's 0.22, while at `D ≥ 18` the formula's slopes\n1024\t0.40 / 0.58 / 0.70 / 0.79 match the measured 0.40 / 0.60 / 0.72 / 0.80 to\n1025\t0.02. At `n ≤ 40` the low intercept and the high sparse slope cancel (2.2 +\n1026\t0.22·40 = 11.0 against the measured 11.0 at `Random-40-40-2`), which is how a\n1027\tformula fitted there scored MAE 0.49 on cell means and still runs away at 125.\n1028\tSo `opt / n` at density 2.8 is **still falling at 125** (0.182), heading for\n1029\t0.146 and not the 0.21 the formula's `c/(D + c)` saturation predicts, and the\n1030\t§11 remark that the 125 × 125 density-2 classes sit *below* the `opt / n ≈ 0.3`\n1031\tpeak band is confirmed with its mechanism: at fixed sparse density the\n1032\tfraction falls as `0.146 + 5/n`.\n1033\t### (b) The nodes: exponential to 125, at rates the campaign overstates by 0.01–0.02 per customer\n1034\tThe counts this item computed (default configuration; `csearch` differs on\n1035\tthe 47 settled pairs where `better_move` is on, see the note at the end of\n1036\tthis part): 280 calls, 273 settled,\n1037\t7 censored at 1,500 s, all seven in `Random-100-100-2` at 2.1–5.4 × 10⁹ nodes\n1038\tand still running; 5.65 core-hours, 27 minutes wall on 16 workers. Class\n1039\tmedians of `log10 nodes` against the laws (default configuration; the\n1040\t`Random-125-125-2` and `-4` rows are the recertify counts, `csearch`):\n1041\t**The form holds.** Read off the corpus alone, with `fit_scaling` on the class\n1042\tmedians over every size where all five counts settled (30–125 for `d = 6, 8,\n1043\t10`; 30–100 for `d = 4`; 30–75 for `d = 2`), the exponential beats the power\n1044\tlaw in every class, by a factor of 3–5 in RMS residual (0.036–0.10 against\n1045\t0.21–0.49 log10), on five or six points spanning a factor of 2.5–4 in `n` — a\n1046\tfar stronger test than §11's six points over 15–40. The local rate between\n1047\tconsecutive sizes does not decline with `n` (`d = 6`: 0.078, 0.054, 0.079,\n1048\t0.064, 0.082 per customer over 30 → 40 → 50 → 75 → 100 → 125): **at fixed\n1049\tdensity the refutation grows exponentially in `n` from 30 to 125.**\n1050\t**The rates do not, quite.** Rate per customer, corpus 30–125 against the\n1051\tcampaign's `n ≤ 40` cell (§11 table):\n1052\tEvery campaign rate is above the corpus's by 0.005–0.018 log10 per\n1053\tcustomer — a fifth to a quarter of the rate itself, invisible at `n ≤ 40`\n1054\t(0.018 × 10 customers is a factor of 1.5, inside a cell's spread) and a\n1055\tfactor of 6–34 at 125. The §11 law interpolated in `col_mean` is therefore\n1056\tright on average at 30–40 (bias +0.02 log10), and over by +0.39 at 50, +0.52\n1057\tat 75, +0.68 at 100 and **+1.29 at 125** (15 observations, `d = 6, 8, 10`),\n1058\tgrowing with density: +1.1 at `d = 6`, +1.15 at `d = 8`, +1.8 at `d = 10`.\n1059\tTwo exceptions run the other way and are the ones that matter for the day-long\n1060\tclasses. The **`d = 4` law holds to 125**: 11.5 predicted against 10.7–11.4\n1061\tobserved on three counts, over by 0.1–0.8. And the **ridge law pre-registered\n1062\tin §11 — 5 × 10¹¹ nodes at 125 — lands within a factor of 3 of the two\n1063\tdensity-2 counts on record, 1.6 and 1.7 × 10¹¹**, after an extrapolation of\n1064\teight decades from `n = 40`; it tracks the whole `d = 2` class (2.66 / 3.61 /\n1065\t4.56 / 6.94 / 9.32 / 11.6 against 2.84 / 3.64 / 4.35 / 6.42 / ≥ 9.08–9.73 /\n1066\t11.2), never more than 0.5 log10 off. The interpolated law under-predicts that\n1067\tclass (10.5 at 125, 8.47 at 100 against lower bounds of 9.5–9.73) because it\n1068\tblends the `d = 3` cell with the campaign's `d = 2` cell, which is 50–64%\n1069\tdecomposable at `n ≥ 20` (§10) and easy for it; Chu & Stuckey's \"density 2\"\n1070\tis the ridge, as §11 placed it, and the ridge law is its law. The two\n1071\tday-long classes are thus the two the `n ≤ 40` laws predict best, to within\n1072\ta factor of 3, and the classes they predict worst are the ones that take\n1073\tseconds.\n1074\t**The `d = 2` class stays above the `d = 4` class through 125.** Class\n1075\tmedians, `d = 2` over `d = 4`: ×4.1 at 30, ×3.7 at 40, ×3.1 at 50, ×1.8 at 75,\n1076\t**≥ ×6 at 100** (four lower bounds of 3.2–5.4 × 10⁹ against a settled median\n1077\tof 5.1 × 10⁸), ×2.7 at 125 (medians of two and three `csearch` counts,\n1078\t1.65 × 10¹¹ against 0.61 × 10¹¹). §11 left open whether the peak at 125 would\n1079\thave moved to the density-4 side, as it must if `optimum / n ≈ 0.3` is the\n1080\torder parameter, since at 125 the density-2 classes have `optimum / n` 0.18.\n1081\tIt has not: the peak stays at `col_mean` 2.7–3 while `optimum / n` there\n1082\tfalls as `0.146 + 5 / n` (part a). **`col_mean` at fixed `m / n`, not\n1083\t`optimum / n`, is the size-stable location of the ridge**, on the evidence of\n1084\ttwo classes with two to five counts each at 100 and 125.\n1085\t**Theorem 2 saves more at 50–125 than at `n ≤ 40`** (a §13 follow-up that\n1086\tcame free). On the 47 settled pairs where the `csearch` rule turns\n1087\t`better_move` on (mean products per customer ≤ 5), `csearch / default` nodes\n1088\tare median 0.80 at `n = 50` (10 pairs, range 0.34–0.94), 0.83 at 75 (10,\n1089\t0.53–0.90) and **0.63 at 100** (27, 0.12–0.95), against §13's 0.87–0.90 at\n1090\t`n ≤ 40`; by class at 100: 0.69 at `Random-100-100-4`, 0.42 at\n1091\t`Random-100-50-2`, **0.17 at `Random-100-50-4`** (a 6× saving, five of five\n1092\tinstances). The saving grows with `n` and is largest at `m = n / 2`, a ratio\n1093\tthe campaign never generated. It never costs. So a `default`-configuration\n1094\tlaw over-predicts a `csearch` count by up to 0.8 log10 at 100, which is a\n1095\tfurther reason the recertify counts are compared with `csearch` laws only.\n1096\t**Coverage collapses at the first band beyond calibration.** The surface in\n1097\t`(n, log col_mean, optimum / n, degree)`, the best-calibrated predictor at\n1098\t35–40 (MAE 0.22 log10, 90% radius ±0.42), covers 0.92 at 30–40, **0.72 at\n1099\t50**, 0.40 at 75, 0.20 at 100, 0.27 at 125 — it fails at 50 and never\n1100\trecovers, with bias +0.9 at 100 and +1.3 at 125. The cell law's split-conformal\n1101\tradius is ±1.54 log10 (its 15–30 fit has no row for `d ≥ 8`, whose cell\n1102\tmedians are under 2 nodes below `n = 25`, so it clamps dense classes to the\n1103\t`d = 7` law — the 15–40 law is the honest one and is what the tables above\n1104\tuse); even that covers 0.74 at 50, 0.60 at 75, 0.50 at 100 and 0.13 at 125.\n1105\tThe `n`-scaled radii \"hold\" at ±1.4 (surface) to ±4.9 (cell law) decades at\n1106\t125, and mean nothing at that width. **Kill criterion met for the nodes as\n1107\twell**: no interval calibrated at `n ≤ 40` is to be quoted at `n ≥ 50`.\n1108\t**Finding, in one paragraph.** Of the laws fitted at `n ≤ 40`, the *forms*\n1109\tsurvive the trip to 125 and the *constants* do not, and every calibrated\n1110\tinterval fails at the first size beyond its calibration. The optimum's mean is\n1111\tlinear in `n` at fixed density from 30 to 125 in all five Chu & Stuckey classes\n1112\t(r² ≥ 0.986, class means within 1.2 stacks of the line), but with intercept\n1113\t4–5 stacks where the §12 formula has 2.2 and, at 2.8 customers per product,\n1114\tslope 0.146 where the formula saturates at 0.22, so the formula's bias grows\n1115\tlinearly to +8 stacks at `Random-125-125-2` while staying within ±2 at\n1116\tdensities 4–10; `tw_min_fill + 1`, exact on 86% of the corpus, is +4.2 at 125.\n1117\tThe refutation grows exponentially in `n` at fixed density through 125 in\n1118\tevery class (power law 3–5× worse), at rates 0.046–0.093 per customer that\n1119\tthe campaign's `n ≤ 40` cells overstate by 0.005–0.018, which compounds to a\n1120\tfactor of 6–34 at 125 for densities 6–10 and to a factor of 3 or less for the\n1121\ttwo classes that take a day — the `d = 4` law and §11's pre-registered ridge\n1122\tlaw (5 × 10¹¹ against 1.6–1.7 × 10¹¹) — so the campaign's claim that those\n1123\tclasses are hard because they sit on an exponential ridge with the steepest\n1124\trate is upheld at 125, within an order of magnitude and eight decades of\n1125\textrapolation. The `d = 2` class stays 2–6× harder than `d = 4` at every size\n1126\tto 125 while its `optimum / n` falls from 0.29 to 0.18, so the ridge's\n1127\tsize-stable coordinate is customers per product, not `optimum / n`. Conformal\n1128\tintervals for both quantities cover 0.92–1.00 at 30–40 and 0.40–0.74 at 50–75,\n1129\tcollapsing to 0.08–0.27 at 100–125 for every absolute radius; the `n`-scaled\n1130\tones hold only by being 6–10 stacks or 1.4–4.9 decades wide. **Kill met:\n1131\tnothing from §11, §12 or this section is to be quoted with an interval at\n1132\t`n ≥ 50`.**\n1133\t**Where each finding stops holding.**\n1134\t**Size range covered.** Chu & Stuckey's 200 `Random-n-m-d` corpus instances,\n1135\t`n ∈ {30, 40, 50, 75, 100, 125}`, `m ∈ {n, 2n, n/2}` (the last two at\n1136\t`n = 50` and `100` only), `d ∈ {2, 4, 6, 8, 10}`, realised `col_mean` 2.6–10.2,\n1137\toptima 8.6–103.2, certified; node counts on all 200 under `default` except\n1138\tthe two 125 × 125 day-long classes, and under `csearch` on 190 (five of the\n1139\tten from recertify, five without any count); 7 of 280 calls censored. The\n1140\tcampaign side is §10's 37,800 instances at 10–40. Every rate above 40 rests on\n1141\tclass medians of five instances, and the 125 rows on two and three counts —\n1142\tenough to place a decade, not a factor of two. The `m ≠ n` classes have no\n1143\tcampaign law at `m = n / 2`, and the `m = 2n` law over-predicts the\n1144\t`Random-50-100` classes by 0.5 log10 at `d ≥ 4`; the formula is worst there\n1145\t(+10.6 at `Random-100-50-2`).\n1146\t**Kill criterion** (§2.8: coverage collapses across the size boundary →\n1147\tintervals not to be quoted at large sizes): **met, for both quantities and\n1148\tevery estimate.** The section quotes point predictions and their measured\n1149\terrors at 50–125 and no interval.\n1150\t**Method notes.** *The ledger has no node counts.* The brief said the 50–125\n1151\tcounts were there; `compute_ledger.csv` has the column and no values, so the\n1152\tcounts were computed here (5.65 core-hours) and are committed as\n1153\t`scale_nodes.csv`. *Recertify is `csearch`.* Its configuration\n1154\t(`better_move=True, better_move_dominators=0, memo=True`) equals the `csearch`\n1155\trule on any instance with ≤ 5 products per customer, which all five are\n1156\t(2.7–4.3); they are therefore compared with the `csearch` laws and never with\n1157\t`default`. *Censoring is a lower bound, not a missing value.* A call that hits\n1158\tthe deadline reports the nodes it visited; the four censored `Random-100-100-2`\n1159\tcalls already exceed the interpolated law's interval and are the reason the\n1160\tridge law, not the interpolation, is that class's law. *Fit on 15–30 loses the\n1161\tdense classes.* The split-conformal design fits on `n ≤ 30`, where the `d ≥ 8`\n1162\tcells have medians under 2 nodes at `n ≤ 25` and no law can be fitted, so the\n1163\t15–30 cell law clamps them to `d = 7`; the tables compare against the 15–40 law\n1164\t(§11's own) for that reason, and its radius is not available since 40 is in\n1165\tits fit. *Seconds per node moved with `n`*: 0.28–0.5 µs at 100, 0.55 µs on the\n1166\trecertify 125s, 0.9 µs at 125 `d = 6` — seconds are still not a hardness\n1167\tmeasure (§9). *A column-key collision* silently overwrote the 15–30 law's\n1168\tcolumn with the 15–40 law's in the per-class table's first draft; caught by\n1169\treading the table against the summary, now keyed by full name.\n1170\t## 15. The differential harness: is every refutation sound under relabelling? (plan 2 §2.1(a), loop0003 item 01)\n1171\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"85602c8d-df04-47a8-a103-12762ff7e492","uuid":"35352902-7940-427b-8f74-460c48d0df5b","timestamp":"2026-09-26T19:23:34.465Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/85602c8d-df04-47a8-a103-12762ff7e492/tool-results/b0jmjz0m9.txt","content":"its row's `(base_name, method, k)` through `recover`, the base from the\ncampaign manifest), the re-covered witnesses under\n`learning/data/ensemble/recover_solutions/` (64 MB, git-ignored, re-solved in\nthree minutes), and every table below in full in\n`reports/graph_story_tables.md`. Nothing was written to `solutions/`.*\n**Question.** The optimum is `pathwidth(MOSP graph) + 1`, a function of the\ngraph alone. Is the *hardness* — the nodes the complete customer search\nvisits to refute `optimum − 1` — a function of the graph too, or does the\nmatrix (which cliques cover which edges) carry information the reduction\ndiscards? And §8's two counts: is the number of optimal closing orders under\nthe search measure (`count_search`) equal on two instances with the same\ngraph, and is the number under the construction value (`count_closing`) free\nto differ? **Kill**: graph-only features predict nodes as well as the full set.\n**What the code says before any measurement.** `customer_search.decide` and\nits C port consume `_neighbour_masks(instance)` — the self-inclusive\nneighbourhoods `N[c]` of the MOSP graph, indexed by customer — and nothing\nelse of the matrix. So under the default configuration the node count is a\nfunction of the *labelled* graph, and the measurement below is a test of the\nimplementation rather than of the problem. The matrix enters in exactly one\nplace: the `csearch` configuration turns Theorem 2 (`better_move`) on when the\nmean number of products per customer is at most 5\n(`sparse_enough_for_better_move`), and a re-covering can move that mean across\nthe threshold. Everything else that differs between two matrices with one\ngraph — the upper-bound heuristics simulate the *constructed* product order,\n`lb_trivial` is a column sum, the SAT encoding has a position variable per\nproduct — is outside the refutation being counted. The study therefore\nseparates three things and says which is which.\n**Method.** Three sources of pairs with the same graph and different products.\n(1) *Re-coverings with fixed labels*, the item's construction: 1,400 base\ninstances from the campaign, 200 per `n ∈ {10, 15, …, 40}`, one per\nisomorphism class, non-complete, half drawn uniformly and half from the\nhardest decile by nodes so the ratios have room to move (base nodes median 9\nat n = 10 → 3,850 at n = 40, max 30,755). For each base, four re-coverings by\neach of three methods: **split** one product `P` into `A ⊂ P` and `B`, where\n`B` is `P \\ A` plus every vertex of `A` whose edge into `P \\ A` no other\nproduct covers (so the cut's crossing edges are kept where needed and left to\nthe other products where they can be); **merge** two overlapping products\nwhose union is a clique of the graph, preferring a pair neither of which\ncontains the other; and a fresh **greedy edge-clique cover**, maximal cliques\ngrown from uncovered edges in a shuffled order, with a singleton product kept\nfor every customer that had products but no neighbours, so the set of\ncustomers that ever open is unchanged. Each re-covering is seeded from\n`(base_name, method, k)`, checked to have the same neighbour masks and nauty\ncertificate as its base, **solved independently** with `solve_mosp_exact`\ninto its own solutions directory, its witness re-simulated, and `optimum − 1`\nrefuted under both `node_counts` configurations; the base is re-refuted in\nthe same process and compared with the campaign CSV. (2) *Relabellings*: 20\nrandom row-and-column permutations of each base, refuted under both\nconfigurations. Every corpus pair with one graph and different products also\ndiffers in labelling, so this is the variance no graph-invariant can see and\nthe floor against which the kill test has to be read. (3) *The corpus*: §1's\n150 graph classes at n ≤ 40 holding several matrix classes, with their node\ncounts from `learning/data/node_counts.csv`. For §8's counts,\n`learning.degeneracy.analyse` on every re-covered pair at n ≤ 15 (the `2^n`\nlattice is pure Python). The kill test predicts `log10(1 + nodes)` on the\ndeduplicated campaign (27,321 rows, one per class per cell) with LightGBM\n(400 trees) under `GroupKFold(5)` by MOSP-graph class, from three feature\nsets built from `learning.features` group names: **graph-only** (the graph\ngroup, the label-free invariants `tw_min_fill`, `tw_min_degree`, `bw_rcm`,\n`spectral_radius`, `fiedler`, `fiedler_lcc`, `sep_size`, `sep_frac`,\n`lb_contraction`, and the optimum, itself a graph invariant: 21 features),\n**matrix-only** (the matrix group and the optimum: 18) and **full** (all 49\nand the optimum: 50); variants without the optimum and with `nodes_csearch`\nas target. Paired statistics are on `log10(1 + nodes)`; Wilcoxon on the\nnon-zero differences.\n**Baseline.** The hypothesis the plan states: two matrices with the same\ngraph cost the search differently, so the matrix carries information the\nreduction discards. For the kill test, the per-size median (what size alone\nknows) and the matrix-only set.\n### Re-coverings with fixed labels: 15,900 pairs, every default node count equal\n15,900 applicable re-coverings (merge needs two overlapping products whose\nunion is a clique, split a product of three or more customers; 900 attempts\nfound none), 14,280 distinct matrices in 12,031 matrix classes, all 15,900\nwith the base's neighbour masks and nauty certificate. All 15,900 solved\nindependently to the base's optimum with a witness that re-simulates — 15,900\nfree audits of Yanasse's equality, none failed. **Under the default\nconfiguration the node count is equal to the base's in 15,900 of 15,900\npairs**: median and maximum `|log10 ratio|` are 0, the Wilcoxon test has no\nnon-zero differences to run on. All 1,400 base re-refutations reproduce the\ncampaign CSV exactly.\nUnder `csearch` the count differs in 394 pairs, and **every one of the 394 is\namong the 622 pairs where the re-covering moved the mean products per\ncustomer across the `better_move` threshold** (0 differ outside them). That\nis the one matrix effect, and it is an effect of the configuration rule, not\nof the search. Read as Theorem 2 on against off on the same labelled graph:\nTheorem 2 saves 10–13% of the nodes at the median for n ≥ 25, up to 20–25% at\nthe tenth percentile, and never costs a node (on > off: 0 of 622). The\nre-coverings that flip it are mostly the greedy cover (536 of 622): maximal\ncliques are fewer and larger than the generator's products, and the mean\nproducts per customer moves by −2.0 to +1.7 (interquartile), either way\nacross 5.\n### Relabellings of the same matrix: the label floor\n29,400 refutations (20 relabellings and the base, 1,400 bases), all `unsat`.\nRenaming the customers — the same matrix, the same graph — changes the\ndefault node count on 588 of the 800 bases at n ≥ 25, by 2–6% at the median\nover 20 relabellings and 14–18% at the ninetieth percentile, at most 2.26×;\nunder `csearch` the spread is three to four times wider (p90 1.4–1.7×, max\n8.1×), because Theorem 2's dominance depends on which of several tied\ncandidates the index order puts first. **The labelling moves the count; the\nmatrix, with labels fixed, does not.** The mean absolute deviation of\n`log10(1 + nodes)` within a base, 0.005–0.008 (default) and 0.012–0.026\n(`csearch`), is the floor below which no label-free predictor can go.\n### §8's counts within re-covered pairs (n = 10 and 15, 4,516 pairs)\nAs §8 predicted: **`count_search` is equal in 4,516 of 4,516 pairs** (it is a\ngraph invariant, and the lattice minimum equals the optimum on every one),\nand **`count_closing` is not** — a greedy re-cover leaves it unchanged on 19\nof 1,600 pairs and multiplies it by 2.9 (n = 10) and 4.5 (n = 15) at the\nmedian, by 13–40× at the ninetieth percentile and by up to 1,540×. The\ndirection is systematic: covering the same edges with fewer, larger products\nmakes more closing orders construct to the optimum; splitting a product makes\nfewer (median ratio 0.94–0.97, minimum 0.11). So the number of optimal\nclosing orders *under the search measure* is a property of the graph and the\nnumber *under the construction value* is a property of the matrix, by up to\nthree orders of magnitude at 15 customers.\n### The corpus pairs are uninformative\n§1's 150 graph classes at n ≤ 40 with several matrix classes hold 2,796\nmatrix classes and instances (Simonis, Harvey, Faggioli–Bentivoglio, and the\ntwo Wilson/Challenge `NWRS7`/`NWRS8` pairs). Node counts never differ within\na class under either configuration — but they could not: the classes are\nnear-complete graphs (optimum n − 1 or n − 2 at n = 10, 15, 20, 30; 28–29 at\nn = 30) refuted in 0–3 nodes, and the maximum in the whole set is 13, the two\n`NWRS` pairs at n = 25 which are the same matrix with one all-zero column\nremoved. The corpus offers no pair on which the question has room to move;\nthe generated pairs above are the evidence.\n### The kill test: graph-only features predict nodes as well as the full set\n`log10(1 + nodes_default)`, deduplicated campaign, `GroupKFold(5)` by graph\nclass:\nGraph-only beats the full set by 0.001–0.002 MAE on both targets and both\nrow sets, and is within 0.003 of it without the optimum; by size it is ahead\nat n ≥ 25 and behind by at most 0.001 at n ≤ 20 (table in\n`reports/graph_story_tables.md`). Matrix-only is 25–30% worse everywhere.\nBoth are an order of magnitude above the relabelling floor (0.005–0.008), so\nwhat remains is model and feature error, not label noise. **Kill criterion\nmet**: the 29 matrix, bound and matrix-derived invariant features add nothing\nto the prediction of hardness that the 21 graph features do not already carry.\n**Finding.** **No: the matrix is not part of the story for hardness, as\nhardness is measured here.** With the customer labels fixed, re-covering the\nsame edge set with different cliques — 15,900 re-coverings of 1,400\ninstances, 12,031 matrix classes, products removed or added by up to 30 —\nleaves the default node count to refute `optimum − 1` *exactly* unchanged in\nevery pair, and the kill test agrees from the other direction: graph-only\nfeatures predict nodes as well as graph-plus-matrix features (MAE 0.095\nagainst 0.097 in log10), and matrix-only features are 25–30% worse. Two\nthings do move the count, and neither is the clique cover. The **labelling**\ndoes — renaming the customers of the same matrix changes the default count\non three bases in four at n ≥ 25, by 2–6% at the median and up to 2.3×, and\nby up to 8× under `csearch` — which is a fact about the search's tie-breaks,\ninvisible to every graph invariant and the floor for any label-free\npredictor. And the **configuration rule** does: `csearch` turns Theorem 2 on\nby a matrix statistic, mean products per customer ≤ 5, and a re-covering that\ncrosses it changes the count by 10–13% at the median, up to 25%, always in\nTheorem 2's favour; that is the only channel through which the matrix reaches\nthe refutation, and it is a design choice of `sparse_enough_for_better_move`,\nnot a property of the problem. Where the matrix *does* carry information is\nexactly where §8 said: the number of optimal closing orders under the\nconstruction value, which the same graph's re-coverings multiply or divide by\nup to three orders of magnitude at n = 15 while the count under the search\nmeasure is identical in all 4,516 pairs. Part of this finding is a theorem\nabout the code — the search reads neighbour masks and nothing else — and the\nmeasurement's value is that it confirms the C port and the Python behave as\nthe code says on 15,900 pairs, quantifies the two exceptions, and hands §2.8\na clean statement: hardness is a function of the labelled graph, so a\npredictor of nodes at 125 × 125 needs graph features and a label-noise term,\nand no matrix feature. For the SAT encoding, whose formula has a position\nvariable per product, the story would be different; it is not the hardness\nmeasured here.\n**Size range covered.** Re-coverings and relabellings at n ∈ {10, 15, 20, 25,\n30, 35, 40}, m ∈ {n, 2n} before re-covering, 200 bases per size with base\nnode counts up to 30,755; the §8 counts at n = 10 and 15 only (4,516 pairs);\nthe corpus pairs at n ≤ 40; the kill test on the whole deduplicated campaign\n(10 ≤ n ≤ 40). The exact-equality result is structural and does not depend\non size; the sizes of the two exceptions (label spread, Theorem 2's saving)\nare measured to n = 40 and nothing here says how they scale to 125.\n**Kill criterion.** Met: graph-only MAE 0.0948 against full 0.0968 on all\nrows, 0.0937 against 0.0952 on non-complete rows (tolerance 0.01); met again\non `nodes_csearch` and within 0.003 without the optimum.\n**Not a bound, not a solver change.** No default changed; `_lower_bound` and\nevery decision path are untouched; nothing was written to `solutions/`. No\nfinding rests on a handful of instances: every re-covered instance was solved\nindependently and matched its base's optimum (15,900 of 15,900), every\nrefutation returned `unsat` (15,900 + 29,400), and the lattice minimum equals\nthe optimum on all 4,916 instances it was computed for.\n**Method notes.** *The itertuples row of a DataFrame does not pickle* across a\npool; pass `to_dict(\"records\")` and wrap. *tabulate prints integers above\n1,000 as `1.09e+03` under `floatfmt`*; format the frame to strings first.\n*Greedy re-covering can return the base's own matrix class* — 44 of 5,600 were\na column permutation of the base — so \"matrix differs\" and \"matrix class\ndiffers\" are both reported. *Which way the flip goes matters for nothing\nhere*: 247 re-coverings became sparse enough for Theorem 2 and 375 too dense,\nand the table above is folded to on-against-off. *The witnesses are the\nexpensive-looking part of the artifact and the cheapest to regenerate*: 64 MB\nfor 15,900 instances that re-solve in 170 s, so they are git-ignored and the\nCSVs — 2 MB gzipped, from which every instance regenerates — are committed.\n## 14. Does it hold at 125 × 125? The n ≤ 40 laws against the Chu & Stuckey corpus (§2.8, scaling only, item 06)\n*loop0002 iteration 6, 2026-09-26. Code: `learning/scale_test.py`. Regenerate:*\n```bash\npython -m learning.scale_test --refute --workers 16 --deadline 1500 --wall-budget 4200   # the 50-125 node counts -> learning/data/ensemble/scale_nodes.csv (resumable)\npython -m learning.scale_test                                                             # reports/scale_test_tables.md + reports/figures/scale_test.png, ~15 s\npython -m pytest tests/test_scale_test.py -q                                              # 8 tests on hand-checkable data\n```\n*Reads the campaign (`learning/data/ensemble/results.csv`, §10), the corpus\nfeature table (`learning/data/instances.csv`), the n = 30 and 40 node counts of\n§3 (`learning/data/node_counts.csv`), `recertify/results.json`, and the node\ncounts this item refuted itself (`learning/data/ensemble/scale_nodes.csv`,\ncommitted with the code). Writes nothing under `solutions/`; no solver default\nchanged; `_lower_bound` untouched. Every table below is in full in\n`reports/scale_test_tables.md`.*\n**Question.** Items 03 and 04 fitted two laws on generated instances with at\nmost 40 customers: the refutation node count grows exponentially with `n` at\nfixed density (§11, doubling every 2.9–3.5 customers on the ridge, every\n4.5–4.9 off it), and the mean optimum is `E[opt] ≈ a(1 − q) + n[1 − √(1 − q)·c/(D + c)]`\nin the random-intersection-graph parameters `q = 1 − (1 − p²)^m`,\n`D = (n − 1)q` (§12). Taken exactly as fitted, do they predict (a) the certified\noptimum and (b) the nodes to refute `optimum − 1` for Chu & Stuckey's 200\n`Random-n-m-d` corpus instances at `n = 30, 40, 50, 75, 100, 125`? At what\nsize does each prediction leave a conformal interval calibrated on `n ≤ 40`?\nThe plan's kill: if coverage collapses across the size boundary, the intervals\nsay nothing at large sizes and must not be quoted there.\n**Data, and what had to be computed.** The item's brief said the 50–125 node\ncounts were in the compute ledger. **They are not**: `compute_ledger.csv` has\na `nodes` column and no row carries a value (the column post-dates the runs\nthat filled the corpus). The only large counts on record were the five\n125 × 125 refutations of `recertify/results.json`. So this item refuted\n`optimum − 1` itself for the 140 corpus instances at `n ≥ 50` outside the two\nday-long classes (`Random-125-125-2` and `-4`, where the recertify counts stand\nin), under both `learning.node_counts` configurations, 16 workers, a 1,500 s\ndeadline per call. A call that hits the deadline is **censored**: its count is\na lower bound and enters coverage only when that lower bound already exceeds\nthe interval, otherwise as \"undetermined\". The recertify configuration\n(`better_move=True, better_move_dominators=0, memo=True`) is exactly the\n`csearch` configuration on instances with at most five products per customer\n(`sparse_enough_for_better_move` is true for all five), so those five counts\nare `csearch` observations, and not `default` ones.\n**Method.** Fits use campaign `n ≤ 30`; conformal radii are the\n⌈(N + 1)·0.9⌉/N quantile of |residual| on the campaign's `n = 35` and `40`\ninstances (split conformal, so an \"`n ≤ 40`\" interval is honest about the\n5–10 customers of extrapolation it already contains); coverage is then read on\nthe corpus by band (30–40, 50, 75, 100, 125; 50 instances in the first, 25 in\neach other, of which the 125 band has 15 `default` and 20 `csearch`\nobservations). Two radii per estimate: an **absolute** one, and one **scaled\nwith `n`** (the campaign's residual spread grows roughly linearly in `n` for\nthe optimum, §12), whose width at 50 and 125 is printed beside it, because a\nwide enough interval \"holds\" trivially. A 90% interval is said to hold on a\nband if at least 80% of the determined observations fall inside it (the\nbinomial slack of 25 draws), and the deliverable is the first band where it\ndoes not. For the optimum: the §12 form with constants refit on `n ≤ 30`\n(nominal `p = d/n`, and realised `p` = matrix density), the §12 constants\nthemselves, and the baselines `tw_min_fill + 1` (§4's best point estimate),\nthe §5 sandwich midpoint, `lb_best`, `ub_best`, and a LightGBM on\n**scale-free** graph features predicting `optimum / n` (trees cannot\nextrapolate in `n`, so every feature is a ratio). For the nodes: §11's cell\nlaw `log10(median) = a_d + b_d·n` per fixed `m = n` density, interpolated in\n`log10 col_mean` between adjacent densities (Chu & Stuckey's \"d = 2\" has\n`col_mean` 2.7–2.8, §11), the same fitted on 15–40 as §11 did, §11's\npre-registered ridge law (`d = 3`), and a linear **surface** in `n`,\n`log10 col_mean`, `optimum / n`, mean degree and their products, fitted on all\nfour campaign series at `n ≤ 30` (linear in `n`, hence extrapolable; §13\nlicenses graph-only features). Then the laws are read off the corpus itself:\nper density class, the class-median nodes at each `n` where all five counts\nare uncensored, fitted exponential against power law with `fit_scaling` over\n30–125, and the class-mean optimum against `n` as a line.\n**Baseline.** For the optimum, `tw_min_fill + 1`. For the nodes, §11's own\nfits extrapolated, which is what a reader of §11 would do.\n### (a) The optimum: the line holds to 125, the formula's constants do not, and no interval survives 100\nConstants refit on the campaign's `n ≤ 30` cell means: `a = 2.26`, `c = 27.3`\n(§12 on `n ≤ 40`: 2.1, 27). Error in stacks over the 200 corpus instances, and\nthe share inside the 90% interval calibrated on campaign `n = 35, 40`\n(absolute radius, and the `n`-scaled radius whose width at 125 is in the\nlast column):\nEvery absolute interval that covers at 30–40 has stopped covering by 100,\nwhere the best of them (the realised-`p` formula) holds 40% of the instances\ninside a ±2.9-stack band; `tw_min_fill + 1`, exact on 85.7% of the corpus\n(§4), is off by 4.2 stacks on average at 125 and inside its ±1 band on 2 of\n25. The `n`-scaled intervals \"hold\" for the formula and for `ub_best`, and\nsay nothing: at 125 they are ±9.8 and ±6.3 stacks wide on optima of 23–103.\n**Kill criterion met for the optimum**: the coverage of every interval\ncalibrated on `n ≤ 40` collapses between 50 and 100, and no interval from\nthis section is to be quoted at `n ≥ 75`.\nWhere the formula goes wrong is specific. Per class (mean signed error,\nrealised `p`): `d = 2` +1.6 at 30, +1.8 at 40, +2.4 at 50, +5.1 at 75, +5.6\nat 100, +8.1 at 125 — a bias growing linearly in `n`; `d = 4` within ±1.5 at\nevery size; `d = 6, 8, 10` within −1.9 … −0.2 at every size from 30 to 125. The\n`m ≠ n` classes are worse (`Random-100-50-2`: +10.6; `Random-50-100-2`: −0.2\nwith realised `p` but −5.8 nominal), which is the `m` in `q = 1 − (1 − p²)^m`\nbeing carried through without ever having been tested off `m ∈ {n, 2n}`.\nWhat the corpus says about `E[opt]` itself is cleaner than the formula. The\nclass-mean optimum against `n` at fixed density, `m = n`, six sizes from 30 to\n125:\n**§12's linearity holds through 125** — `E[opt] = α(density)·n + β` with\nr² ≥ 0.986 in every class and no class mean more than 1.2 stacks off its\nline. What fails is the constants: the intercept is **4–5 stacks, not the\nformula's `a(1 − q) ≈ 2.2`**, and the slope at `col_mean` 2.8 (`D ≈ 8`) is\n0.146 against the formula's 0.22, while at `D ≥ 18` the formula's slopes\n0.40 / 0.58 / 0.70 / 0.79 match the measured 0.40 / 0.60 / 0.72 / 0.80 to\n0.02. At `n ≤ 40` the low intercept and the high sparse slope cancel (2.2 +\n0.22·40 = 11.0 against the measured 11.0 at `Random-40-40-2`), which is how a\nformula fitted there scored MAE 0.49 on cell means and still runs away at 125.\nSo `opt / n` at density 2.8 is **still falling at 125** (0.182), heading for\n0.146 and not the 0.21 the formula's `c/(D + c)` saturation predicts, and the\n§11 remark that the 125 × 125 density-2 classes sit *below* the `opt / n ≈ 0.3`\npeak band is confirmed with its mechanism: at fixed sparse density the\nfraction falls as `0.146 + 5/n`.\n### (b) The nodes: exponential to 125, at rates the campaign overstates by 0.01–0.02 per customer\nThe counts this item computed (default configuration; `csearch` differs on\nthe 47 settled pairs where `better_move` is on, see the note at the end of\nthis part): 280 calls, 273 settled,\n7 censored at 1,500 s, all seven in `Random-100-100-2` at 2.1–5.4 × 10⁹ nodes\nand still running; 5.65 core-hours, 27 minutes wall on 16 workers. Class\nmedians of `log10 nodes` against the laws (default configuration; the\n`Random-125-125-2` and `-4` rows are the recertify counts, `csearch`):\n**The form holds.** Read off the corpus alone, with `fit_scaling` on the class\nmedians over every size where all five counts settled (30–125 for `d = 6, 8,\n10`; 30–100 for `d = 4`; 30–75 for `d = 2`), the exponential beats the power\nlaw in every class, by a factor of 3–5 in RMS residual (0.036–0.10 against\n0.21–0.49 log10), on five or six points spanning a factor of 2.5–4 in `n` — a\nfar stronger test than §11's six points over 15–40. The local rate between\nconsecutive sizes does not decline with `n` (`d = 6`: 0.078, 0.054, 0.079,\n0.064, 0.082 per customer over 30 → 40 → 50 → 75 → 100 → 125): **at fixed\ndensity the refutation grows exponentially in `n` from 30 to 125.**\n**The rates do not, quite.** Rate per customer, corpus 30–125 against the\ncampaign's `n ≤ 40` cell (§11 table):\nEvery campaign rate is above the corpus's by 0.005–0.018 log10 per\ncustomer — a fifth to a quarter of the rate itself, invisible at `n ≤ 40`\n(0.018 × 10 customers is a factor of 1.5, inside a cell's spread) and a\nfactor of 6–34 at 125. The §11 law interpolated in `col_mean` is therefore\nright on average at 30–40 (bias +0.02 log10), and over by +0.39 at 50, +0.52\nat 75, +0.68 at 100 and **+1.29 at 125** (15 observations, `d = 6, 8, 10`),\ngrowing with density: +1.1 at `d = 6`, +1.15 at `d = 8`, +1.8 at `d = 10`.\nTwo exceptions run the other way and are the ones that matter for the day-long\nclasses. The **`d = 4` law holds to 125**: 11.5 predicted against 10.7–11.4\nobserved on three counts, over by 0.1–0.8. And the **ridge law pre-registered\nin §11 — 5 × 10¹¹ nodes at 125 — lands within a factor of 3 of the two\ndensity-2 counts on record, 1.6 and 1.7 × 10¹¹**, after an extrapolation of\neight decades from `n = 40`; it tracks the whole `d = 2` class (2.66 / 3.61 /\n4.56 / 6.94 / 9.32 / 11.6 against 2.84 / 3.64 / 4.35 / 6.42 / ≥ 9.08–9.73 /\n11.2), never more than 0.5 log10 off. The interpolated law under-predicts that\nclass (10.5 at 125, 8.47 at 100 against lower bounds of 9.5–9.73) because it\nblends the `d = 3` cell with the campaign's `d = 2` cell, which is 50–64%\ndecomposable at `n ≥ 20` (§10) and easy for it; Chu & Stuckey's \"density 2\"\nis the ridge, as §11 placed it, and the ridge law is its law. The two\nday-long classes are thus the two the `n ≤ 40` laws predict best, to within\na factor of 3, and the classes they predict worst are the ones that take\nseconds.\n**The `d = 2` class stays above the `d = 4` class through 125.** Class\nmedians, `d = 2` over `d = 4`: ×4.1 at 30, ×3.7 at 40, ×3.1 at 50, ×1.8 at 75,\n**≥ ×6 at 100** (four lower bounds of 3.2–5.4 × 10⁹ against a settled median\nof 5.1 × 10⁸), ×2.7 at 125 (medians of two and three `csearch` counts,\n1.65 × 10¹¹ against 0.61 × 10¹¹). §11 left open whether the peak at 125 would\nhave moved to the density-4 side, as it must if `optimum / n ≈ 0.3` is the\norder parameter, since at 125 the density-2 classes have `optimum / n` 0.18.\nIt has not: the peak stays at `col_mean` 2.7–3 while `optimum / n` there\nfalls as `0.146 + 5 / n` (part a). **`col_mean` at fixed `m / n`, not\n`optimum / n`, is the size-stable location of the ridge**, on the evidence of\ntwo classes with two to five counts each at 100 and 125.\n**Theorem 2 saves more at 50–125 than at `n ≤ 40`** (a §13 follow-up that\ncame free). On the 47 settled pairs where the `csearch` rule turns\n`better_move` on (mean products per customer ≤ 5), `csearch / default` nodes\nare median 0.80 at `n = 50` (10 pairs, range 0.34–0.94), 0.83 at 75 (10,\n0.53–0.90) and **0.63 at 100** (27, 0.12–0.95), against §13's 0.87–0.90 at\n`n ≤ 40`; by class at 100: 0.69 at `Random-100-100-4`, 0.42 at\n`Random-100-50-2`, **0.17 at `Random-100-50-4`** (a 6× saving, five of five\ninstances). The saving grows with `n` and is largest at `m = n / 2`, a ratio\nthe campaign never generated. It never costs. So a `default`-configuration\nlaw over-predicts a `csearch` count by up to 0.8 log10 at 100, which is a\nfurther reason the recertify counts are compared with `csearch` laws only.\n**Coverage collapses at the first band beyond calibration.** The surface in\n`(n, log col_mean, optimum / n, degree)`, the best-calibrated predictor at\n35–40 (MAE 0.22 log10, 90% radius ±0.42), covers 0.92 at 30–40, **0.72 at\n50**, 0.40 at 75, 0.20 at 100, 0.27 at 125 — it fails at 50 and never\nrecovers, with bias +0.9 at 100 and +1.3 at 125. The cell law's split-conformal\nradius is ±1.54 log10 (its 15–30 fit has no row for `d ≥ 8`, whose cell\nmedians are under 2 nodes below `n = 25`, so it clamps dense classes to the\n`d = 7` law — the 15–40 law is the honest one and is what the tables above\nuse); even that covers 0.74 at 50, 0.60 at 75, 0.50 at 100 and 0.13 at 125.\nThe `n`-scaled radii \"hold\" at ±1.4 (surface) to ±4.9 (cell law) decades at\n125, and mean nothing at that width. **Kill criterion met for the nodes as\nwell**: no interval calibrated at `n ≤ 40` is to be quoted at `n ≥ 50`.\n**Finding, in one paragraph.** Of the laws fitted at `n ≤ 40`, the *forms*\nsurvive the trip to 125 and the *constants* do not, and every calibrated\ninterval fails at the first size beyond its calibration. The optimum's mean is\nlinear in `n` at fixed density from 30 to 125 in all five Chu & Stuckey classes\n(r² ≥ 0.986, class means within 1.2 stacks of the line), but with intercept\n4–5 stacks where the §12 formula has 2.2 and, at 2.8 customers per product,\nslope 0.146 where the formula saturates at 0.22, so the formula's bias grows\nlinearly to +8 stacks at `Random-125-125-2` while staying within ±2 at\ndensities 4–10; `tw_min_fill + 1`, exact on 86% of the corpus, is +4.2 at 125.\nThe refutation grows exponentially in `n` at fixed density through 125 in\nevery class (power law 3–5× worse), at rates 0.046–0.093 per customer that\nthe campaign's `n ≤ 40` cells overstate by 0.005–0.018, which compounds to a\nfactor of 6–34 at 125 for densities 6–10 and to a factor of 3 or less for the\ntwo classes that take a day — the `d = 4` law and §11's pre-registered ridge\nlaw (5 × 10¹¹ against 1.6–1.7 × 10¹¹) — so the campaign's claim that those\nclasses are hard because they sit on an exponential ridge with the steepest\nrate is upheld at 125, within an order of magnitude and eight decades of\nextrapolation. The `d = 2` class stays 2–6× harder than `d = 4` at every size\nto 125 while its `optimum / n` falls from 0.29 to 0.18, so the ridge's\nsize-stable coordinate is customers per product, not `optimum / n`. Conformal\nintervals for both quantities cover 0.92–1.00 at 30–40 and 0.40–0.74 at 50–75,\ncollapsing to 0.08–0.27 at 100–125 for every absolute radius; the `n`-scaled\nones hold only by being 6–10 stacks or 1.4–4.9 decades wide. **Kill met:\nnothing from §11, §12 or this section is to be quoted with an interval at\n`n ≥ 50`.**\n**Where each finding stops holding.**\n**Size range covered.** Chu & Stuckey's 200 `Random-n-m-d` corpus instances,\n`n ∈ {30, 40, 50, 75, 100, 125}`, `m ∈ {n, 2n, n/2}` (the last two at\n`n = 50` and `100` only), `d ∈ {2, 4, 6, 8, 10}`, realised `col_mean` 2.6–10.2,\noptima 8.6–103.2, certified; node counts on all 200 under `default` except\nthe two 125 × 125 day-long classes, and under `csearch` on 190 (five of the\nten from recertify, five without any count); 7 of 280 calls censored. The\ncampaign side is §10's 37,800 instances at 10–40. Every rate above 40 rests on\nclass medians of five instances, and the 125 rows on two and three counts —\nenough to place a decade, not a factor of two. The `m ≠ n` classes have no\ncampaign law at `m = n / 2`, and the `m = 2n` law over-predicts the\n`Random-50-100` classes by 0.5 log10 at `d ≥ 4`; the formula is worst there\n(+10.6 at `Random-100-50-2`).\n**Kill criterion** (§2.8: coverage collapses across the size boundary →\nintervals not to be quoted at large sizes): **met, for both quantities and\nevery estimate.** The section quotes point predictions and their measured\nerrors at 50–125 and no interval.\n**Method notes.** *The ledger has no node counts.* The brief said the 50–125\ncounts were there; `compute_ledger.csv` has the column and no values, so the\ncounts were computed here (5.65 core-hours) and are committed as\n`scale_nodes.csv`. *Recertify is `csearch`.* Its configuration\n(`better_move=True, better_move_dominators=0, memo=True`) equals the `csearch`\nrule on any instance with ≤ 5 products per customer, which all five are\n(2.7–4.3); they are therefore compared with the `csearch` laws and never with\n`default`. *Censoring is a lower bound, not a missing value.* A call that hits\nthe deadline reports the nodes it visited; the four censored `Random-100-100-2`\ncalls already exceed the interpolated law's interval and are the reason the\nridge law, not the interpolation, is that class's law. *Fit on 15–30 loses the\ndense classes.* The split-conformal design fits on `n ≤ 30`, where the `d ≥ 8`\ncells have medians under 2 nodes at `n ≤ 25` and no law can be fitted, so the\n15–30 cell law clamps them to `d = 7`; the tables compare against the 15–40 law\n(§11's own) for that reason, and its radius is not available since 40 is in\nits fit. *Seconds per node moved with `n`*: 0.28–0.5 µs at 100, 0.55 µs on the\nrecertify 125s, 0.9 µs at 125 `d = 6` — seconds are still not a hardness\nmeasure (§9). *A column-key collision* silently overwrote the 15–30 law's\ncolumn with the 15–40 law's in the per-class table's first draft; caught by\nreading the table against the summary, now keyed by full name.\n## 15. The differential harness: is every refutation sound under relabelling? (plan 2 §2.1(a), loop0003 item 01)\n","numLines":441,"startLine":731,"totalLines":1171}}}
