{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V4FJRMhCv5hjhdzyHLBywD","type":"tool_result","content":"741\tcells, `results_upward.csv` (§16, whose run straddled the fix) — was made\n742\tbefore the 2026-09-26 fix of the C `better_move` (`reports/better_move_bug.md`\n743\t§7), which measured its cost at n ≤ 40 as +7.3% in total, p90 1.18. This\n744\tstudy's identity rows are post-fix counts for 1,572 of those instances, and\n745\tthe ratio (after + 1)/(before + 1) on the identity labelling, where Theorem 2\n746\tis on, is:\n747\tDense classes, where Theorem 2 is off, are unchanged to the node (all 40\n748\t`Random-*-*-6/8/10` instances, the `default` configuration everywhere). The\n749\tover-pruning the fix removed grew with size on the sparse classes, which is\n750\twhere the day-long refutations are; the 125 × 125 recertify counts (1.6 ×\n751\t10¹¹ nodes, 25–28 h) are pre-fix numbers and the post-fix cost at that size\n752\tis not known. Every §14 and §16 rate for `csearch` is a pre-fix rate.\n753\t### (f) Projection, recommendation, verdict\n754\t**The projected wall clock for the 125 × 125 ridge on 16 cores with a\n755\trelabelling portfolio is the identity's wall clock.** The refutation speed-up\n756\tof min-of-16 is 1.00–1.01 at the median at every size from 40 to 100 and is\n757\tnot growing; the recertify counts say 25–28 h per `Random-125-125-2` instance\n758\tand 10–53 h per `-4` under the pre-fix rule, and §16's revised predictions\n759\t(1.5 × 10¹¹ and 4 × 10¹⁰ nodes) say 23 h and 6 h at 0.55 µs per node; a\n760\tportfolio would return the same numbers on sixteen cores instead of one.\n761\tUnder the fixed rule each is larger by a factor this item measured as ≥ 15 on\n762\tthe one ridge instance at n = 100 where both sides exist, and did not measure\n763\tat 125.\n764\t**Recommendation for `benchmarks.recertify`, stated and not applied: do not\n765\tadd a relabelling portfolio.** One worker per open entry, as it runs today,\n766\tis the right use of the cores — sixteen entries side by side is a sixteen-fold\n767\tthroughput, sixteen labellings of one entry is a one-fold one. What the\n768\tportfolio *would* buy is the satisfiable side: where the witness search at\n769\tthe optimum is hard (identity ≥ 10⁴ nodes, 19% of campaign instances at\n770\tn = 75) min-of-16 saves 8–13× at the median at a core efficiency of 0.5–0.8,\n771\tand the spread there grows with `n`. The consumers of satisfiable calls are\n772\t`benchmarks.ratchet`, `heuristics.restricted_dfs`'s seeded searches and the\n773\t`k ≥ optimum` calls of a descent; a portfolio on those costs little because\n774\tthose calls are short, and it is a driver change for the owner, not made here.\n775\tTwo alerts from the race, outside this item: the recertify run started\n776\t2026-09-24 has three entries still open on the pre-fix C, and its five\n777\tcompleted re-certifications rest on the rule §7 of the bug report shows\n778\tunsound; and the ridge law's `csearch` constants (§14, §16) are pre-fix.\n779\t**Kill criterion** (median min-of-8 refutation speed-up at n ≥ 60 under\n780\t1.5×): **met**, and by a wide margin — 1.003 over the 804 instances at\n781\tn ≥ 60 and 1.009 over the 389 with identity refutations of at least 10⁴\n782\tnodes. The spread is too small to spend cores on.\n783\t**Size range covered.** Campaign 40–75 (8 instances from each of 171 cells,\n784\t1,368 instances); corpus 50–100 (204 of the 214 certified instances, the\n785\tten `Random-100-100-2/4` excluded); the race at 100 on four instances, one\n786\tof them the only pre/post-fix pair at that size. The projection to 125 is an\n787\textrapolation of a flat curve and rests on counts made under the pre-fix\n788\trule. Nothing at 125 was run.\n789\t**Regenerate.** `python -m learning.relabel_portfolio --workers 16 --deadline 120`\n790\t(5.95 core-hours, 22 min on 16 workers; writes `learning/data/ensemble/portfolio.csv.gz`\n791\tand `reports/portfolio_tables.md`); `python -m learning.relabel_portfolio --stage race\n792\t--race-instances Random-100-100-2-4_0 --no-early-stop --race-deadline 600` then\n793\t`--stage race --race-instances Random-100-100-2-1_0 Random-100-100-2-3_0 Random-100-100-2-5_0\n794\t--race-deadline 600` (10.5 core-hours, 40 min on 16 cores; `portfolio_race.csv`);\n795\t`python -m learning.relabel_portfolio --stage tables`. The pre/post-fix comparison\n796\tjoins `portfolio.csv.gz`'s identity rows to `results.csv`, `results_upward.csv` and\n797\t`scale_nodes.csv` by instance name. Tests: `tests/test_relabel_portfolio.py`.\n798\t**Method notes.** *Nodes, not seconds, once more*: the seconds spread over\n799\tlabellings on a loaded machine (1.49× median) is thirty times the node spread\n800\t(1.05×), and would have passed the kill criterion. *A count is dated by the\n801\tcode that made it*: node counts are only comparable within one version of the\n802\tsearch, and the repository now holds `csearch` counts from three versions\n803\t(before 2026-09-23, between the two fixes, after); `portfolio.csv.gz` carries\n804\tnone of that in its columns and should be read as \"2026-09-26 after commit\n805\t`0eb33915`\". *Parallelise at the call, not the instance*: one job per\n806\t(instance, labelling) kept the longest straggler under four minutes where\n807\tone job per instance would have been an hour. *The first flush should come\n808\tearly*: 500 calls at the heaviest instances was 17 minutes of silence.\n809\t## 19. Predicting cost: how many nodes will a refutation take, before it is run? (plan 2 §2.3, loop0003 item 05)\n810\t*2026-09-26. `learning/cost_model.py`; tables in `reports/cost_model_tables.md`;\n811\tper-instance predictions at the test sizes in\n812\t`learning/data/ensemble/cost_model_predictions.csv`, the recertify table in\n813\t`cost_model_recertify.csv`. Regenerate with `python -m learning.cost_model`\n814\t(11 s). Tests: `tests/test_cost_model.py`.*\n815\t**The question.** `benchmarks.recertify` allocates five-day budgets blind, and\n816\tthe two cost laws on record — §11's per-cell exponential in `n` and §14's\n817\tlinear surface fitted at n ≤ 30 — over-predict by a decade or more at 125\n818\t(§14 (b)). Can `log10 nodes` to refute `optimum − 1` be predicted from\n819\tlabel-free graph features, trained at n ≤ 75, well enough at 100 and 125 to\n820\tbudget with? The plan's kill: fewer than 80% of the 100–125 counts within one\n821\tdecade → not to be used for budgeting.\n822\t**The method.** Target `log10(1 + nodes)`; a call that hit its deadline is a\n823\tright-censored observation (a lower bound, kept, never dropped). Neither\n824\t`lifelines` nor `scikit-survival` is installed, so the censored regression is\n825\ta **Tobit** (Gaussian in log space, censored rows contributing `P(y* ≥ y_obs)`)\n826\twritten by hand with its analytic gradient; it fits 50,000 rows in 0.2 s. The\n827\tdesign is linear and therefore extrapolable: `n`, `log10 col_mean` and its\n828\tsquare, each multiplied by `n`; `optimum / n`, mean degree, degree dispersion,\n829\tlargest-component fraction, `log10 components`, `log10(m / n)`, min-fill\n830\ttreewidth and degeneracy as fractions of `n`, clustering, each alone and times\n831\t`n`; and §16's drift of the rate as `n²` and `n²·log10 col_mean`. On top of\n832\tit, optionally, a LightGBM correction of the Tobit residual on **scale-free**\n833\tfeatures only (`x`, `m / n`, `opt / n`, degree / n, dispersion, degeneracy / n,\n834\ttreewidth / n, component fraction, clustering, `log n`), fitted on the\n835\tuncensored training rows — a tree model on raw features cannot extrapolate\n836\tin `n`, one on scale-free features can carry a density- and structure-shaped\n837\tcorrection upward. Model selection never touches the test sizes: every\n838\tvariant is fitted at n ≤ 60 and scored at 75, the winner is refitted at ≤ 75\n839\tand sent to 100–125 once.\n840\t**Two configurations, dated.** `default` counts are unaffected by the\n841\t2026-09-26 `better_move` fix and are the primary target. `csearch` counts\n842\tchanged with it (§18 (e)), so the `csearch` model trains only on pre-fix\n843\tsources and is tested only against pre-fix counts. That the whole upward run\n844\t(§16) is pre-fix is *established, not assumed*: in every one of the 70 upward\n845\tcells where Theorem 2 is on, §18's post-fix identity count differs from the\n846\trun's count on at least one of the eight instances both studies ran (0 of 70\n847\tcells match on all eight; the other 65 cells have the rule off and are the\n848\tsame under both versions). `date_upward_csearch` records this. §18 (e)'s\n849\t\"71 of 135 cells\" is the count of cells where the distinction matters, not of\n850\tpost-fix cells.\n851\t**Data.** Training, n ≤ 75: the 37,800 campaign instances at 10–40 (§10), the\n852\t6,750 upward instances at 50–75 (§16), the 6,135 corpus instances at ≤ 40 (§3)\n853\tand the 75 corpus `Random` instances at 50–75 (§14): 50,747 `default` counts\n854\t(8 censored) and 50,747 pre-fix `csearch` counts (7 censored). Test, 100 and\n855\t125: every count on record — 33 generated instances at 100 (§16 (d): 25 in\n856\tthe decomposable `d = 2` cell, 8 on the ridge, 7 of them censored at 1,500 s;\n857\tthe two whose value was not optimal are excluded, since a `sat` call is not a\n858\trefutation), the 50 corpus `Random-100-*` instances (4 censored under\n859\t`default`, 3 under `csearch`), and at 125 the 15 dense-class counts\n860\t(`d = 6, 8, 10`, both configurations) plus, for `csearch`, the five recertify\n861\tcounts. **98 `default` counts (11 censored) and 103 `csearch` counts (10\n862\tcensored).** Grouping for the in-range cross-validation is file ∪ isomorphism\n863\tclass (`union_groups`; cell stands for file on the generated instances).\n864\t**The noise floor.** A label-free predictor cannot see which labelling will\n865\trun, so the spread over relabellings of one instance is the error it can never\n866\tremove. Standard deviation of `log10(1 + nodes)` over labellings, refutation\n867\tside, settled calls:\n868\tThe floor is 0.003–0.017 decades where the counts are large. Every model error\n869\tbelow is 10–100× that: **the floor is nowhere near binding**, and the\n870\tunexplained variance is structure the features do not carry, not labels.\n871\t### (a) Model selection at ≤ 60 → 75, and the in-range error\n872\tFit at n ≤ 60, scored on the 2,272 counts at 75 (8 censored), `default`:\n873\t(`csearch`: 0.182 / 0.267 / 0.267 / 0.280 in the same order.) The drift term\n874\tremoves the no-drift model's downward bias at 75 (−0.14 → +0.05), as §16 says\n875\tit should; the residual GBM takes a further 0.08 off the MAE. Refitted at\n876\t≤ 75 and cross-validated five-fold by file ∪ class, the chosen model's\n877\tin-range error is **MAE 0.086 / 0.086 / 0.137 / 0.205 at 10–20 / 21–40 /\n878\t50–60 / 75** (`default`; `csearch` 0.084 / 0.086 / 0.137 / 0.191), bias\n879\twithin ±0.02 in every band, 96.5–100% within a decade, σ of the Tobit 0.164\n880\t(`default`) and 0.179 (`csearch`).\n881\t### (b) The test: 100 and 125, fit at ≤ 75\n882\t(*the cell law has no law for `m = n / 2`: 25 of the 83 counts at 100 get\n883\tnone.) A censored count is a lower bound: the prediction is a hit if it is\n884\tnot more than a decade below it. The decade claim, all 100–125 counts:\n885\tBy class at the test sizes (`default`, chosen model; `csearch` in the tables\n886\tfile): every corpus class at 100 and 125 is 100% within a decade except\n887\t`Random-100-100-2` (4 of 5, the four censored counts at ≥ 9.5–9.7 against a\n888\tprediction of 9.43, which is *under* the lower bound by 0.1–0.3 and could be\n889\tunder by more), and the biases are −0.18 to +0.49 at 100, +0.12 to +0.62 at\n890\t125 (`Random-125-125-6`: median 8.60 observed, 9.20 predicted). **The one\n891\tfailing class is the generated `f_n100_m100_d2` cell**: 25 instances, 96%\n892\tdecomposable (§16 (d)), observed median 10^5.45, predicted 10^6.47, 14 of 25\n893\twithin a decade. Without it the 100-customer band is **57 of 58 (0.983)**\n894\tunder `default` and 58 of 58 under `csearch`. The model learned the campaign's\n895\t`d = 2` cells at 50–75, which decompose less often than the 100-cell (§10:\n896\t50–64% at n ≥ 20), and carries the component count only through\n897\t`log10 components` and the largest-component fraction, which is not enough to\n898\tsay that a 100-customer instance in five pieces refutes in the time of its\n899\tlargest piece. A model that predicts per component and sums would fix that\n900\tclass and is not built here.\n901\tFor the recertify counts at 125 under `csearch` — the two `Random-125-125-2`\n902\tand three `-4` counts, the only ridge counts on record at that size — the\n903\tchosen model's class medians are 11.4 against 11.2 observed (bias +0.16) and\n904\t11.4 against 10.8 (bias +0.53); the linear Tobit alone gives 10.9 / 11.0\n905\t(−0.3 / +0.2). §16 (e)'s drift-corrected extrapolation from the 75-cells gave\n906\t11.18 and 10.62 for the class medians; the per-instance model is no better\n907\tthan that class-level number on those five, and is not worse.\n908\t### (c) The recertify entries: predicted against spent\n909\tThe eight entries `benchmarks.recertify` opened on 2026-09-24 00:07, on the\n910\tpre-fix C, under the `csearch` rule; predicted `log10 nodes` from the\n911\t`csearch` model (pre-fix counts), hours at §16's 0.55 µs per node; the\n912\t`default` model's linear variant beside it as the other configuration's\n913\treference; the §11 law for comparison. Cheapest first by the chosen model:\n914\tThe \"≥ 11.49\" rows are censored observations made here: the three workers have\n915\tbeen running 63.3 hours (checked by PID: three `benchmarks.recertify` workers\n916\tat 100% CPU), and at the finished entries' median rate of 0.74 µs per node\n917\t(0.555–0.784 across the five) they have visited at least 10^11.49 nodes each.\n918\tOn the five finished entries every predictor except §14's surface is within a\n919\tdecade; the chosen `csearch` model has **MAE 0.38 and bias +0.38** (it\n920\tover-predicts: 29 / 31 / 37 / 46 / 93 hours predicted against 28 / 10 / 13 /\n921\t25 / 53 spent at the 0.55 µs rate, with the two `-4` entries that finished in\n922\t10–13 hours the worst, 3× over), Spearman rank correlation with the spent\n923\tnodes **0.4** (linear `csearch` 0.3; the `default` model's linear variant\n924\t**0.8** with MAE 0.13; the §11 law −0.3, since it puts the whole `-2` class\n925\tbelow the `-4` class and recertify found the reverse). **Cheapest-first order\n926\tfor what remains withdrawn: `2-5_0`, then `2-3_0`, then `2-2_0`** — the\n927\tlast predicted the most expensive of all eight by both models and by the §11\n928\tlaw's own ranking within its class. The first two have already run past their\n929\tpredictions (by ≥ 0.34 and ≥ 0.15 decades) and are still inside a decade;\n930\t`2-2_0`'s prediction of 10^11.85 is 107 hours at 0.55 µs and 145 at the\n931\trealised 0.74, against 63 spent, so the model expects it to run until about\n932\t2026-09-30 on the pre-fix rule.\n933\t### (d) Post-fix counts against the pre-fix model (labelled, not the test)\n934\tAgainst §18's post-fix identity counts on the corpus at 99–100: the dense\n935\tclasses, where Theorem 2 is off, are within a decade 5 of 5 each (they are\n936\tthe same counts under both versions); `Random-100-50-2` median 5.78 post-fix\n937\tagainst 5.77 predicted; `Random-100-50-4` 7.59 against 7.40 (3 of 5); and the\n938\trace's four censored `Random-100-100-2` identities at ≥ 9.05 after 600 s\n939\tagainst predictions of 9.07 — consistent, and the true post-fix counts there\n940\tare ≥ 15× the pre-fix ones (§18 (d)), so the model, which has never seen a\n941\tpost-fix count at that size, will under-predict them by an amount that is not\n942\tknown. **A prediction is a prediction for the version of the search that made\n943\tits training counts**; the recertify run is pre-fix, which is why its table\n944\tis the pre-fix model's.\n945\t**Finding, in one paragraph.** A censored linear model in `n` and label-free\n946\tgraph terms, with §16's drift and a scale-free residual correction, trained at\n947\t10–75 customers, puts **88% of the 98 `default` counts and 89% of the 103\n948\tpre-fix `csearch` counts at 100–125 within one decade**, against 58–65% for\n949\t§11's cell law and 59–61% for §14's surface (which both fail at 125 by more\n950\tthan a decade on average); its bias is +0.4 decades (it over-predicts), its\n951\tp90 error one decade at 100 and 0.6 at 125, its in-range error 0.09–0.2 decades\n952\tby five-fold grouped cross-validation, and the relabelling noise floor\n953\t(0.003–0.017) is fifty times below any of it. The one class it misses is the\n954\tgenerated 100-customer `d = 2` cell, 96% decomposable, where it over-predicts\n955\tby a decade because it does not model components; every corpus class at 100\n956\tand 125 is within a decade, including the five day-long recertify counts,\n957\twhich it predicts to +0.38 decades with the right most-expensive entry and a\n958\tweak ordering among the rest. The drift term and the residual correction are\n959\teach worth 0.08–0.1 of MAE at the first size beyond the fit; the linear model\n960\talone fails the kill at 73%. The remaining three recertify entries are, in\n961\tpredicted order, `2-5_0`, `2-3_0`, `2-2_0`; all three have already visited\n962\t≥ 10^11.5 nodes, two of them past their prediction and still within a decade.\n963\t**Size range covered.** Training 10–75 customers (44,550 generated, 6,210\n964\tcorpus). Test 100–125: 50 corpus `Random` instances at 100, 15 at 125 in the\n965\tdense classes (both configurations), 5 recertify counts at 125 (`csearch`,\n966\tpre-fix), 33 generated at 100. **The claim at 125 rests on 15–20 counts, and\n967\ton the ridge at 125 on 5**; 21 of the 201 test counts are lower bounds, and\n968\tthe model sits below 7 of the 11 `default` ones, so its error on the\n969\t100-customer ridge is at least what the table shows and could be more. Nothing\n970\tat 125 is post-fix.\n971\t**Kill criterion (plan 2 §2.3: fewer than 80% of the 100–125 counts within\n972\tone decade → not for budgeting): NOT MET** for the chosen model in either\n973\tconfiguration (87.8% and 89.3%; 87.4% and 88.2% on settled counts alone),\n974\t**MET** for the linear Tobit alone (73.5%, 73.8%) and for both baselines\n975\t(57.5–65.4%). The predictor may be used to *order* a queue and to *size* a\n976\tbudget to within a factor of ten; it is a prediction, biased upward by a\n977\tfactor of about 2.5 on average, never a bound, and never an input to any\n978\tpath that decides `k`.\n979\t**Method notes.** *Censoring by hand is cheap*: the Tobit with its analytic\n980\tgradient converges in under a second on 50,000 rows; a survival library was\n981\tnot needed and none was installed. *Date a count by comparing it with a run\n982\tof known version*: the upward run's version was settled by checking its\n983\tcounts against §18's on the instances both ran, cell by cell — 0 of 70\n984\tTheorem-2-on cells matched, so the whole run predates the fix. *Trees\n985\textrapolate on scale-free features and not on raw ones*: LightGBM on the\n986\tresidual with `n` entering only as `log n` carried the density-shaped\n987\tcorrection to 125; §14's GBM on raw features could not leave its training\n988\trange. *Do not read the linear coefficients one at a time*: the `n²` term's\n989\tsign is positive in both fits while the rate demonstrably falls with `n`\n990\t(§16); the `n`-interaction terms are collinear (`n · tw / n`, `n · opt / n`,\n991\t`n²`) and the drift is carried jointly, which the no-drift variant's −0.14\n992\tbias at 75 shows. *A `sat` at value − 1 is not a refutation count*: two of the\n993\tten 100-customer ridge rows are excluded on that ground (§16 (d)). *`pgrep -f`\n994\tmatched this session's own process* because the item text was in its command\n995\tline; anchor on the interpreter (`^python -m benchmarks.recertify`).\n996\t## 20. Fan order: should equal-cost candidates be tried by remaining degree instead of by index? (plan 2 §2.5(a), loop0003 item 06)\n997\t*2026-09-26. Code: `learning/fan_order.py`; the flag is `fan_order` on\n998\t`satisfiability.customer_search.decide` (Python and C) and on\n999\t`satisfiability.heuristics.restricted_dfs` (registered as `cs-dfs+degree`).\n1000\tRegenerate:*\n1001\t```bash\n1002\tpython -m learning.fan_order --workers 16          # the study: 50,896 instances, ~35 min on 16 cores\n1003\tpython -m learning.fan_order --stage tables        # reports/fan_order_tables.md from the CSV\n1004\tpython -m pytest tests/test_fan_order.py -q\n1005\t```\n1006\t*Writes `learning/data/ensemble/fan_order.csv.gz` (one wide row per instance:\n1007\tstatus, nodes and seconds of every decision call under both configurations\n1008\tand both fan orders, both witness values, both DFS values; committed) and\n1009\t`reports/fan_order_tables.md` (every table below in full). Nothing is written\n1010\tto `solutions/`; no default is changed.*\n1011\t**Question.** Both searches expand candidates cheapest first — the cost of\n1012\tclosing `c` is `|O(S ∪ {c}) − S|` — and break ties by customer index, which is\n1013\tan accident of labelling that §13 and §18 showed moves the node count by up to\n1014\t2.3× (`default`) and 8× (`csearch`) without changing the answer. §7 found the\n1015\tbest one-sentence greedy on record to be *close the customer that opens the\n1016\tfewest new stacks; on ties, the one with the most unclosed neighbours*, and\n1017\tobserved that its first key is the DFS's own fan order. The conjecture (plan 2\n1018\tfinding 6) is that the second key is the tie-break the search should use.\n1019\tDoes it change what a refutation costs? What a witness search costs? What the\n1020\t`cs-dfs` bound returns?\n1021\t**The flag.** `fan_order=\"index\"` is today's order and the default everywhere;\n1022\t`fan_order=\"degree\"` sorts the surviving candidates by `(cost, −|N[c] ∩ R| + 1,\n1023\tc)`, where `R` is the set of customers not yet closed after the node's free\n1024\tmoves — so the tie-break is the remaining degree in the MOSP graph, highest\n1025\tfirst, then index. The C implements it in `dominance_filter`'s final sort\n1026\tbehind a new entry point `cs_decide_fan`; `cs_decide` keeps its signature and\n1027\tits meaning, so a process that loaded the library before the flag existed —\n1028\tthe `benchmarks.recertify` run that has been up since 2026-09-24 forks its\n1029\tworkers late — calls what it always called. The library is now also built to a\n1030\tscratch name and renamed into place, so a running process keeps its inode. A\n1031\tfan order changes which branches are visited *first* and never which are\n1032\tvisited: the cost cut, the memo and every dominance rule are properties of the\n1033\tstate `S`, not of the order the fan is read in. So the *status* of every\n1034\tdecision must be identical under both orders, and that is what the tests and\n1035\tthe study check.\n1036\t**Method.** Every certified campaign instance at 10–75 customers (`results.csv`\n1037\t37,800 at 10–40; `results_upward.csv` 6,750 at 50–75, 12 uncertified rows and\n1038\tthe 35 at n = 100 excluded) and every certified corpus instance at 9–100\n1039\tcustomers (6,349: 6,135 at ≤ 40, 214 at 41–100, the 125 × 125 classes out of\n1040\tscope). For each: `decide(optimum − 1)` (the refutation) and `decide(optimum)`\n1041\t(the witness search) under both configurations (`default`; `csearch` =\n1042\tTheorem 2 on where `sparse_enough_for_better_move`, every candidate a\n1043\tdominator) and both fan orders — eight decision calls, item 01's protocol —\n1044\twith the `sat` side's closing order simulated (`witness_ok`), a 60 s deadline\n1045\tper call on the campaign and 120 s on the corpus above 40; and `restricted_dfs`\n1046\tunder both fan orders with its default MCN seed and 200,000-node budget. The\n1047\tdefault arm is compared node for node with the counts recorded before the flag\n1048\texisted (`nodes_default` in `results.csv` / `results_upward.csv`, §3's\n1049\t`node_counts.csv`, §14's `scale_nodes.csv`) and its `cs-dfs` value with the\n1050\trecorded `ub_cs_dfs`: a byte-for-byte check that the default path is unchanged,\n1051\tover every instance in the study rather than over a test's sample. The paired\n1052\tstatistic is `(nodes_degree + 1) / (nodes_index + 1)` per instance; a call\n1053\tcensored at its deadline is a lower bound and its pair is counted but not\n1054\tpooled into the ratio statistics. Every `csearch` count here is a **post-fix**\n1055\tcount (the C `better_move` fix of 2026-09-26, §18 (e)), on both arms.\n1056\t**Baseline.** The index fan order — what every recorded node count and every\n1057\t`cs-dfs` value in this repository was made with.\n1058\t**Kill (plan 2 §2.5a).** If the paired median node change is within ±5% at\n1059\tevery size, the fan order does not matter and the conjecture is closed.\n1060\t### The run\n1061\t50,896 instances; 407,168 decision calls and 101,792 DFS runs; 9.25\n1062\tcore-hours, 35 min on 16 workers (`learning/data/ensemble/fan_order_run.log`).\n1063\tThe audit came out clean on every line:\n1064\tSo the default path is byte-for-byte what it was, over every instance in the\n1065\tstudy and not only over a test's sample, and the flag never changed an\n1066\tanswer in 407,168 calls.\n1067\t### (a) The refutation: the order matters more and more often, and cancels\n1068\tPaired ratio `(nodes_degree + 1) / (nodes_index + 1)` at `optimum − 1`,\n1069\t`default` configuration (`csearch` is the same table to the third decimal;\n1070\t`reports/fan_order_tables.md` has both, and the witness side):\n1071\tThe median is 1.000 in every band under both configurations, and so is the\n1072\tgeometric mean; the p10–p90 band never leaves [0.992, 1.008]; the widest\n1073\tratios at `n ≥ 50` are 0.664 and 1.121; at `n = 75` the absolute log10\n1074\tratio has median 0.0003, p90 0.0058, p99 0.0225. One instance per\n1075\tisomorphism class (32,745 classes) gives medians of 1.000 at every `n`. By\n1076\trealised density at 50–75 and by cell (`reports/fan_order_tables.md`), every\n1077\tcell's median lies in [0.994, 1.015], and the sign of the cell medians is not\n1078\teven consistent between configurations (`f_n75_m75_d4` is the cell where\n1079\t`degree` saves most under both, 0.996–0.999; the `m = n/2` fixed-`d` cells\n1080\tlean the other way at 1.01). Total nodes at 75: 8.71 G index against 8.54 G\n1081\tdegree; at the corpus 99–100 band 3.00 G against 3.04 G. Seconds beside them:\n1082\t4,421 s against 4,434 s at 75, so the degree sort costs nothing per node in\n1083\tthe C either.\n1084\tWhat *does* change with size is how often the order matters at all: the\n1085\tfraction of refutations whose node count differs between the two orders is\n1086\t0.1% at `n = 10`, 5.7% at 20, 23.9% at 30, 36.6% at 40, 61.4% at 50, 68.4%\n1087\tat 60 and 74.4% at 75. The order reaches the count on three refutations in\n1088\tfour at 75 and moves it by a fraction of a percent, in either direction. The\n1089\tmechanism is the one the docstrings state: the cost cut, the memo and the\n1090\tdominance rules are properties of the state `S`, so a refutation visits the\n1091\tsame set of states whichever order the fan is read in, and the order changes\n1092\tonly how the memo and the old-move set `Q(S)` happen to fill — which is why\n1093\tthe count changes at all, and why it cancels.\n1094\tThe censored pairs say the same thing at the deadline: on the 44 (`default`)\n1095\tand 40 (`csearch`) instances where both arms ran out, the degree arm had\n1096\tvisited 0.99× the index arm's nodes at the same wall clock (median), and on\n1097\tthe one instance per configuration where only the degree arm finished\n1098\t(`ens_f_n75_m150_d2_i010`: 134 M nodes `unsat` against ≥ 144 M unfinished;\n1099\t`_i045` under `csearch`: 76 M against ≥ 85 M) the two counts are within 7%\n1100\tof each other — a deadline artefact, not a speed-up.\n1101\t**Kill criterion (plan 2 §2.5a): met, for both configurations.** The paired\n1102\tmedian node change is 0.0% at every size from 10 to 100 customers; the fan\n1103\torder does not matter for refutations and the conjecture, as a statement about\n1104\tthe complete search's cost, is closed.\n1105\t### (b) The witness search: the same median, a different distribution\n1106\t`decide(optimum)` under both orders, `default` configuration (`csearch`\n1107\twithin 0.03 of every entry):\n1108\tThe median is still 1.000 — most witness searches take a handful of nodes and\n1109\tthe first leaf is the witness under either order — but the distribution is\n1110\tskewed left and the skew grows with `n`: the geometric mean falls from 0.998\n1111\tat 10 to 0.945 at 40, 0.859 at 60 and 0.813 at 75, the total node count of\n1112\tthe band falls to 0.65 at 75 and 0.87 at 99–100, and the p10 reaches 0.35.\n1113\tWhere the witness search is *hard* (index arm ≥ 10⁴ nodes) the effect is\n1114\tlarge and still two-sided:\n1115\tBy cell the gain sits on the ridge and its sparse side: `f_n60_m120_d2`\n1116\t(total 0.43), `f_n75_m75_d2` (0.45), `f_n75_m150_d2` (0.51), `f_n75_m75_d3`\n1117\t(0.62), `b_n75_m75_p0.025` (0.62); the dense cells are neutral to slightly\n1118\tworse (`f_n50_m25_d9` 1.05, `b_n50_m25_p0.05` 1.15). This is §7's finding in\n1119\tthe search's own terms: the degree tie-break is the two-key rule's second\n1120\tkey, and under it the first leaf of the fan is the rule's greedy whenever the\n1121\tcost cut lets it through, so the witness is found earlier on the instances\n1122\twhere the rule beats MCN — the sparse ones. It is also §18 (b)'s finding —\n1123\tlabels move the satisfiable side, not the refutation — with the label noise\n1124\treplaced by a rule. The p90 above 1 on every band is the price: a third of\n1125\tthe hard searches get slower, some by 10×, because a greedy that is right on\n1126\taverage is wrong on a third of the instances and the DFS then has to back out\n1127\tof its first leaf.\n1128\tWhat it is worth in a descent is bounded by §18 (b): at `n = 75` the witness\n1129\tsearch is 204 nodes against 26,200 for the refutation (medians), 456 M\n1130\tagainst 8.7 G in total, so a 35% saving on the witness side is 1.7% of the\n1131\tband's node count. Where it would count is a driver that asks only\n1132\tsatisfiable questions — `benchmarks.ratchet`, or the `k ≥ optimum` calls at\n1133\tthe top of a descent, whose seconds are what `on_improve` exists to save.\n1134\t### (c) `restricted_dfs`: a smaller overshoot, a slower Python\n1135\tThe `cs-dfs` bound with its default MCN seed and 200,000-node budget, value\n1136\tover the certified optimum:\n1137\tThe total overshoot falls from 24,178 to 23,860 stacks (−1.3%); the gain is\n1138\twhere the DFS is weakest — `corpus 99–100` 3.21 → 2.95, worst 10 → 8 — and it\n1139\thas the same sign structure as the witness search: sparse cells better\n1140\t(`f_n50_m25_d4` 17 better / 2 worse), dense `m = n/2` cells worse\n1141\t(`b_n60_m30_p0.2` 2 / 11). The extra 50% of wall clock is the Python sort\n1142\tkey, a lambda called once per candidate per node, not the search; a\n1143\t`(cost, -degree, index)` tuple built in the scoring loop would remove it.\n1144\tRead against §7, where seeding the DFS with the rule's own greedy order took\n1145\tthe held-out MAE from 0.311 to 0.127: changing the fan order under the MCN\n1146\tseed recovers almost none of that. The gain of `cs-dfs+rule` came from the\n1147\t*incumbent* the DFS prunes against, not from the order it reads the fan in;\n1148\twith the MCN incumbent, the cost cut removes the rule's leaf before the DFS\n1149\treaches it on most of the instances where the rule would have won.\n1150\t### Proposed defaults — stated, not applied\n1151\t- **`decide`**: keep `fan_order=\"index\"`. The refutation is indifferent to the\n1152\t  order at every size measured (median change 0.0%, p10–p90 within ±0.8%),\n1153\t  and the refutation is 95–98% of a descent's cost. Nothing to gain, and\n1154\t  every recorded node count in the repository stays comparable.\n1155\t- **Satisfiable-side drivers** (`benchmarks.ratchet`, the `k ≥ optimum`\n1156\t  calls of a descent, a portfolio on the witness side as §18 (f) proposed):\n1157\t  `fan_order=\"degree\"` saves 35% of the witness-search nodes at 75 and 13%\n1158\t  at 100 in total, at a p90 cost of 1.1–1.3× per instance. Worth enabling\n1159\t  there and nowhere else; the owner's change.\n1160\t- **`restricted_dfs`**: `cs-dfs+degree` is registered and marginally better\n1161\t  (+0.1 pt exact, −1.3% total overshoot, better on the sparse large\n1162\t  instances where the bound is worst) and 50% slower in the current Python.\n1163\t  Not worth changing the default for; if a stronger `cs-dfs` is wanted, §7's\n1164\t  rule as the *seed* is the measured route (0.127 vs 0.311).\n1165\t**Size range.** Campaign 10–75 customers (44,547 instances, both generators,\n1166\t`m ∈ {n/2, n, 2n}`), corpus 9–100 (6,349). Nothing here is evidence about\n1167\t125 × 125 except that the refutation ratio's spread *narrows* with `n`\n1168\t(p90 1.004 at 40, 1.008 at 75–100) while the fraction of affected\n1169\trefutations rises; an extrapolation would put the 125 × 125 ratio at 1.00 ±\n1170\t0.01, and the 11 censored corpus pairs at 100 show 0.99 at the deadline.\n1171\t**Method notes.** The C keeps `cs_decide` with its old signature and adds\n1172\t`cs_decide_fan`; a long-running process that forks workers late (the\n1173\t`benchmarks.recertify` run, up since 2026-09-24, nine processes before and\n1174\tafter) would otherwise have loaded a library whose entry point took one more\n1175\targument than its in-memory `argtypes` said. `native._build` now compiles to a\n1176\tscratch name and renames into place for the same reason. The Python omits\n1177\tcustomers with no products from its witness and the C lists them as free\n1178\tmoves — a pre-existing difference in witness *format* that a naive C-vs-Python\n1179\torder comparison trips on; compare on the active customers. And a recorded\n1180\tcount is only as exact as the CSV that stored it: `results_upward.csv` wrote\n1181\tsix significant digits.\n1182\t## 21. Extremal search and exact treewidth: what are the worst small instances, and is pathwidth − treewidth bounded? (plan 2 §2.7, loop0003 item 08)\n1183\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"85602c8d-df04-47a8-a103-12762ff7e492","uuid":"9701683d-148e-4a7e-93ba-691527077a3b","timestamp":"2026-09-26T19:23:35.806Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/85602c8d-df04-47a8-a103-12762ff7e492/tool-results/bgfzanggn.txt","content":"cells, `results_upward.csv` (§16, whose run straddled the fix) — was made\nbefore the 2026-09-26 fix of the C `better_move` (`reports/better_move_bug.md`\n§7), which measured its cost at n ≤ 40 as +7.3% in total, p90 1.18. This\nstudy's identity rows are post-fix counts for 1,572 of those instances, and\nthe ratio (after + 1)/(before + 1) on the identity labelling, where Theorem 2\nis on, is:\nDense classes, where Theorem 2 is off, are unchanged to the node (all 40\n`Random-*-*-6/8/10` instances, the `default` configuration everywhere). The\nover-pruning the fix removed grew with size on the sparse classes, which is\nwhere the day-long refutations are; the 125 × 125 recertify counts (1.6 ×\n10¹¹ nodes, 25–28 h) are pre-fix numbers and the post-fix cost at that size\nis not known. Every §14 and §16 rate for `csearch` is a pre-fix rate.\n### (f) Projection, recommendation, verdict\n**The projected wall clock for the 125 × 125 ridge on 16 cores with a\nrelabelling portfolio is the identity's wall clock.** The refutation speed-up\nof min-of-16 is 1.00–1.01 at the median at every size from 40 to 100 and is\nnot growing; the recertify counts say 25–28 h per `Random-125-125-2` instance\nand 10–53 h per `-4` under the pre-fix rule, and §16's revised predictions\n(1.5 × 10¹¹ and 4 × 10¹⁰ nodes) say 23 h and 6 h at 0.55 µs per node; a\nportfolio would return the same numbers on sixteen cores instead of one.\nUnder the fixed rule each is larger by a factor this item measured as ≥ 15 on\nthe one ridge instance at n = 100 where both sides exist, and did not measure\nat 125.\n**Recommendation for `benchmarks.recertify`, stated and not applied: do not\nadd a relabelling portfolio.** One worker per open entry, as it runs today,\nis the right use of the cores — sixteen entries side by side is a sixteen-fold\nthroughput, sixteen labellings of one entry is a one-fold one. What the\nportfolio *would* buy is the satisfiable side: where the witness search at\nthe optimum is hard (identity ≥ 10⁴ nodes, 19% of campaign instances at\nn = 75) min-of-16 saves 8–13× at the median at a core efficiency of 0.5–0.8,\nand the spread there grows with `n`. The consumers of satisfiable calls are\n`benchmarks.ratchet`, `heuristics.restricted_dfs`'s seeded searches and the\n`k ≥ optimum` calls of a descent; a portfolio on those costs little because\nthose calls are short, and it is a driver change for the owner, not made here.\nTwo alerts from the race, outside this item: the recertify run started\n2026-09-24 has three entries still open on the pre-fix C, and its five\ncompleted re-certifications rest on the rule §7 of the bug report shows\nunsound; and the ridge law's `csearch` constants (§14, §16) are pre-fix.\n**Kill criterion** (median min-of-8 refutation speed-up at n ≥ 60 under\n1.5×): **met**, and by a wide margin — 1.003 over the 804 instances at\nn ≥ 60 and 1.009 over the 389 with identity refutations of at least 10⁴\nnodes. The spread is too small to spend cores on.\n**Size range covered.** Campaign 40–75 (8 instances from each of 171 cells,\n1,368 instances); corpus 50–100 (204 of the 214 certified instances, the\nten `Random-100-100-2/4` excluded); the race at 100 on four instances, one\nof them the only pre/post-fix pair at that size. The projection to 125 is an\nextrapolation of a flat curve and rests on counts made under the pre-fix\nrule. Nothing at 125 was run.\n**Regenerate.** `python -m learning.relabel_portfolio --workers 16 --deadline 120`\n(5.95 core-hours, 22 min on 16 workers; writes `learning/data/ensemble/portfolio.csv.gz`\nand `reports/portfolio_tables.md`); `python -m learning.relabel_portfolio --stage race\n--race-instances Random-100-100-2-4_0 --no-early-stop --race-deadline 600` then\n`--stage race --race-instances Random-100-100-2-1_0 Random-100-100-2-3_0 Random-100-100-2-5_0\n--race-deadline 600` (10.5 core-hours, 40 min on 16 cores; `portfolio_race.csv`);\n`python -m learning.relabel_portfolio --stage tables`. The pre/post-fix comparison\njoins `portfolio.csv.gz`'s identity rows to `results.csv`, `results_upward.csv` and\n`scale_nodes.csv` by instance name. Tests: `tests/test_relabel_portfolio.py`.\n**Method notes.** *Nodes, not seconds, once more*: the seconds spread over\nlabellings on a loaded machine (1.49× median) is thirty times the node spread\n(1.05×), and would have passed the kill criterion. *A count is dated by the\ncode that made it*: node counts are only comparable within one version of the\nsearch, and the repository now holds `csearch` counts from three versions\n(before 2026-09-23, between the two fixes, after); `portfolio.csv.gz` carries\nnone of that in its columns and should be read as \"2026-09-26 after commit\n`0eb33915`\". *Parallelise at the call, not the instance*: one job per\n(instance, labelling) kept the longest straggler under four minutes where\none job per instance would have been an hour. *The first flush should come\nearly*: 500 calls at the heaviest instances was 17 minutes of silence.\n## 19. Predicting cost: how many nodes will a refutation take, before it is run? (plan 2 §2.3, loop0003 item 05)\n*2026-09-26. `learning/cost_model.py`; tables in `reports/cost_model_tables.md`;\nper-instance predictions at the test sizes in\n`learning/data/ensemble/cost_model_predictions.csv`, the recertify table in\n`cost_model_recertify.csv`. Regenerate with `python -m learning.cost_model`\n(11 s). Tests: `tests/test_cost_model.py`.*\n**The question.** `benchmarks.recertify` allocates five-day budgets blind, and\nthe two cost laws on record — §11's per-cell exponential in `n` and §14's\nlinear surface fitted at n ≤ 30 — over-predict by a decade or more at 125\n(§14 (b)). Can `log10 nodes` to refute `optimum − 1` be predicted from\nlabel-free graph features, trained at n ≤ 75, well enough at 100 and 125 to\nbudget with? The plan's kill: fewer than 80% of the 100–125 counts within one\ndecade → not to be used for budgeting.\n**The method.** Target `log10(1 + nodes)`; a call that hit its deadline is a\nright-censored observation (a lower bound, kept, never dropped). Neither\n`lifelines` nor `scikit-survival` is installed, so the censored regression is\na **Tobit** (Gaussian in log space, censored rows contributing `P(y* ≥ y_obs)`)\nwritten by hand with its analytic gradient; it fits 50,000 rows in 0.2 s. The\ndesign is linear and therefore extrapolable: `n`, `log10 col_mean` and its\nsquare, each multiplied by `n`; `optimum / n`, mean degree, degree dispersion,\nlargest-component fraction, `log10 components`, `log10(m / n)`, min-fill\ntreewidth and degeneracy as fractions of `n`, clustering, each alone and times\n`n`; and §16's drift of the rate as `n²` and `n²·log10 col_mean`. On top of\nit, optionally, a LightGBM correction of the Tobit residual on **scale-free**\nfeatures only (`x`, `m / n`, `opt / n`, degree / n, dispersion, degeneracy / n,\ntreewidth / n, component fraction, clustering, `log n`), fitted on the\nuncensored training rows — a tree model on raw features cannot extrapolate\nin `n`, one on scale-free features can carry a density- and structure-shaped\ncorrection upward. Model selection never touches the test sizes: every\nvariant is fitted at n ≤ 60 and scored at 75, the winner is refitted at ≤ 75\nand sent to 100–125 once.\n**Two configurations, dated.** `default` counts are unaffected by the\n2026-09-26 `better_move` fix and are the primary target. `csearch` counts\nchanged with it (§18 (e)), so the `csearch` model trains only on pre-fix\nsources and is tested only against pre-fix counts. That the whole upward run\n(§16) is pre-fix is *established, not assumed*: in every one of the 70 upward\ncells where Theorem 2 is on, §18's post-fix identity count differs from the\nrun's count on at least one of the eight instances both studies ran (0 of 70\ncells match on all eight; the other 65 cells have the rule off and are the\nsame under both versions). `date_upward_csearch` records this. §18 (e)'s\n\"71 of 135 cells\" is the count of cells where the distinction matters, not of\npost-fix cells.\n**Data.** Training, n ≤ 75: the 37,800 campaign instances at 10–40 (§10), the\n6,750 upward instances at 50–75 (§16), the 6,135 corpus instances at ≤ 40 (§3)\nand the 75 corpus `Random` instances at 50–75 (§14): 50,747 `default` counts\n(8 censored) and 50,747 pre-fix `csearch` counts (7 censored). Test, 100 and\n125: every count on record — 33 generated instances at 100 (§16 (d): 25 in\nthe decomposable `d = 2` cell, 8 on the ridge, 7 of them censored at 1,500 s;\nthe two whose value was not optimal are excluded, since a `sat` call is not a\nrefutation), the 50 corpus `Random-100-*` instances (4 censored under\n`default`, 3 under `csearch`), and at 125 the 15 dense-class counts\n(`d = 6, 8, 10`, both configurations) plus, for `csearch`, the five recertify\ncounts. **98 `default` counts (11 censored) and 103 `csearch` counts (10\ncensored).** Grouping for the in-range cross-validation is file ∪ isomorphism\nclass (`union_groups`; cell stands for file on the generated instances).\n**The noise floor.** A label-free predictor cannot see which labelling will\nrun, so the spread over relabellings of one instance is the error it can never\nremove. Standard deviation of `log10(1 + nodes)` over labellings, refutation\nside, settled calls:\nThe floor is 0.003–0.017 decades where the counts are large. Every model error\nbelow is 10–100× that: **the floor is nowhere near binding**, and the\nunexplained variance is structure the features do not carry, not labels.\n### (a) Model selection at ≤ 60 → 75, and the in-range error\nFit at n ≤ 60, scored on the 2,272 counts at 75 (8 censored), `default`:\n(`csearch`: 0.182 / 0.267 / 0.267 / 0.280 in the same order.) The drift term\nremoves the no-drift model's downward bias at 75 (−0.14 → +0.05), as §16 says\nit should; the residual GBM takes a further 0.08 off the MAE. Refitted at\n≤ 75 and cross-validated five-fold by file ∪ class, the chosen model's\nin-range error is **MAE 0.086 / 0.086 / 0.137 / 0.205 at 10–20 / 21–40 /\n50–60 / 75** (`default`; `csearch` 0.084 / 0.086 / 0.137 / 0.191), bias\nwithin ±0.02 in every band, 96.5–100% within a decade, σ of the Tobit 0.164\n(`default`) and 0.179 (`csearch`).\n### (b) The test: 100 and 125, fit at ≤ 75\n(*the cell law has no law for `m = n / 2`: 25 of the 83 counts at 100 get\nnone.) A censored count is a lower bound: the prediction is a hit if it is\nnot more than a decade below it. The decade claim, all 100–125 counts:\nBy class at the test sizes (`default`, chosen model; `csearch` in the tables\nfile): every corpus class at 100 and 125 is 100% within a decade except\n`Random-100-100-2` (4 of 5, the four censored counts at ≥ 9.5–9.7 against a\nprediction of 9.43, which is *under* the lower bound by 0.1–0.3 and could be\nunder by more), and the biases are −0.18 to +0.49 at 100, +0.12 to +0.62 at\n125 (`Random-125-125-6`: median 8.60 observed, 9.20 predicted). **The one\nfailing class is the generated `f_n100_m100_d2` cell**: 25 instances, 96%\ndecomposable (§16 (d)), observed median 10^5.45, predicted 10^6.47, 14 of 25\nwithin a decade. Without it the 100-customer band is **57 of 58 (0.983)**\nunder `default` and 58 of 58 under `csearch`. The model learned the campaign's\n`d = 2` cells at 50–75, which decompose less often than the 100-cell (§10:\n50–64% at n ≥ 20), and carries the component count only through\n`log10 components` and the largest-component fraction, which is not enough to\nsay that a 100-customer instance in five pieces refutes in the time of its\nlargest piece. A model that predicts per component and sums would fix that\nclass and is not built here.\nFor the recertify counts at 125 under `csearch` — the two `Random-125-125-2`\nand three `-4` counts, the only ridge counts on record at that size — the\nchosen model's class medians are 11.4 against 11.2 observed (bias +0.16) and\n11.4 against 10.8 (bias +0.53); the linear Tobit alone gives 10.9 / 11.0\n(−0.3 / +0.2). §16 (e)'s drift-corrected extrapolation from the 75-cells gave\n11.18 and 10.62 for the class medians; the per-instance model is no better\nthan that class-level number on those five, and is not worse.\n### (c) The recertify entries: predicted against spent\nThe eight entries `benchmarks.recertify` opened on 2026-09-24 00:07, on the\npre-fix C, under the `csearch` rule; predicted `log10 nodes` from the\n`csearch` model (pre-fix counts), hours at §16's 0.55 µs per node; the\n`default` model's linear variant beside it as the other configuration's\nreference; the §11 law for comparison. Cheapest first by the chosen model:\nThe \"≥ 11.49\" rows are censored observations made here: the three workers have\nbeen running 63.3 hours (checked by PID: three `benchmarks.recertify` workers\nat 100% CPU), and at the finished entries' median rate of 0.74 µs per node\n(0.555–0.784 across the five) they have visited at least 10^11.49 nodes each.\nOn the five finished entries every predictor except §14's surface is within a\ndecade; the chosen `csearch` model has **MAE 0.38 and bias +0.38** (it\nover-predicts: 29 / 31 / 37 / 46 / 93 hours predicted against 28 / 10 / 13 /\n25 / 53 spent at the 0.55 µs rate, with the two `-4` entries that finished in\n10–13 hours the worst, 3× over), Spearman rank correlation with the spent\nnodes **0.4** (linear `csearch` 0.3; the `default` model's linear variant\n**0.8** with MAE 0.13; the §11 law −0.3, since it puts the whole `-2` class\nbelow the `-4` class and recertify found the reverse). **Cheapest-first order\nfor what remains withdrawn: `2-5_0`, then `2-3_0`, then `2-2_0`** — the\nlast predicted the most expensive of all eight by both models and by the §11\nlaw's own ranking within its class. The first two have already run past their\npredictions (by ≥ 0.34 and ≥ 0.15 decades) and are still inside a decade;\n`2-2_0`'s prediction of 10^11.85 is 107 hours at 0.55 µs and 145 at the\nrealised 0.74, against 63 spent, so the model expects it to run until about\n2026-09-30 on the pre-fix rule.\n### (d) Post-fix counts against the pre-fix model (labelled, not the test)\nAgainst §18's post-fix identity counts on the corpus at 99–100: the dense\nclasses, where Theorem 2 is off, are within a decade 5 of 5 each (they are\nthe same counts under both versions); `Random-100-50-2` median 5.78 post-fix\nagainst 5.77 predicted; `Random-100-50-4` 7.59 against 7.40 (3 of 5); and the\nrace's four censored `Random-100-100-2` identities at ≥ 9.05 after 600 s\nagainst predictions of 9.07 — consistent, and the true post-fix counts there\nare ≥ 15× the pre-fix ones (§18 (d)), so the model, which has never seen a\npost-fix count at that size, will under-predict them by an amount that is not\nknown. **A prediction is a prediction for the version of the search that made\nits training counts**; the recertify run is pre-fix, which is why its table\nis the pre-fix model's.\n**Finding, in one paragraph.** A censored linear model in `n` and label-free\ngraph terms, with §16's drift and a scale-free residual correction, trained at\n10–75 customers, puts **88% of the 98 `default` counts and 89% of the 103\npre-fix `csearch` counts at 100–125 within one decade**, against 58–65% for\n§11's cell law and 59–61% for §14's surface (which both fail at 125 by more\nthan a decade on average); its bias is +0.4 decades (it over-predicts), its\np90 error one decade at 100 and 0.6 at 125, its in-range error 0.09–0.2 decades\nby five-fold grouped cross-validation, and the relabelling noise floor\n(0.003–0.017) is fifty times below any of it. The one class it misses is the\ngenerated 100-customer `d = 2` cell, 96% decomposable, where it over-predicts\nby a decade because it does not model components; every corpus class at 100\nand 125 is within a decade, including the five day-long recertify counts,\nwhich it predicts to +0.38 decades with the right most-expensive entry and a\nweak ordering among the rest. The drift term and the residual correction are\neach worth 0.08–0.1 of MAE at the first size beyond the fit; the linear model\nalone fails the kill at 73%. The remaining three recertify entries are, in\npredicted order, `2-5_0`, `2-3_0`, `2-2_0`; all three have already visited\n≥ 10^11.5 nodes, two of them past their prediction and still within a decade.\n**Size range covered.** Training 10–75 customers (44,550 generated, 6,210\ncorpus). Test 100–125: 50 corpus `Random` instances at 100, 15 at 125 in the\ndense classes (both configurations), 5 recertify counts at 125 (`csearch`,\npre-fix), 33 generated at 100. **The claim at 125 rests on 15–20 counts, and\non the ridge at 125 on 5**; 21 of the 201 test counts are lower bounds, and\nthe model sits below 7 of the 11 `default` ones, so its error on the\n100-customer ridge is at least what the table shows and could be more. Nothing\nat 125 is post-fix.\n**Kill criterion (plan 2 §2.3: fewer than 80% of the 100–125 counts within\none decade → not for budgeting): NOT MET** for the chosen model in either\nconfiguration (87.8% and 89.3%; 87.4% and 88.2% on settled counts alone),\n**MET** for the linear Tobit alone (73.5%, 73.8%) and for both baselines\n(57.5–65.4%). The predictor may be used to *order* a queue and to *size* a\nbudget to within a factor of ten; it is a prediction, biased upward by a\nfactor of about 2.5 on average, never a bound, and never an input to any\npath that decides `k`.\n**Method notes.** *Censoring by hand is cheap*: the Tobit with its analytic\ngradient converges in under a second on 50,000 rows; a survival library was\nnot needed and none was installed. *Date a count by comparing it with a run\nof known version*: the upward run's version was settled by checking its\ncounts against §18's on the instances both ran, cell by cell — 0 of 70\nTheorem-2-on cells matched, so the whole run predates the fix. *Trees\nextrapolate on scale-free features and not on raw ones*: LightGBM on the\nresidual with `n` entering only as `log n` carried the density-shaped\ncorrection to 125; §14's GBM on raw features could not leave its training\nrange. *Do not read the linear coefficients one at a time*: the `n²` term's\nsign is positive in both fits while the rate demonstrably falls with `n`\n(§16); the `n`-interaction terms are collinear (`n · tw / n`, `n · opt / n`,\n`n²`) and the drift is carried jointly, which the no-drift variant's −0.14\nbias at 75 shows. *A `sat` at value − 1 is not a refutation count*: two of the\nten 100-customer ridge rows are excluded on that ground (§16 (d)). *`pgrep -f`\nmatched this session's own process* because the item text was in its command\nline; anchor on the interpreter (`^python -m benchmarks.recertify`).\n## 20. Fan order: should equal-cost candidates be tried by remaining degree instead of by index? (plan 2 §2.5(a), loop0003 item 06)\n*2026-09-26. Code: `learning/fan_order.py`; the flag is `fan_order` on\n`satisfiability.customer_search.decide` (Python and C) and on\n`satisfiability.heuristics.restricted_dfs` (registered as `cs-dfs+degree`).\nRegenerate:*\n```bash\npython -m learning.fan_order --workers 16          # the study: 50,896 instances, ~35 min on 16 cores\npython -m learning.fan_order --stage tables        # reports/fan_order_tables.md from the CSV\npython -m pytest tests/test_fan_order.py -q\n```\n*Writes `learning/data/ensemble/fan_order.csv.gz` (one wide row per instance:\nstatus, nodes and seconds of every decision call under both configurations\nand both fan orders, both witness values, both DFS values; committed) and\n`reports/fan_order_tables.md` (every table below in full). Nothing is written\nto `solutions/`; no default is changed.*\n**Question.** Both searches expand candidates cheapest first — the cost of\nclosing `c` is `|O(S ∪ {c}) − S|` — and break ties by customer index, which is\nan accident of labelling that §13 and §18 showed moves the node count by up to\n2.3× (`default`) and 8× (`csearch`) without changing the answer. §7 found the\nbest one-sentence greedy on record to be *close the customer that opens the\nfewest new stacks; on ties, the one with the most unclosed neighbours*, and\nobserved that its first key is the DFS's own fan order. The conjecture (plan 2\nfinding 6) is that the second key is the tie-break the search should use.\nDoes it change what a refutation costs? What a witness search costs? What the\n`cs-dfs` bound returns?\n**The flag.** `fan_order=\"index\"` is today's order and the default everywhere;\n`fan_order=\"degree\"` sorts the surviving candidates by `(cost, −|N[c] ∩ R| + 1,\nc)`, where `R` is the set of customers not yet closed after the node's free\nmoves — so the tie-break is the remaining degree in the MOSP graph, highest\nfirst, then index. The C implements it in `dominance_filter`'s final sort\nbehind a new entry point `cs_decide_fan`; `cs_decide` keeps its signature and\nits meaning, so a process that loaded the library before the flag existed —\nthe `benchmarks.recertify` run that has been up since 2026-09-24 forks its\nworkers late — calls what it always called. The library is now also built to a\nscratch name and renamed into place, so a running process keeps its inode. A\nfan order changes which branches are visited *first* and never which are\nvisited: the cost cut, the memo and every dominance rule are properties of the\nstate `S`, not of the order the fan is read in. So the *status* of every\ndecision must be identical under both orders, and that is what the tests and\nthe study check.\n**Method.** Every certified campaign instance at 10–75 customers (`results.csv`\n37,800 at 10–40; `results_upward.csv` 6,750 at 50–75, 12 uncertified rows and\nthe 35 at n = 100 excluded) and every certified corpus instance at 9–100\ncustomers (6,349: 6,135 at ≤ 40, 214 at 41–100, the 125 × 125 classes out of\nscope). For each: `decide(optimum − 1)` (the refutation) and `decide(optimum)`\n(the witness search) under both configurations (`default`; `csearch` =\nTheorem 2 on where `sparse_enough_for_better_move`, every candidate a\ndominator) and both fan orders — eight decision calls, item 01's protocol —\nwith the `sat` side's closing order simulated (`witness_ok`), a 60 s deadline\nper call on the campaign and 120 s on the corpus above 40; and `restricted_dfs`\nunder both fan orders with its default MCN seed and 200,000-node budget. The\ndefault arm is compared node for node with the counts recorded before the flag\nexisted (`nodes_default` in `results.csv` / `results_upward.csv`, §3's\n`node_counts.csv`, §14's `scale_nodes.csv`) and its `cs-dfs` value with the\nrecorded `ub_cs_dfs`: a byte-for-byte check that the default path is unchanged,\nover every instance in the study rather than over a test's sample. The paired\nstatistic is `(nodes_degree + 1) / (nodes_index + 1)` per instance; a call\ncensored at its deadline is a lower bound and its pair is counted but not\npooled into the ratio statistics. Every `csearch` count here is a **post-fix**\ncount (the C `better_move` fix of 2026-09-26, §18 (e)), on both arms.\n**Baseline.** The index fan order — what every recorded node count and every\n`cs-dfs` value in this repository was made with.\n**Kill (plan 2 §2.5a).** If the paired median node change is within ±5% at\nevery size, the fan order does not matter and the conjecture is closed.\n### The run\n50,896 instances; 407,168 decision calls and 101,792 DFS runs; 9.25\ncore-hours, 35 min on 16 workers (`learning/data/ensemble/fan_order_run.log`).\nThe audit came out clean on every line:\nSo the default path is byte-for-byte what it was, over every instance in the\nstudy and not only over a test's sample, and the flag never changed an\nanswer in 407,168 calls.\n### (a) The refutation: the order matters more and more often, and cancels\nPaired ratio `(nodes_degree + 1) / (nodes_index + 1)` at `optimum − 1`,\n`default` configuration (`csearch` is the same table to the third decimal;\n`reports/fan_order_tables.md` has both, and the witness side):\nThe median is 1.000 in every band under both configurations, and so is the\ngeometric mean; the p10–p90 band never leaves [0.992, 1.008]; the widest\nratios at `n ≥ 50` are 0.664 and 1.121; at `n = 75` the absolute log10\nratio has median 0.0003, p90 0.0058, p99 0.0225. One instance per\nisomorphism class (32,745 classes) gives medians of 1.000 at every `n`. By\nrealised density at 50–75 and by cell (`reports/fan_order_tables.md`), every\ncell's median lies in [0.994, 1.015], and the sign of the cell medians is not\neven consistent between configurations (`f_n75_m75_d4` is the cell where\n`degree` saves most under both, 0.996–0.999; the `m = n/2` fixed-`d` cells\nlean the other way at 1.01). Total nodes at 75: 8.71 G index against 8.54 G\ndegree; at the corpus 99–100 band 3.00 G against 3.04 G. Seconds beside them:\n4,421 s against 4,434 s at 75, so the degree sort costs nothing per node in\nthe C either.\nWhat *does* change with size is how often the order matters at all: the\nfraction of refutations whose node count differs between the two orders is\n0.1% at `n = 10`, 5.7% at 20, 23.9% at 30, 36.6% at 40, 61.4% at 50, 68.4%\nat 60 and 74.4% at 75. The order reaches the count on three refutations in\nfour at 75 and moves it by a fraction of a percent, in either direction. The\nmechanism is the one the docstrings state: the cost cut, the memo and the\ndominance rules are properties of the state `S`, so a refutation visits the\nsame set of states whichever order the fan is read in, and the order changes\nonly how the memo and the old-move set `Q(S)` happen to fill — which is why\nthe count changes at all, and why it cancels.\nThe censored pairs say the same thing at the deadline: on the 44 (`default`)\nand 40 (`csearch`) instances where both arms ran out, the degree arm had\nvisited 0.99× the index arm's nodes at the same wall clock (median), and on\nthe one instance per configuration where only the degree arm finished\n(`ens_f_n75_m150_d2_i010`: 134 M nodes `unsat` against ≥ 144 M unfinished;\n`_i045` under `csearch`: 76 M against ≥ 85 M) the two counts are within 7%\nof each other — a deadline artefact, not a speed-up.\n**Kill criterion (plan 2 §2.5a): met, for both configurations.** The paired\nmedian node change is 0.0% at every size from 10 to 100 customers; the fan\norder does not matter for refutations and the conjecture, as a statement about\nthe complete search's cost, is closed.\n### (b) The witness search: the same median, a different distribution\n`decide(optimum)` under both orders, `default` configuration (`csearch`\nwithin 0.03 of every entry):\nThe median is still 1.000 — most witness searches take a handful of nodes and\nthe first leaf is the witness under either order — but the distribution is\nskewed left and the skew grows with `n`: the geometric mean falls from 0.998\nat 10 to 0.945 at 40, 0.859 at 60 and 0.813 at 75, the total node count of\nthe band falls to 0.65 at 75 and 0.87 at 99–100, and the p10 reaches 0.35.\nWhere the witness search is *hard* (index arm ≥ 10⁴ nodes) the effect is\nlarge and still two-sided:\nBy cell the gain sits on the ridge and its sparse side: `f_n60_m120_d2`\n(total 0.43), `f_n75_m75_d2` (0.45), `f_n75_m150_d2` (0.51), `f_n75_m75_d3`\n(0.62), `b_n75_m75_p0.025` (0.62); the dense cells are neutral to slightly\nworse (`f_n50_m25_d9` 1.05, `b_n50_m25_p0.05` 1.15). This is §7's finding in\nthe search's own terms: the degree tie-break is the two-key rule's second\nkey, and under it the first leaf of the fan is the rule's greedy whenever the\ncost cut lets it through, so the witness is found earlier on the instances\nwhere the rule beats MCN — the sparse ones. It is also §18 (b)'s finding —\nlabels move the satisfiable side, not the refutation — with the label noise\nreplaced by a rule. The p90 above 1 on every band is the price: a third of\nthe hard searches get slower, some by 10×, because a greedy that is right on\naverage is wrong on a third of the instances and the DFS then has to back out\nof its first leaf.\nWhat it is worth in a descent is bounded by §18 (b): at `n = 75` the witness\nsearch is 204 nodes against 26,200 for the refutation (medians), 456 M\nagainst 8.7 G in total, so a 35% saving on the witness side is 1.7% of the\nband's node count. Where it would count is a driver that asks only\nsatisfiable questions — `benchmarks.ratchet`, or the `k ≥ optimum` calls at\nthe top of a descent, whose seconds are what `on_improve` exists to save.\n### (c) `restricted_dfs`: a smaller overshoot, a slower Python\nThe `cs-dfs` bound with its default MCN seed and 200,000-node budget, value\nover the certified optimum:\nThe total overshoot falls from 24,178 to 23,860 stacks (−1.3%); the gain is\nwhere the DFS is weakest — `corpus 99–100` 3.21 → 2.95, worst 10 → 8 — and it\nhas the same sign structure as the witness search: sparse cells better\n(`f_n50_m25_d4` 17 better / 2 worse), dense `m = n/2` cells worse\n(`b_n60_m30_p0.2` 2 / 11). The extra 50% of wall clock is the Python sort\nkey, a lambda called once per candidate per node, not the search; a\n`(cost, -degree, index)` tuple built in the scoring loop would remove it.\nRead against §7, where seeding the DFS with the rule's own greedy order took\nthe held-out MAE from 0.311 to 0.127: changing the fan order under the MCN\nseed recovers almost none of that. The gain of `cs-dfs+rule` came from the\n*incumbent* the DFS prunes against, not from the order it reads the fan in;\nwith the MCN incumbent, the cost cut removes the rule's leaf before the DFS\nreaches it on most of the instances where the rule would have won.\n### Proposed defaults — stated, not applied\n- **`decide`**: keep `fan_order=\"index\"`. The refutation is indifferent to the\n  order at every size measured (median change 0.0%, p10–p90 within ±0.8%),\n  and the refutation is 95–98% of a descent's cost. Nothing to gain, and\n  every recorded node count in the repository stays comparable.\n- **Satisfiable-side drivers** (`benchmarks.ratchet`, the `k ≥ optimum`\n  calls of a descent, a portfolio on the witness side as §18 (f) proposed):\n  `fan_order=\"degree\"` saves 35% of the witness-search nodes at 75 and 13%\n  at 100 in total, at a p90 cost of 1.1–1.3× per instance. Worth enabling\n  there and nowhere else; the owner's change.\n- **`restricted_dfs`**: `cs-dfs+degree` is registered and marginally better\n  (+0.1 pt exact, −1.3% total overshoot, better on the sparse large\n  instances where the bound is worst) and 50% slower in the current Python.\n  Not worth changing the default for; if a stronger `cs-dfs` is wanted, §7's\n  rule as the *seed* is the measured route (0.127 vs 0.311).\n**Size range.** Campaign 10–75 customers (44,547 instances, both generators,\n`m ∈ {n/2, n, 2n}`), corpus 9–100 (6,349). Nothing here is evidence about\n125 × 125 except that the refutation ratio's spread *narrows* with `n`\n(p90 1.004 at 40, 1.008 at 75–100) while the fraction of affected\nrefutations rises; an extrapolation would put the 125 × 125 ratio at 1.00 ±\n0.01, and the 11 censored corpus pairs at 100 show 0.99 at the deadline.\n**Method notes.** The C keeps `cs_decide` with its old signature and adds\n`cs_decide_fan`; a long-running process that forks workers late (the\n`benchmarks.recertify` run, up since 2026-09-24, nine processes before and\nafter) would otherwise have loaded a library whose entry point took one more\nargument than its in-memory `argtypes` said. `native._build` now compiles to a\nscratch name and renames into place for the same reason. The Python omits\ncustomers with no products from its witness and the C lists them as free\nmoves — a pre-existing difference in witness *format* that a naive C-vs-Python\norder comparison trips on; compare on the active customers. And a recorded\ncount is only as exact as the CSV that stored it: `results_upward.csv` wrote\nsix significant digits.\n## 21. Extremal search and exact treewidth: what are the worst small instances, and is pathwidth − treewidth bounded? (plan 2 §2.7, loop0003 item 08)\n","numLines":443,"startLine":741,"totalLines":1183}}}
