{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0188pWNJx3oZ1dVZ3Si6Tsfa","type":"tool_result","content":"715\t*tight* on 40.6% of them and beats `lb_best` on 92.5%. Above 60 customers the\n716\tone-second search settles nothing and the row is unchanged. Among the 209\n717\tgap instances with exact treewidth, pathwidth strictly exceeds it on 142\n718\t(68%) — consistent with §21 (c)'s 164 of 338 under a 60-second budget, of\n719\twhich this is a subset.\n720\t### (b) The mined candidates: 47 survivors, 36 of them treewidth in disguise\n721\t11,250 of the 11,589 candidates are valid (every fitted one by construction;\n722\t12 of the 351 raw ones, among them `tw + 1`, `ω`, the expansion bound, the\n723\tbranch lemma, `degeneracy`, `deg_min` and the block–cut and clique-tree\n724\tpathwidths). **47 exceed `lb_best` on more than 5% of the gap instances.**\n725\tSorted by `exceed_gap`, the first sixteen are `tw_lo + 1`, `tw1`, and `tw1`\n726\tdivided by things that are 1 on connected graphs (`components`, `blt1`) —\n727\tthe theorem restated fourteen ways, all at 58.0–58.9%. Then come the fitted\n728\tones:\n729\tThe leading fitted survivor — treewidth times the sixth expansion level,\n730\tscaled — beats `tw + 1` on 19% of the gap instances, yet **never** beats\n731\t`max(lb_best, tw + 1)` on any of the 50,948 instances where it is defined: it\n732\tis a lower envelope of two proved bounds, fitted. **Of the 47 survivors, 36\n733\tare pointwise ≤ tw + 1 on the gap instances and 11 are novel; two of the 11\n734\tare clean on hold-out.** Over the whole table, 77% of the fitted valid\n735\tcandidates (8,668 of 11,238) put at least one held-out instance above its\n736\toptimum when their constant is re-chosen on four fifths of the groups: a\n737\tconstant fitted on 51,000 instances is not a conjecture, it is the maximum\n738\tof a sample.\n739\t### (c) Where novelty lives: only where treewidth is unresolved\n740\tEvery gap instance on which any of the five beats the proved reference has\n741\t60 or more customers and an unresolved treewidth interval. Over all 3,689\n742\tnovel candidates: 14,850 beat-rows in total, 5,215 of them on the 44,578\n743\trows with exact treewidth, spread over 1,161 candidates, **none of which\n744\tbeats the proved bound on more than 0.1% of the exact rows** (maximum 28\n745\trows, median 2 among those that beat it at all) — the signature of a\n746\tconstant scraping the maximum of a sample, not of a bound. Three of the five\n747\tnever claim more than min-fill treewidth + 1 on the rows where they are\n748\tnovel, so what they \"know\" is that the true treewidth of a dense 100-customer\n749\tgraph is above MMD+; that is a statement about a heuristic, not about\n750\tpathwidth. Only `0.1472 · sqrt(n_cliques) · contraction` claims above\n751\t`tw_hi + 1` on more than a single row (15), and it leaks on hold-out twice.\n752\t### (d) The adversary: nothing broke, and that means little\n753\tAll fifteen candidates of the n ≤ 15 attack set (the `tw1` control, the\n754\tfourteen leading attackable distinct survivors, and the two that beat the\n755\tproved reference on more than 5% of the gap instances) **survived 19,200\n756\texact-solver evaluations each**; 528 of the 720 jobs reached an instance\n757\twhere the candidate equals the optimum (violation 0), and none exceeded it.\n758\tThe two novel ones never even reached the optimum (best −1): at n ≤ 15 their\n759\tfitted constants make them slack by construction. The supplementary run at\n760\tn ∈ {20, 25, 30} (ten candidates, 900 evaluations each, oracle 0.1–0.6 s\n761\tper call) also found nothing: the treewidth-times-expansion candidate and\n762\tthe clique-tree ones touch the optimum (0) and the three additive candidates\n763\tsit 14–18 below it, as their constants of −13.8 to −30.8 dictate. **No\n764\tcounterexample was drawn because none was found**; the counterexample files\n765\tare committed empty. The search is duplicate-bound at these sizes — 22.2\n766\tmillion canonical repeats for 288,000 evaluations — so more steps would buy\n767\tnothing; what would test the five novel candidates is an oracle at 75–125\n768\tcustomers on dense graphs, which is the corpus itself, and there they are\n769\tvalid by construction. Their adversarial record is therefore *vacuous*, not\n770\t*clean*, and the section says so.\n771\t### (e) A bug the \"must be 0\" column caught\n772\tThe branch-lemma bound as first written counted every component of `G − v`\n773\tas a branch at `v`, including components of `G` that never touched `v`. On a\n774\tforest with a claw and three disjoint edges it took the third-largest\n775\ttreewidth over six components (1) and claimed pathwidth ≥ 2 on an instance of\n776\toptimum 2 — **347 rows above the optimum**, all by exactly one, all with\n777\tseveral components (297 campaign, 48 upward, 2 corpus). The lemma is right;\n778\tthe count was wrong. Fixed (`branching_invariants` keeps only the components\n779\tcontaining a neighbour of `v`), with a regression test, and the 8,292\n780\tmulti-component rows recomputed in place by the `repair` stage (6,778 rows\n781\tchanged). After the fix the branch lemma is valid on all 50,949, tight on 388\n782\t(0.8%), above `lb_best` on 28 rows (0.05%) and never on a gap instance: only\n783\t10 of the 338 gap instances have a cut vertex with three branches at all, 99\n784\thave any cut vertex, and 31 have a block–cut tree of pathwidth ≥ 2. The\n785\t\"trees of cliques with branching\" of §6 are branching in the sense of\n786\t*separators*, not of *cut vertices*; the lemma sees the wrong kind.\n787\t### The finding, in one paragraph\n788\tThe family of two-invariant formulas over branching-aware invariants\n789\tproduces **no conjecture**. Its 47 survivors are 36 restatements of the\n790\ttheorem `optimum ≥ tw + 1`, two fitted lower envelopes of that theorem and\n791\t`lb_best` that never beat their pair, and nine fitted constants that are\n792\t\"novel\" only on 100–125-customer graphs where treewidth is an unresolved\n793\tinterval — 77% of all fitted candidates leak on hold-out, none beats the\n794\tproved bound on more than 0.1% of the instances where treewidth is exact,\n795\tand the adversary cannot reach the sizes where they claim anything. The one\n796\tobject the mining put on the table is the theorem it started from: **exact\n797\ttreewidth**, which the solver's bound does not compute and which lifts the\n798\tcertified lower bound on 933 corpus instances and 199 of the 338 gap\n799\tinstances, to tightness on 67. The plan's kill criterion is met in the sense\n800\tthat matters — *the bound work needs a new idea, not a new formula* — and\n801\tthe new idea has to be a pathwidth argument that sees separators of trees of\n802\tcliques, which none of the thirty invariants here does.\n803\t**Kill criterion (plan 2 §2.6: no survivor exceeds `lb_best` on more than 5%\n804\tof the gap instances while surviving the adversary).** *Literally not met*:\n805\t`tw + 1` exceeds `lb_best` on 58.9% and survives, being a theorem, and the\n806\tfitted `⌊0.9428 · sqrt(tw · f6)⌋` exceeds it on 32.3% and survives 20,100\n807\tevaluations at 8–30 customers. *Met against the honest reference*: no\n808\tcandidate exceeds `max(lb_best, tw + 1)` on more than 0.1% of the 44,578\n809\tinstances with exact treewidth, none on any gap instance below 60\n810\tcustomers, and the five above 5% on the gap instances do so only where\n811\ttreewidth is unresolved. The section's verdict is the second.\n812\t**Handed to item 12.** Two theorems and one conjecture, in the deliverable's\n813\tform. (1) *Theorem:* `optimum ≥ tw(G) + 1` — via `optimum = pw + 1` and\n814\t`tw ≤ pw`; tight on 77.9% of 50,949 certified instances at 9–134 customers,\n815\ton 19.8% of the gap instances; beats `lb_best` on 14.8% / 58.9%. (2)\n816\t*Theorem:* if a cut vertex has three branches of treewidth ≥ k then\n817\t`pw ≥ k + 1` (the branch lemma; the proof is the interval argument on a path\n818\tdecomposition of width k, in which each branch owns a full bag and the\n819\tmiddle one separates the other two from `v`); valid on all 50,949, tight on\n820\t0.8%, and it never exceeds `lb_best` on a gap instance, so its value is as a\n821\tstatement, not a bound. (3) *Conjecture, stated because the deliverable asks\n822\tfor survivors and labelled worthless:* `optimum ≥ ⌊0.9428 · sqrt(tw(G) · f(6))⌋`\n823\twhere `f(6) = min_{|C| = 6} |N[C]|` — valid on 50,948 certified instances,\n824\tclean on grouped hold-out, undefeated by 20,100 adversarial evaluations at\n825\t8–30 customers, and pointwise ≤ `max(lb_best, tw + 1)` on every one of them,\n826\tso proving it would prove nothing new. No counterexample to draw.\n827\t**Proposed, not applied.** Exact/interval treewidth as a component of\n828\t`_lower_bound`: the subset DP to 26 customers and the decision search to 64\n829\tunder a one-second deadline cost 0.2–1 s per instance (39 s at worst over\n830\tthe 50,949) and raise the certified floor on 933 corpus instances, making 67\n831\tgap instances bound-certified with no refutation. It rests on Yanasse's\n832\tequality like the contraction component. Against §6's ceiling it changes\n833\tnothing where `pw > tw`, which is 68% of the exact gap instances, so it is a\n834\tbetter floor, not a different kind of one. The owner's change; nothing here\n835\ttouches the solver.\n836\t**Size range covered.** Invariants and validity: 9–134 customers, all\n837\t50,949 certified instances (corpus 6,376; campaign 37,800 at 10–40; upward\n838\t6,773 at 50–100). Exact treewidth: to 26 by DP (25,731 rows) and to 64 by a\n839\tone-second decision search (18,680 of 22,825 rows settled); above 64 nothing\n840\tis exact (2,393 rows, heuristics only), and every statement about \"where\n841\ttreewidth is exact\" is a statement about n ≤ 64. The adversary: 8–15 (plan)\n842\tand 20–30 (supplementary); it cannot test a candidate whose fitted constant\n843\tmakes it vacuous below 30, and says so per candidate (`attackable`). The\n844\tfive novel candidates are supported only at 60–125 customers on the dense\n845\tChu & Stuckey and upward `m = n/2` cells.\n846\t**Not a bound, not a solver change.** No candidate reaches\n847\t`satisfiability/mosp_solver.py::_lower_bound` or any path that decides `k`;\n848\t`learning.treewidth` is called by the study only. The one number that could\n849\tmove the solver — exact treewidth beats `lb_best` on 933 corpus instances —\n850\tis a proposal above.\n851\t**Method notes.** The \"above optimum (must be 0)\" column is the cheapest\n852\ttheorem-checker in the repository and found a real bug in a lemma\n853\timplementation on the first run: keep it in every table that lists proved\n854\tbounds. A constant fitted as the extreme of 51,000 values leaks on hold-out\n855\t77% of the time; the plan's \"valid on all certified instances\" is necessary\n856\tand nowhere near sufficient, and an honest reference (`max` of the proved\n857\tbounds) separates theorems-in-disguise from candidates before the adversary\n858\tis spent on them. The adversary at n ≤ 15 exhausts its neighbourhoods —\n859\t77 duplicates per evaluation — so the budget should go to sizes, not steps;\n860\tat n = 30 the oracle (`solve_mosp_exact` plus exact treewidth plus the\n861\texpansion profile) still costs under a second. `kill $!` after\n862\t`nohup python -m …` killed a wrapper and left a 16-worker pool running for\n863\ttwo minutes: kill the pool by its parent PID (`pgrep -P`) and check with\n864\t`ps` that the tree is gone. `_generated_matrix` takes an `itertuples` row,\n865\tnot a `Series` (whose `.index` is the Index).\n866\t## 25. Why the ridge is where it is: one parameter across m / n (plan 2 §2.9, loop0003 item 11)\n867\t*Iteration 13 of loop0003, 2026-09-26. Code: `learning/ridge_theory.py`. Regenerate:*\n868\t```bash\n869\tpython -m learning.ridge_theory --stage run --workers 16      # the deciding cells: m = n/4 at n = 50, 60, 75 and m = n/8 at 75; 2,750 instances, 82 s wall, 0.36 core-hours\n870\tpython -m learning.ridge_theory --stage cliques --workers 16  # maximal-clique excess on the 7,125 instances at n >= 40 with MOSP-graph density <= 0.35; ~2 min\n871\tpython -m learning.ridge_theory --stage tables                # reports/ridge_theory_tables.md + reports/figures/ridge_theory.png, 2 s\n872\tpython -m pytest tests/test_ridge_theory.py -q                # 9 tests\n873\t```\n874\t*Reads `learning/data/ensemble/results.csv` (§10, 37,800 at 10–40),\n875\t`results_upward.csv` with its finish stage (§16, 6,773 certified at 50–100)\n876\tand the new `results_ratio.csv` (2,750 at 50–75, all certified, every witness\n877\tre-simulating, zero censored refutations; manifest `manifest_ratio.csv`, 300\n878\tsampled and regenerated byte for byte; witnesses under\n879\t`learning/data/ensemble/solutions/`; the ensemble directory is 82 MB\n880\tapparent). Writes `reports/ridge_theory_tables.md` (every table in full),\n881\t`learning/data/ensemble/ridge_clique_excess.csv` and two run logs. Nothing\n882\tunder `solutions/`, no solver default, no bound.*\n883\t**Question.** §11 put the hardness ridge — the density at which the nodes to\n884\trefute `optimum − 1` peak at fixed `n` — at three customers per product when\n885\t`m = n` and two when `m = 2n`; §16 found it at five to six when `m = n / 2`.\n886\tThe plan's hypothesis was that both §11 locations are one MOSP-graph mean\n887\tdegree, 8–9. Is there a parameter of `G(n, m, p)` at which the ridge sits for\n888\tevery `m / n`, and if so which? What does the literature's transition for\n889\tsparse random graphs (giant component; linear treewidth and pathwidth) have to\n890\tdo with it?\n891\t**Method.** (a) *Analytics in `col_mean` coordinates* (`c` = realised customers\n892\tper product, `r = m / n`): the expected degree of the MOSP graph — fixed\n893\tgenerator `(n − 1)[1 − (1 − c(c − 1) / (n(n − 1)))^m]`, Bernoulli\n894\t`(n − 1)[1 − (1 − (c/n)²)^m]`; the edge probability; the branching factor\n895\t`r c (c − 1)` (fixed) or `r c²` (Bernoulli), which is the expected number of new\n896\tcustomers reached through one customer's other products and whose crossing of\n897\t1 is the giant-component threshold of the random hypergraph and hence of its\n898\t2-section (Schmidt-Pruzan & Shamir 1985; Karoński & Łuczak 2002); and the\n899\t**excess** `(n_ones − m) / n = Σ_j (|C_j| − 1) / n = r (c − 1)`, the mass of the\n900\tproduct cliques over a spanning tree of cliques per customer — at 1 the\n901\tcustomer–product incidence graph has as many edges as vertices, and\n902\t`excess − 1` is its cyclomatic number per customer. The degree derivation is\n903\tchecked against the measured `g_deg_mean` in every cell. (b) *Peaks*: for every\n904\t(generator, `m / n`, `n ≥ 30`) the cell with the largest censoring-aware median\n905\tof `log10(1 + nodes)` (`default` configuration), the vertex of the parabola in\n906\t`log10 col_mean` through it and its two neighbours when the peak is interior,\n907\tand a bootstrap interval (500 resamples of instances within those cells).\n908\t(c) *Constancy*: each candidate's value at the peak (its cell medians\n909\tinterpolated to the vertex), and the coefficient of variation across the\n910\tratios at fixed (generator, `n`) for `n ≥ 50`, where three to five ratios\n911\texist. (d) *Calibrated prediction*: every hypothesis \"the ridge is where\n912\tcandidate `X` equals its `m = n` value\" is calibrated on the `m = n` peaks at\n913\t50–75 of its own generator and predicts the other ratios' peak `col_mean` —\n914\tby inversion for the analytic candidates, by interpolating `X`'s cell medians\n915\talong the series for the empirical ones — scored by `|log10(predicted /\n916\tmeasured)|` and by the bootstrap interval. (e) *The deciding cells*: the\n917\tcampaign had `m ∈ {n, 2n}` and §16 added `n / 2`; at `m = n / 4` the degree\n918\tand excess hypotheses are 0.17 decades apart and at `m = n / 8` 0.37, so this\n919\titem generated fixed `d ∈ {3, …, 10, 12}` and Bernoulli `p ∈ {0.05, …, 0.2}`\n920\tat `m = n / 4` for `n ∈ {50, 60, 75}` (50 per cell) and fixed `d ∈ {4, 6, …,\n921\t20, 24}` at `m = n / 8`, `n = 75`. Also: §11's collapse test with the series\n922\tkeyed by (generator, ratio) at 50–75; the ridge's *height* against `m` at\n923\tfixed `n`; the instances at 30–40 customers binned by branching factor, to see\n924\twhere the giant component and the linear regime of the optimum begin relative\n925\tto the ridge; and a graph-side excess, `Σ over maximal cliques (|K| − 1) / n`,\n926\tto test whether the incidence statement is also a graph statement.\n927\t**Baseline.** \"Three customers per product\" (§11), and the plan's \"mean\n928\tdegree 8–9\".\n929\t### (a) The derivation holds; the plan's premise does not\n930\tPredicted against measured mean degree, cells at `n ≥ 40`: median relative\n931\terror within ±0.5% at `m ∈ {n, 2n}`, −1% to −6% at `m ≤ n / 2` (the fixed\n932\tgenerator's empty-row repair adds edges the formula omits); the worst cells\n933\t(+39% and +66%) are the two sparsest Bernoulli ones, where `p n < 1` and the\n934\trepairs dominate. In `col_mean` coordinates the giant threshold is at\n935\t`c = (1 + √(1 + 4 / r)) / 2` (fixed) or `1 / √r` (Bernoulli): 1.37 / 1.62 / 2.0\n936\t/ 2.6 / 3.4 for `r` = 2 / 1 / ½ / ¼ / ⅛ on the fixed grid. The ridge is\n937\t**not** there: it sits 1.5× (`m = 2n`) to 6× (`m = n / 8`) above it, at\n938\tbranching factors 4 to 49 — the ratio to the threshold is not constant either.\n939\tAnd the mean degree at the peak is **3.9 / 7.4–8.4 / 10.5–12.0 / 15.8–21.4 /\n940\t37.6** for `m = 2n / n / n/2 / n/4 / n/8` (fixed generator, 50–75), a factor\n941\tof 9.5 across the ratios; the \"8–9\" of the plan is the Bernoulli `m ∈ {n, 2n}`\n942\tcoincidence at `n ≤ 40` (6.7–8.4), which the fixed generator at the same\n943\tsizes already breaks (3.8 against 6.4–6.9).\n944\t### (b) The peaks, interpolated (n = 50–75; the `m = 2n` fixed peak is at its grid edge, d = 2, a lower bound on its location)\n945\tThe `col_mean` of the ridge runs from 2.0 to 20.7 — a factor of ten — as `m`\n946\tfalls from `2n` to `n / 8`, and the products per customer at the ridge run\n947\t4.0 / 3.3–3.5 / 2.6–2.8 / 2.2–2.7 / 2.5 (fixed): neither matrix coordinate is\n948\tconstant, as §16 (b) said. The **excess** at the ridge is 2.0–2.5 in every\n949\tfixed series and 1.8–3.0 in every Bernoulli series, at every `n`; it is the\n950\tonly analytic quantity that stays within a factor of 1.65 over the five\n951\tratios (§25 (c)). The `m = 2n` fixed peaks sit at the grid edge with excess\n952\texactly 2.0 — the excess-2 line is the edge — and the Bernoulli `m = 2n`\n953\tseries, whose grid does go sparser, has an interior peak at excess 2.1–3.0.\n954\t### (c) Constancy across m / n at fixed (generator, n), n = 50–75 (six (generator, n) pairs with three to five ratios each)\n955\t### (d) Calibrated on the m = n ridge, predicted at the other ratios (19 series; the 13 at m ≤ n / 2 are the deciding ones)\n956\t**The `m = n / 2` cells decide against the mean degree**: calibrated at its\n957\t`m = n` value it predicts the half-ratio ridge at 4.7 customers per product\n958\t(fixed) and 4.8–4.9 (Bernoulli); the measured peaks are 5.24–5.64 (fixed, every\n959\tinterval excluding 4.7) and 4.87–5.44 (Bernoulli, inside the interval at 50\n960\tand 60, outside at 75). The excess predicts 5.86–5.92 — inside the interval\n961\tat `n = 50` and 60 and 0.05 decades above the peak (0.03 above its interval) at 75 (fixed), 0.04–0.08 above\n962\t(Bernoulli). At `m = n / 4` the gap opens: degree says 6.4–6.5 and 6.9–7.1,\n963\tthe peaks are at 9.2–10.6 and 8.2–8.4; the excess says 10.7–11.1, inside the\n964\tfixed interval at `n = 60` and 0.06–0.08 decades above it at 50 and 75, and\n965\t0.12 decades above the Bernoulli peaks (whose excess is 1.8, not 2.4). At\n966\t`m = n / 8` only the excess survives: 21.2 against a measured 20.7 [18.7,\n967\t21.6], where the degree says 8.9, `optimum / n` 13.8 and treewidth 15.1.\n968\tThe two empirical invariants — `optimum / n` and `tw_min_fill / n`, both\n969\t≈ 0.32 at the ridge — predict the `n / 2` and `n / 4` peaks as well as the\n970\texcess or better, and fail by 0.14–0.18 decades at `n / 8`; they are also\n971\t*outputs* of the instance rather than parameters of the generator, and §14\n972\tshowed `optimum / n` is not the ridge's size-stable coordinate (0.16–0.20 on\n973\tthe 125 × 125 ridge class against 0.28–0.43 at `n ≤ 40`). The excess is\n974\tsize-free by construction, and the fixed `m = n` ridge has sat at `d = 3` —\n975\texcess 2 — at every `n` from 15 to 75 (§11, §16).\n976\t### (e) What the excess is, and what it is not\n977\t`(n_ones − m) / n` is `Σ_j (|C_j| − 1) / n`: each product, as a clique, needs\n978\t`|C_j| − 1` edges to be spanned, so the excess is the number of edges in a\n979\tspanning forest of the union of product cliques *if the products were glued\n980\tinto a tree of cliques*, per customer. At excess 1 (`n_ones = n + m`) the\n981\tcustomer–product incidence graph has as many edges as vertices — the\n982\thypergraph is at the edge of Berge-acyclicity; every unit above 1 is one\n983\tindependent cycle of the incidence graph per customer. The ridge at excess\n984\t≈ 2–2.4 is therefore \"one independent cycle per customer\", or \"the product\n985\tcliques carry twice the mass of a spanning tree of cliques\". In `col_mean`\n986\tcoordinates: **the ridge is at `col_mean ≈ 1 + 2.4 · n / m`** (fixed\n987\tgenerator; `1 + 2 n / m` puts it at 2 / 3 / 5 / 9 / 17 for the five ratios\n988\tagainst measured 2.0 / 3.3–3.5 / 5.2–5.6 / 9.2–10.6 / 20.7).\n989\tIt is **an incidence-graph parameter, not a MOSP-graph invariant**. §13 showed\n990\thardness is a property of the labelled graph and of nothing else, so the\n991\tstatement must be read as: for these generators, the graph the search sees is\n992\thardest to refute when its *generating cover* has excess about 2. The\n993\tgraph-side analogue — the sum over maximal cliques of `|K| − 1`, per customer\n994\t— equals the matrix excess where the products are the maximal cliques (ratio\n995\t1.00 at the median below MOSP-graph density 0.1, 2,130 instances) but not at\n996\tthe ridge: there, maximal cliques already outnumber products (0.8–0.85× at\n997\tthe `m = 2n` ridge, 1.3× at `m = n`, 2.6–5× at `n / 2`, 7–14× at `n / 4`;\n998\tcross-product triangles), and the maximal-clique excess at the peaks runs\n999\t1.8–11.8 (CV 0.33, worse than `optimum / n`), with the `n / 8` ridge past the\n1000\t20,000-clique cap. No MOSP-graph invariant among the fourteen tested is\n1001\tconstant at the ridge across `m / n` to better than a factor of 1.9\n1002\t(bandwidth / n, 1.94); the two that come closest (`bw_rcm / n`, `optimum / n`)\n1003\tare width measures, i.e. the answer itself.\n1004\t### (f) The literature's transition is not the ridge\n1005\tInstances at 30–40 customers (`m ∈ {n, 2n}`, 16,200) binned by branching\n1006\tfactor: below 2 the largest component holds 13–53% of the customers, 88–100%\n1007\tof instances decompose and `optimum / n` is 0.10–0.13; from 4 up every\n1008\tinstance is connected and `optimum / n` is 0.26 / 0.46 / 0.65 / 0.91 in the\n1009\tbins 4–8 / 8–16 / 16–32 / ≥ 32. Median nodes: 19 / 72 / **898 / 961** / 226 /\n1010\t5. So the giant component (threshold 1 in the limit; realised at 2–4 at these\n1011\tsizes because of the repairs and finite `n`) is where the optimum becomes\n1012\tlinear in `n` — the regime the literature describes, treewidth and pathwidth\n1013\tboth `Θ(n)` above the giant threshold of `G(n, c/n)` (Lee, Lee & Oum 2012; Gao\n1014\t2012, whose result covers random intersection graphs; Kloks 1994 for `c >\n1015\t2.36`), against `O(log n)` pathwidth below it where every component is a tree\n1016\tor unicyclic — and the ridge sits an order of magnitude further in, at\n1017\tbranching factors 4–49 (excess 2), well inside the linear regime. The\n1018\tliterature's transition says where the problem stops being trivially small;\n1019\tthe ridge is where, among linear-width instances, the refutation is most\n1020\texpensive, and that is a property of the cover's excess, not of the\n1021\tconnectivity threshold.\n1022\t### (g) The ridge's height scales with m, steeply\n1023\tAt the interpolated peak, median log10 nodes against `m` at fixed `n` (fixed\n1024\tgenerator): `n = 75`: 2.14 (m = 9) / 3.73 (18) / 5.46 (37) / 6.78 (75) /\n1025\t≥ 8.12 (150); slope of log10 height in log10 `m` **2.9 at n = 50, 3.4 at 60,\n1026\t4.9 at 75** (Bernoulli 2.0 / 2.2 / 3.5) — one to one and a half decades per\n1027\tdoubling of the products at fixed customers, and steepening with `n`. So no\n1028\tsingle parameter *collapses* the surface: §11's collapse test over the eight\n1029\tto nine (generator, ratio) series at 50–75 gives dispersion 2.0–2.4 decades\n1030\tand r² 0.38–0.56 against a cell ceiling of 0.94–0.97 for every candidate,\n1031\tbecause the series differ in height by four decades at the same location.\n1032\tThe excess locates the ridge; the number of products sets its height.\n1033\t**Finding, in one paragraph.** The ridge is not a mean-degree condition: the\n1034\tMOSP-graph mean degree at the peak runs from 3.9 at `m = 2n` to 37.6 at\n1035\t`m = n / 8` (a factor of 9.5), and the hypothesis calibrated at `m = n`\n1036\tpredicts the `m = n / 2` peak at 4.7 customers per product against a measured\n1037\t5.2–5.6 whose intervals all exclude it, the `n / 4` peak at 6.4 against 9.2–10.6,\n1038\tand the `n / 8` peak at 8.9 against 20.7. The parameter that is the same\n1039\tacross `m / n` is the **excess of the product cover**, `(n_ones − m) / n =\n1040\tΣ_j (|C_j| − 1) / n`, equal to 2.0–2.5 at every fixed-generator peak from\n1041\t`m = 2n` to `m = n / 8` and 1.8–3.0 at every Bernoulli peak (n = 50–75; the\n1042\tbest constancy among fifteen candidates, CV 0.13, and the only one within a\n1043\tfactor of 1.65 across ratios); calibrated at `m = n` it predicts the other\n1044\tridges to 0.055 decades on average and the deciding `n / 8` ridge to 0.012.\n1045\tIn `col_mean` coordinates the ridge is at about `1 + 2.4 n / m` customers per\n1046\tproduct — 2 / 3 / 5–6 / 9–11 / 21 — which is also why it is at \"three\" for\n1047\t`m = n`. It is a condition on the incidence graph (one independent cycle per\n1048\tcustomer), not on a MOSP-graph invariant: the maximal-clique version of the\n1049\tsame sum is not constant (1.8–11.8) and no graph invariant tested is within a\n1050\tfactor of 1.9. The giant-component threshold of the literature (branching\n1051\tfactor 1, the onset of linear width) lies 1.5–6× below the ridge in `col_mean`\n1052\tand is where the optimum becomes linear, not where the search is hardest; the\n1053\tridge's *height* is set by `m` (1–1.5 decades per doubling at fixed `n`),\n1054\twhich is why the `m = 2n` series is the hardest at every size.\n1055\t**Size range covered.** Peaks and predictions at `n ∈ {50, 60, 75}`, `m / n\n1056\t∈ {⅛ (n = 75 only, fixed only), ¼, ½, 1, 2}`, both generators, 50–150\n1057\tinstances per cell, 9,497 certified instances in 190 cells; the `n ≤ 40`\n1058\tpeaks (`m ∈ {n, 2n}`) from the §10 campaign confirm the excess at 2.0–2.6 at\n1059\t30–40. The branching-factor bins cover 30–40. Nothing here is evidence about\n1060\t125 × 125 except through §14/§16: at `Random-125-125-2` (`col_mean` 2.76,\n1061\t`m = n`) the excess is 1.76 and at density 4 (4.2) it is 3.2, so the excess\n1062\tplaces the 125 × 125 ridge at `col_mean ≈ 3`, between Chu & Stuckey's two\n1063\tday-long classes and nearer density 2 — consistent with density 2 being 2–6×\n1064\tharder than density 4 at every size (§14), and a prediction, not a\n1065\tmeasurement.\n1066\t**Kill criterion.** None stated by the plan. Deliverable as asked: the ridge\n1067\tas a condition on one parameter across `m / n` — the excess of the cover,\n1068\t`(n_ones − m) / n ≈ 2`–2.4 — decided by the `m = n / 2` cells against the mean\n1069\tdegree and confirmed at `m = n / 4` and `m = n / 8`, with the qualification\n1070\tthat the parameter is an incidence-graph quantity and that no MOSP-graph\n1071\tinvariant does the same job.\n1072\t**Method notes.** *The grid's coarseness sets the resolution.* Cell-wise peaks\n1073\tat the Bernoulli `m = 2n` series jumped between `col_mean` 1.9 and 3.0 as `p`\n1074\tstepped 0.025 → 0.05; the parabolic vertex through three cells, with the\n1075\tbootstrap over instances within them, is what makes a 0.05-decade comparison\n1076\tpossible, and the bootstrap interval is a within-cell interval only (it does\n1077\tnot see grid placement), so the \"within interval\" counts understate agreement\n1078\tand the log error is the primary score. *Kill a pool by its parent.* The\n1079\tmaximal-clique stage was first run on every instance; two near-complete\n1080\tBernoulli graphs at `n = 75` have exponentially many maximal cliques and two\n1081\tworkers ran while fourteen slept — the stage now takes a density cutoff and a\n1082\t20,000-clique cap (past it both values are NaN), and the stuck run was killed\n1083\tby `pgrep -P <parent>`. *Instance files.* `ensemble.run` writes `.mosp` files\n1084\tunless told not to; the manifest of seeds is the artifact, and the 2,750\n1085\tfiles were removed after the manifest verified. *The `m = n // 2` ratio at\n1086\t`n = 75` is 0.493*: ratios are snapped to the nearest of ⅛, ¼, ½, 1, 2 for\n1087\tgrouping and kept exact for every formula.\n1088\t## 26. The sandwich in Lean: `degeneracy ≤ pathwidth ≤ bandwidth` as theorems (plan 2 §2.10, loop0003 item 12)\n1089\t*Iteration 14 of loop0003, 2026-09-26. Code: `lean/MOSPFormalization/Sandwich.lean`\n1090\t(578 lines, 32 theorems, 3 `sorry`), `learning/sandwich.py`. Regenerate:*\n1091\t```bash\n1092\tcd lean && lake build                       # 1,638 jobs; ~2 s from the cache, the Mathlib build otherwise\n1093\tpython -m learning.sandwich                 # brute force to n = 5, corpus recount, lake build + #print axioms; 5 s\n1094\tpython -m pytest tests/test_sandwich.py -q  # 7 tests\n1095\t```\n1096\t*Writes `reports/sandwich_tables.md` (every table below). Reads\n1097\t`learning/data/instances.csv` for the corpus recount. Nothing here is a bound\n1098\tthe solver uses, a solver change, or a write to `solutions/`.*\n1099\t**The question.** §5 found `degeneracy + 1 ≤ optimum ≤ bw_rcm + 1` on all\n1100\t6,376 corpus instances and called the two ends \"two theorems, checked\". The\n1101\tLean development already had linear layouts, vertex separation, path\n1102\tdecompositions and Kinnersley's `vertexSeparation = pathwidth`\n1103\t(`VSEquivPW.lean`). Can the two ends be *proved* there, over those\n1104\tdefinitions, so that the checked sandwich rests on something other than\n1105\t44,000 instances? Item 09 (§24) added two theorems and one conjecture to\n1106\tstate.\n1107\t### (a) What was defined\n1108\tAll over the repository's `LinearLayout V := V ≃ Fin (Fintype.card V)` and\n1109\tMathlib's `SimpleGraph V` with `[Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj]`,\n1110\tin `namespace MOSPFormalization`:\n1111\tThe two degeneracy forms are both there because the corpus computes the\n1112\tLick–White form and the proof against vertex separation goes through the\n1113\tordering form; they are proved equal (`orderingDegeneracy_eq_degeneracy`),\n1114\tso either may be read as *the* degeneracy.\n1115\t### (b) What was proved (no `sorry`, standard axioms only)\n1116\t`#print axioms` on each of the thirteen theorems named in `learning.sandwich.PROVED`\n1117\tgives `[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]` (`reports/sandwich_tables.md`,\n1118\tlast table; `learning.sandwich --stage lean` fails if `sorryAx` ever appears on\n1119\tone of them or disappears from a stated one).\n1120\t- **Upper half.** `vertexSepAt_le_bandwidthOfLayout`: at cut `i` every active\n1121\t  suffix vertex `v` has a prefix neighbour `u`, so `i < σ v ≤ σ u + b ≤ i + b`;\n1122\t  `σ` is injective, and the window `Ioc i (i + b)` has `b` positions, so\n1123\t  `card (activeSuffix) ≤ b` (`Finset.card_le_card_of_injOn` into `Finset.Ioc`,\n1124\t  `Nat.card_Ioc`). Then `vertexSepOfLayout_le_bandwidthOfLayout` (`sup'_le`),\n1125\t  `vertexSeparation_le_bandwidth` (`le_csInf` over the range, as\n1126\t  `pathwidth_le_vertexSeparation` does), and through `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth`:\n1127\t  **`pathwidth_le_bandwidth`** and its layout-level form\n1128\t  **`pathwidth_le_bandwidthOfLayout σ`** — the latter is the exact statement the\n1129\t  corpus's `bw_rcm + 1 ≥ optimum` checks, since `bw_rcm` is one layout's stretch.\n1130\t- **Lower half.** `laterNeighbors_subset_activeSuffix`: a neighbour placed after\n1131\t  `v` is in the suffix at `σ v` with `v` itself as its prefix neighbour, so\n1132\t  `card (laterNeighbors v) ≤ vertexSepAt (σ v) ≤ vertexSepOfLayout`\n1133\t  (`vertexSepAt_le_vertexSepOfLayout`, a lemma the development lacked), whence\n1134\t  `maxLaterDegree_le_vertexSepOfLayout` and **`orderingDegeneracy_le_vertexSeparation`**.\n1135\t  `degreeIn_le_card_laterNeighbors`: the *first* vertex of any `S` under `σ`\n1136\t  (`Finset.exists_min_image`) has all its `S`-neighbours after it (irreflexivity\n1137\t  and injectivity give strictness), so `minDegreeIn S ≤ maxLaterDegree σ` for\n1138\t  every `S` and every `σ`, i.e. **`degeneracy_le_orderingDegeneracy`**. Chained:\n1139\t  **`degeneracy_le_vertexSeparation`**, **`degeneracy_le_pathwidth`**.\n1140\t- **The sandwich.** `degeneracy_le_pathwidth_le_bandwidth : degeneracy G ≤ pathwidth G ∧ pathwidth G ≤ bandwidth G`,\n1141\t  for every finite simple graph.\n1142\t- **The two degeneracy forms coincide.** `orderingDegeneracy_le_degeneracy`, by\n1143\t  the greedy elimination ordering: `deleteVertex G v` is `G.comap Subtype.val`\n1144\t  on `{u // u ≠ v}`, and `degeneracy_deleteVertex_le` says deleting a vertex\n1145\t  never raises the Lick–White degeneracy (a subset of the subtype is a subset\n1146\t  of `V` with the same induced degrees, `Finset.filter_map` + `card_map`);\n1147\t  `exists_layout_maxLaterDegree_le_degeneracy` is an induction on\n1148\t  `Fintype.card V` across types (`∀ n {V : Type u} …`): pick `v` of minimum\n1149\t  degree (`Finset.exists_mem_eq_inf'` on `univ`), lay out `{u // u ≠ v}` by\n1150\t  the hypothesis, and put `v` at position 0 through\n1151\t  `(optionSubtypeNe v).symm ≫ optionCongr σ' ≫ (finSuccEquiv _).symm ≫ finCongr`;\n1152\t  `v`'s later neighbours are its whole neighbourhood, everyone else's are\n1153\t  unchanged. Hence **`orderingDegeneracy_eq_degeneracy`**.\n1154\t- **In MOSP terms.** `MOSPInstance.degeneracy_add_one_le_mospValue_of_eq`:\n1155\t  `degeneracy + 1 ≤ mospValue` for any instance with `mospValue = pathwidth + 1`\n1156\t  (Yanasse's equality, taken as a hypothesis because the development has only the\n1157\t  `≤` direction, under `IsReduced`, and that one rests on the Hall-theorem `sorry`\n1158\t  of `Reduction.lean`). `MOSPInstance.mospValue_le_bandwidth_add_one` is the\n1159\t  upper half in MOSP terms; it is proved here but *inherits* that `sorry`, so the\n1160\t  axiom table lists it as stated.\n1161\t### (c) Do the definitions compute the right thing?\n1162\tA theorem about a mis-stated definition proves nothing, so the definitions\n1163\tare checked two ways. In the kernel (`decide`, in the Lean file): on the path\n1164\t`P₃` degeneracy 1, `minDegreeIn univ` 1, later-degree and separation and\n1165\tstretch of the identity layout all 1; on `K₃` degeneracy 2 and stretch 2; on\n1166\tthe edgeless graph 0 and 0; on the **spider** (subdivided `K_{1,3}`, treewidth\n1167\t1, pathwidth 2) degeneracy 1 over all 128 subsets (`maxRecDepth 10000`),\n1168\tlater-degree 3 and separation 3 for the centre-first layout. In Python\n1169\t(`learning.sandwich`, a literal transcription of the Lean definitions by\n1170\tbrute force over subsets and permutations) on **every labelled graph on 1–5\n1171\tvertices, 1,099 graphs**:\n1172\tZero violations: the Lick–White form is networkx's core number on every\n1173\tgraph, the ordering form equals it on every graph (the proved\n1174\t`orderingDegeneracy_eq_degeneracy`, here a guard on the two *definitions*),\n1175\tand `vertexSeparation` as defined equals\n1176\t`fixed_parameter_algorithm.pathwidth.compute_pathwidth`. The first graphs\n1177\twith `pw < bandwidth` are the four labellings of the claw `K_{1,3}`\n1178\t(degeneracy 1, pathwidth 1, bandwidth 2: the centre must sit within one\n1179\tposition of three leaves); the first with `degeneracy < pathwidth` appear at\n1180\t`n = 5` (30 of 1,024).\n1181\t### (d) The corpus recount\n1182\t`learning.sandwich --stage corpus`, from `learning/data/instances.csv`:\n1183\tThe 2,823 is §5's \"ends coincide and force the optimum on 2,823\", regenerated.\n1184\tWith (b), both columns of inequalities are now theorems about the graph\n1185\t(given `optimum = pathwidth + 1`), and the recount is a check on the\n1186\t*implementations* of `g_degeneracy`, `bw_rcm` and the certified optima, not\n1187\ton the mathematics. Note how the upper end goes slack with size: tight on\n1188\t80% of the instances at ≤ 10 customers and on 2 of 90 above 75 — reverse\n1189\tCuthill–McKee is one layout, and `bandwidth ≥ pathwidth` is loose on sparse\n1190\tgraphs where a good layout for stretch is a poor one for separation.\n1191\t### (e) Stated with `sorry`: the gap list\n1192\tEach carries a docstring naming the missing step; `learning.sandwich --stage lean`\n1193\tpins that they still use `sorryAx`, so promoting one to a proof will fail that\n1194\tcheck until its row is moved.\n1195\t*(A fourth, `orderingDegeneracy_le_degeneracy`, was on this list for the\n1196\tfirst twenty minutes of the session and was then proved; see (b).)*\n1197\t1. **`treewidth_le_pathwidth`** (item 09's theorem 1, `optimum ≥ tw + 1`\n1198\t   through `optimum = pw + 1`). `TreeDecomposition` and `treewidth` are\n1199\t   defined. *Gap:* a `PathDecomposition` on `Fin (length + 1)` is a\n1200\t   `TreeDecomposition` on `SimpleGraph.pathGraph (length + 1)`; this needs\n1201\t   (i) `(pathGraph n).IsTree` — Mathlib has `pathGraph_connected` but no\n1202\t   acyclicity or tree lemma for `pathGraph` (checked in the vendored\n1203\t   Mathlib), so either `isTree_iff_connected_and_card` with an edge count\n1204\t   `n − 1`, or acyclicity from the Hasse-diagram structure; (ii) the induced\n1205\t   subgraph of `pathGraph` on an interval `{i | v ∈ bag i}` (an interval by\n1206\t   the `interval` field) is connected — an induction on the interval's\n1207\t   length. The width of the transported decomposition equals `P.width` by\n1208\t   `Finset.sup' = Finset.sup` on `univ`. Estimated at 2–3 hours.\n1209\t2. **`branch_lemma`** (item 09's theorem 2). Stated as: `v`, three pairwise\n1210\t   disjoint sets `A B D` not containing `v`, no edges between different sets,\n1211\t   each inducing treewidth `≥ k` (`G.induce (↑A : Set V)`), then\n1212\t   `k + 1 ≤ pathwidth G`. *Gap:* the interval argument — in a path\n1213\t   decomposition of width `k` each branch has a bag it fills to `k + 1`\n1214\t   (needs `treewidth ≤` the width of the restriction of a path decomposition\n1215\t   to a branch, i.e. item 2's transport plus restriction), the bags of the\n1216\t   three branches are pairwise disjoint intervals of the path, and the\n1217\t   middle branch's full bag must also contain `v` (the branch is connected\n1218\t   to `v` and the other two branches lie on both sides), so it has size\n1219\t   `k + 2`. Not started; depends on item 1.\n1220\t3. **`conjecture_sqrt_tw_f6`** (item 09's surviving fitted candidate):\n1221\t   `⌊0.9428 · √(treewidth G · minClosedNeighborhood G 6)⌋₊ ≤ pathwidth G + 1`\n1222\t   for `6 ≤ card V`, stated with the optimum read as `pathwidth + 1` and\n1223\t   over `ℝ` with `Nat.floor`. §24 labels it worthless — pointwise at most\n1224\t   `max(lb_best, tw + 1)` on every certified instance — so nothing in this\n1225\t   development should be spent on it; it is here because the deliverable asks\n1226\t   for survivors to be stated. No proof is attempted or expected.\n1227\tAlso inherited: `MOSPInstance.mospValue_le_bandwidth_add_one` depends on\n1228\t`mosp_le_pathwidth_add_one`'s Hall-theorem `sorry` in `Reduction.lean`, which\n1229\tpredates this loop; the graph-level theorems do not.\n1230\t### (f) What the proofs do and do not cover\n1231\tThey cover every finite simple graph, so every MOSP graph the corpus or the\n1232\tcampaign can produce, at any size; the corpus tables are for 9–134 customers\n1233\tand the brute force for 1–5 vertices. They say nothing about `optimum`\n1234\texcept through the hypothesis `mospValue = pathwidth + 1`: the development\n1235\thas `mospValue ≤ pathwidth + 1` for reduced instances (with a `sorry`) and no\n1236\t`≥`, so Yanasse's equality is still the trusted step between the corpus\n1237\tsandwich and these theorems, exactly as it is for the contraction and\n1238\texpansion components of `_lower_bound`. The `decide` examples cover the\n1239\tdefinitions' *values* on four graphs; the Python brute force covers them on\n1240\tall 1,099 labelled graphs to five vertices against independent\n1241\timplementations (networkx core number, the exact pathwidth DP). The\n1242\t`treewidth` definition is checked by nothing yet (no `decide`, no Python\n1243\tcounterpart run against it) and should be read as a candidate definition\n1244\tuntil item 1 of the gap list uses it.\n1245\t**Finding, in one paragraph.** Both ends of the corpus sandwich are now\n1246\ttheorems in the repository's Lean development: `degeneracy ≤ pathwidth` and\n1247\t`pathwidth ≤ bandwidth` for every finite simple graph, each proved through\n1248\tvertex separation and carried to pathwidth by the existing Kinnersley\n1249\ttheorem, with no `sorry` and the standard three axioms; the layout-level\n1250\tform `pathwidth ≤ bandwidthOfLayout σ` is literally the `bw_rcm` inequality\n1251\t§5 checked. The proofs are short — the upper half is a window-counting\n1252\targument (an active vertex is within `b` of a prefix neighbour), the lower\n1253\thalf is that every later neighbour is active at its vertex's own position\n1254\tand that the first vertex of any subset has all its subset-neighbours after\n1255\tit — and the definitions are pinned to the corpus's implementations on every\n1256\tgraph to five vertices and to the kernel on four hand-checked graphs. Item\n1257\t09's two theorems and its conjecture are stated over a new `TreeDecomposition`\n1258\tstructure and stand with `sorry` behind a three-item gap list, the load-bearing\n1259\tgap being that Mathlib has no `pathGraph.IsTree`; the two degeneracy forms,\n1260\tLick–White's and the elimination ordering's, are proved equal by the greedy\n1261\tordering, an induction on the vertex count across types. `lake build`\n1262\tpasses (1,638 jobs).\n1263\t**Size range covered.** The theorems: every finite simple graph. The\n1264\tcorpus recount: 6,376 certified instances at 9–134 customers (`instances.csv`).\n1265\tThe brute-force definition check: all labelled graphs on 1–5 vertices;\n1266\tthe kernel examples: 3 and 7 vertices.\n1267\t**Kill criterion.** None stated by the plan. Deliverable as asked: two\n1268\ttheorems (plus the chain to pathwidth and the sandwich statement), and for\n1269\tthe stated items a gap list with the missing lemmas named.\n1270\t**Method notes.** *`#print axioms` is the test.* A `sorry` in a lemma three\n1271\tfiles away is invisible in a `lake build` that \"succeeds with warnings\";\n1272\t`learning.sandwich --stage lean` reads the axiom list of every named theorem\n1273\tand fails on `sorryAx` in a proved one — and on its *absence* in a stated\n1274\tone, so a proof that lands must be promoted in `PROVED`/`STATED` to pass.\n1275\tLean wraps long axiom lists across lines: match with `re.DOTALL`. *Kernel\n1276\t`decide` over `Finset (Fin 7)`* (128 subsets) exceeds the default\n1277\t`maxRecDepth`; 10,000 suffices and the check takes under a second. *A doc\n1278\tcomment cannot precede `set_option … in example`* (parse error: expected\n1279\t`lemma`); use a line comment. *`G.loopless` is `Std.Irrefl G.Adj`* in this\n1280\tMathlib, not a function; `G.ne_of_adj h rfl` is the irreflexivity step.\n1281\t*`Mathlib.Data.Real.Sqrt` is deprecated* in favour of `Mathlib.Analysis.Real.Sqrt`.\n1282\t*The lake cache is the whole budget*: the incremental build is 2 s; a\n1283\tMathlib rebuild would be the session.\n1284\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"85602c8d-df04-47a8-a103-12762ff7e492","uuid":"4bb33879-9d25-4018-ae07-124c14c8ae48","timestamp":"2026-09-26T19:23:36.925Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/85602c8d-df04-47a8-a103-12762ff7e492/tool-results/boxjyqqy3.txt","content":"*tight* on 40.6% of them and beats `lb_best` on 92.5%. Above 60 customers the\none-second search settles nothing and the row is unchanged. Among the 209\ngap instances with exact treewidth, pathwidth strictly exceeds it on 142\n(68%) — consistent with §21 (c)'s 164 of 338 under a 60-second budget, of\nwhich this is a subset.\n### (b) The mined candidates: 47 survivors, 36 of them treewidth in disguise\n11,250 of the 11,589 candidates are valid (every fitted one by construction;\n12 of the 351 raw ones, among them `tw + 1`, `ω`, the expansion bound, the\nbranch lemma, `degeneracy`, `deg_min` and the block–cut and clique-tree\npathwidths). **47 exceed `lb_best` on more than 5% of the gap instances.**\nSorted by `exceed_gap`, the first sixteen are `tw_lo + 1`, `tw1`, and `tw1`\ndivided by things that are 1 on connected graphs (`components`, `blt1`) —\nthe theorem restated fourteen ways, all at 58.0–58.9%. Then come the fitted\nones:\nThe leading fitted survivor — treewidth times the sixth expansion level,\nscaled — beats `tw + 1` on 19% of the gap instances, yet **never** beats\n`max(lb_best, tw + 1)` on any of the 50,948 instances where it is defined: it\nis a lower envelope of two proved bounds, fitted. **Of the 47 survivors, 36\nare pointwise ≤ tw + 1 on the gap instances and 11 are novel; two of the 11\nare clean on hold-out.** Over the whole table, 77% of the fitted valid\ncandidates (8,668 of 11,238) put at least one held-out instance above its\noptimum when their constant is re-chosen on four fifths of the groups: a\nconstant fitted on 51,000 instances is not a conjecture, it is the maximum\nof a sample.\n### (c) Where novelty lives: only where treewidth is unresolved\nEvery gap instance on which any of the five beats the proved reference has\n60 or more customers and an unresolved treewidth interval. Over all 3,689\nnovel candidates: 14,850 beat-rows in total, 5,215 of them on the 44,578\nrows with exact treewidth, spread over 1,161 candidates, **none of which\nbeats the proved bound on more than 0.1% of the exact rows** (maximum 28\nrows, median 2 among those that beat it at all) — the signature of a\nconstant scraping the maximum of a sample, not of a bound. Three of the five\nnever claim more than min-fill treewidth + 1 on the rows where they are\nnovel, so what they \"know\" is that the true treewidth of a dense 100-customer\ngraph is above MMD+; that is a statement about a heuristic, not about\npathwidth. Only `0.1472 · sqrt(n_cliques) · contraction` claims above\n`tw_hi + 1` on more than a single row (15), and it leaks on hold-out twice.\n### (d) The adversary: nothing broke, and that means little\nAll fifteen candidates of the n ≤ 15 attack set (the `tw1` control, the\nfourteen leading attackable distinct survivors, and the two that beat the\nproved reference on more than 5% of the gap instances) **survived 19,200\nexact-solver evaluations each**; 528 of the 720 jobs reached an instance\nwhere the candidate equals the optimum (violation 0), and none exceeded it.\nThe two novel ones never even reached the optimum (best −1): at n ≤ 15 their\nfitted constants make them slack by construction. The supplementary run at\nn ∈ {20, 25, 30} (ten candidates, 900 evaluations each, oracle 0.1–0.6 s\nper call) also found nothing: the treewidth-times-expansion candidate and\nthe clique-tree ones touch the optimum (0) and the three additive candidates\nsit 14–18 below it, as their constants of −13.8 to −30.8 dictate. **No\ncounterexample was drawn because none was found**; the counterexample files\nare committed empty. The search is duplicate-bound at these sizes — 22.2\nmillion canonical repeats for 288,000 evaluations — so more steps would buy\nnothing; what would test the five novel candidates is an oracle at 75–125\ncustomers on dense graphs, which is the corpus itself, and there they are\nvalid by construction. Their adversarial record is therefore *vacuous*, not\n*clean*, and the section says so.\n### (e) A bug the \"must be 0\" column caught\nThe branch-lemma bound as first written counted every component of `G − v`\nas a branch at `v`, including components of `G` that never touched `v`. On a\nforest with a claw and three disjoint edges it took the third-largest\ntreewidth over six components (1) and claimed pathwidth ≥ 2 on an instance of\noptimum 2 — **347 rows above the optimum**, all by exactly one, all with\nseveral components (297 campaign, 48 upward, 2 corpus). The lemma is right;\nthe count was wrong. Fixed (`branching_invariants` keeps only the components\ncontaining a neighbour of `v`), with a regression test, and the 8,292\nmulti-component rows recomputed in place by the `repair` stage (6,778 rows\nchanged). After the fix the branch lemma is valid on all 50,949, tight on 388\n(0.8%), above `lb_best` on 28 rows (0.05%) and never on a gap instance: only\n10 of the 338 gap instances have a cut vertex with three branches at all, 99\nhave any cut vertex, and 31 have a block–cut tree of pathwidth ≥ 2. The\n\"trees of cliques with branching\" of §6 are branching in the sense of\n*separators*, not of *cut vertices*; the lemma sees the wrong kind.\n### The finding, in one paragraph\nThe family of two-invariant formulas over branching-aware invariants\nproduces **no conjecture**. Its 47 survivors are 36 restatements of the\ntheorem `optimum ≥ tw + 1`, two fitted lower envelopes of that theorem and\n`lb_best` that never beat their pair, and nine fitted constants that are\n\"novel\" only on 100–125-customer graphs where treewidth is an unresolved\ninterval — 77% of all fitted candidates leak on hold-out, none beats the\nproved bound on more than 0.1% of the instances where treewidth is exact,\nand the adversary cannot reach the sizes where they claim anything. The one\nobject the mining put on the table is the theorem it started from: **exact\ntreewidth**, which the solver's bound does not compute and which lifts the\ncertified lower bound on 933 corpus instances and 199 of the 338 gap\ninstances, to tightness on 67. The plan's kill criterion is met in the sense\nthat matters — *the bound work needs a new idea, not a new formula* — and\nthe new idea has to be a pathwidth argument that sees separators of trees of\ncliques, which none of the thirty invariants here does.\n**Kill criterion (plan 2 §2.6: no survivor exceeds `lb_best` on more than 5%\nof the gap instances while surviving the adversary).** *Literally not met*:\n`tw + 1` exceeds `lb_best` on 58.9% and survives, being a theorem, and the\nfitted `⌊0.9428 · sqrt(tw · f6)⌋` exceeds it on 32.3% and survives 20,100\nevaluations at 8–30 customers. *Met against the honest reference*: no\ncandidate exceeds `max(lb_best, tw + 1)` on more than 0.1% of the 44,578\ninstances with exact treewidth, none on any gap instance below 60\ncustomers, and the five above 5% on the gap instances do so only where\ntreewidth is unresolved. The section's verdict is the second.\n**Handed to item 12.** Two theorems and one conjecture, in the deliverable's\nform. (1) *Theorem:* `optimum ≥ tw(G) + 1` — via `optimum = pw + 1` and\n`tw ≤ pw`; tight on 77.9% of 50,949 certified instances at 9–134 customers,\non 19.8% of the gap instances; beats `lb_best` on 14.8% / 58.9%. (2)\n*Theorem:* if a cut vertex has three branches of treewidth ≥ k then\n`pw ≥ k + 1` (the branch lemma; the proof is the interval argument on a path\ndecomposition of width k, in which each branch owns a full bag and the\nmiddle one separates the other two from `v`); valid on all 50,949, tight on\n0.8%, and it never exceeds `lb_best` on a gap instance, so its value is as a\nstatement, not a bound. (3) *Conjecture, stated because the deliverable asks\nfor survivors and labelled worthless:* `optimum ≥ ⌊0.9428 · sqrt(tw(G) · f(6))⌋`\nwhere `f(6) = min_{|C| = 6} |N[C]|` — valid on 50,948 certified instances,\nclean on grouped hold-out, undefeated by 20,100 adversarial evaluations at\n8–30 customers, and pointwise ≤ `max(lb_best, tw + 1)` on every one of them,\nso proving it would prove nothing new. No counterexample to draw.\n**Proposed, not applied.** Exact/interval treewidth as a component of\n`_lower_bound`: the subset DP to 26 customers and the decision search to 64\nunder a one-second deadline cost 0.2–1 s per instance (39 s at worst over\nthe 50,949) and raise the certified floor on 933 corpus instances, making 67\ngap instances bound-certified with no refutation. It rests on Yanasse's\nequality like the contraction component. Against §6's ceiling it changes\nnothing where `pw > tw`, which is 68% of the exact gap instances, so it is a\nbetter floor, not a different kind of one. The owner's change; nothing here\ntouches the solver.\n**Size range covered.** Invariants and validity: 9–134 customers, all\n50,949 certified instances (corpus 6,376; campaign 37,800 at 10–40; upward\n6,773 at 50–100). Exact treewidth: to 26 by DP (25,731 rows) and to 64 by a\none-second decision search (18,680 of 22,825 rows settled); above 64 nothing\nis exact (2,393 rows, heuristics only), and every statement about \"where\ntreewidth is exact\" is a statement about n ≤ 64. The adversary: 8–15 (plan)\nand 20–30 (supplementary); it cannot test a candidate whose fitted constant\nmakes it vacuous below 30, and says so per candidate (`attackable`). The\nfive novel candidates are supported only at 60–125 customers on the dense\nChu & Stuckey and upward `m = n/2` cells.\n**Not a bound, not a solver change.** No candidate reaches\n`satisfiability/mosp_solver.py::_lower_bound` or any path that decides `k`;\n`learning.treewidth` is called by the study only. The one number that could\nmove the solver — exact treewidth beats `lb_best` on 933 corpus instances —\nis a proposal above.\n**Method notes.** The \"above optimum (must be 0)\" column is the cheapest\ntheorem-checker in the repository and found a real bug in a lemma\nimplementation on the first run: keep it in every table that lists proved\nbounds. A constant fitted as the extreme of 51,000 values leaks on hold-out\n77% of the time; the plan's \"valid on all certified instances\" is necessary\nand nowhere near sufficient, and an honest reference (`max` of the proved\nbounds) separates theorems-in-disguise from candidates before the adversary\nis spent on them. The adversary at n ≤ 15 exhausts its neighbourhoods —\n77 duplicates per evaluation — so the budget should go to sizes, not steps;\nat n = 30 the oracle (`solve_mosp_exact` plus exact treewidth plus the\nexpansion profile) still costs under a second. `kill $!` after\n`nohup python -m …` killed a wrapper and left a 16-worker pool running for\ntwo minutes: kill the pool by its parent PID (`pgrep -P`) and check with\n`ps` that the tree is gone. `_generated_matrix` takes an `itertuples` row,\nnot a `Series` (whose `.index` is the Index).\n## 25. Why the ridge is where it is: one parameter across m / n (plan 2 §2.9, loop0003 item 11)\n*Iteration 13 of loop0003, 2026-09-26. Code: `learning/ridge_theory.py`. Regenerate:*\n```bash\npython -m learning.ridge_theory --stage run --workers 16      # the deciding cells: m = n/4 at n = 50, 60, 75 and m = n/8 at 75; 2,750 instances, 82 s wall, 0.36 core-hours\npython -m learning.ridge_theory --stage cliques --workers 16  # maximal-clique excess on the 7,125 instances at n >= 40 with MOSP-graph density <= 0.35; ~2 min\npython -m learning.ridge_theory --stage tables                # reports/ridge_theory_tables.md + reports/figures/ridge_theory.png, 2 s\npython -m pytest tests/test_ridge_theory.py -q                # 9 tests\n```\n*Reads `learning/data/ensemble/results.csv` (§10, 37,800 at 10–40),\n`results_upward.csv` with its finish stage (§16, 6,773 certified at 50–100)\nand the new `results_ratio.csv` (2,750 at 50–75, all certified, every witness\nre-simulating, zero censored refutations; manifest `manifest_ratio.csv`, 300\nsampled and regenerated byte for byte; witnesses under\n`learning/data/ensemble/solutions/`; the ensemble directory is 82 MB\napparent). Writes `reports/ridge_theory_tables.md` (every table in full),\n`learning/data/ensemble/ridge_clique_excess.csv` and two run logs. Nothing\nunder `solutions/`, no solver default, no bound.*\n**Question.** §11 put the hardness ridge — the density at which the nodes to\nrefute `optimum − 1` peak at fixed `n` — at three customers per product when\n`m = n` and two when `m = 2n`; §16 found it at five to six when `m = n / 2`.\nThe plan's hypothesis was that both §11 locations are one MOSP-graph mean\ndegree, 8–9. Is there a parameter of `G(n, m, p)` at which the ridge sits for\nevery `m / n`, and if so which? What does the literature's transition for\nsparse random graphs (giant component; linear treewidth and pathwidth) have to\ndo with it?\n**Method.** (a) *Analytics in `col_mean` coordinates* (`c` = realised customers\nper product, `r = m / n`): the expected degree of the MOSP graph — fixed\ngenerator `(n − 1)[1 − (1 − c(c − 1) / (n(n − 1)))^m]`, Bernoulli\n`(n − 1)[1 − (1 − (c/n)²)^m]`; the edge probability; the branching factor\n`r c (c − 1)` (fixed) or `r c²` (Bernoulli), which is the expected number of new\ncustomers reached through one customer's other products and whose crossing of\n1 is the giant-component threshold of the random hypergraph and hence of its\n2-section (Schmidt-Pruzan & Shamir 1985; Karoński & Łuczak 2002); and the\n**excess** `(n_ones − m) / n = Σ_j (|C_j| − 1) / n = r (c − 1)`, the mass of the\nproduct cliques over a spanning tree of cliques per customer — at 1 the\ncustomer–product incidence graph has as many edges as vertices, and\n`excess − 1` is its cyclomatic number per customer. The degree derivation is\nchecked against the measured `g_deg_mean` in every cell. (b) *Peaks*: for every\n(generator, `m / n`, `n ≥ 30`) the cell with the largest censoring-aware median\nof `log10(1 + nodes)` (`default` configuration), the vertex of the parabola in\n`log10 col_mean` through it and its two neighbours when the peak is interior,\nand a bootstrap interval (500 resamples of instances within those cells).\n(c) *Constancy*: each candidate's value at the peak (its cell medians\ninterpolated to the vertex), and the coefficient of variation across the\nratios at fixed (generator, `n`) for `n ≥ 50`, where three to five ratios\nexist. (d) *Calibrated prediction*: every hypothesis \"the ridge is where\ncandidate `X` equals its `m = n` value\" is calibrated on the `m = n` peaks at\n50–75 of its own generator and predicts the other ratios' peak `col_mean` —\nby inversion for the analytic candidates, by interpolating `X`'s cell medians\nalong the series for the empirical ones — scored by `|log10(predicted /\nmeasured)|` and by the bootstrap interval. (e) *The deciding cells*: the\ncampaign had `m ∈ {n, 2n}` and §16 added `n / 2`; at `m = n / 4` the degree\nand excess hypotheses are 0.17 decades apart and at `m = n / 8` 0.37, so this\nitem generated fixed `d ∈ {3, …, 10, 12}` and Bernoulli `p ∈ {0.05, …, 0.2}`\nat `m = n / 4` for `n ∈ {50, 60, 75}` (50 per cell) and fixed `d ∈ {4, 6, …,\n20, 24}` at `m = n / 8`, `n = 75`. Also: §11's collapse test with the series\nkeyed by (generator, ratio) at 50–75; the ridge's *height* against `m` at\nfixed `n`; the instances at 30–40 customers binned by branching factor, to see\nwhere the giant component and the linear regime of the optimum begin relative\nto the ridge; and a graph-side excess, `Σ over maximal cliques (|K| − 1) / n`,\nto test whether the incidence statement is also a graph statement.\n**Baseline.** \"Three customers per product\" (§11), and the plan's \"mean\ndegree 8–9\".\n### (a) The derivation holds; the plan's premise does not\nPredicted against measured mean degree, cells at `n ≥ 40`: median relative\nerror within ±0.5% at `m ∈ {n, 2n}`, −1% to −6% at `m ≤ n / 2` (the fixed\ngenerator's empty-row repair adds edges the formula omits); the worst cells\n(+39% and +66%) are the two sparsest Bernoulli ones, where `p n < 1` and the\nrepairs dominate. In `col_mean` coordinates the giant threshold is at\n`c = (1 + √(1 + 4 / r)) / 2` (fixed) or `1 / √r` (Bernoulli): 1.37 / 1.62 / 2.0\n/ 2.6 / 3.4 for `r` = 2 / 1 / ½ / ¼ / ⅛ on the fixed grid. The ridge is\n**not** there: it sits 1.5× (`m = 2n`) to 6× (`m = n / 8`) above it, at\nbranching factors 4 to 49 — the ratio to the threshold is not constant either.\nAnd the mean degree at the peak is **3.9 / 7.4–8.4 / 10.5–12.0 / 15.8–21.4 /\n37.6** for `m = 2n / n / n/2 / n/4 / n/8` (fixed generator, 50–75), a factor\nof 9.5 across the ratios; the \"8–9\" of the plan is the Bernoulli `m ∈ {n, 2n}`\ncoincidence at `n ≤ 40` (6.7–8.4), which the fixed generator at the same\nsizes already breaks (3.8 against 6.4–6.9).\n### (b) The peaks, interpolated (n = 50–75; the `m = 2n` fixed peak is at its grid edge, d = 2, a lower bound on its location)\nThe `col_mean` of the ridge runs from 2.0 to 20.7 — a factor of ten — as `m`\nfalls from `2n` to `n / 8`, and the products per customer at the ridge run\n4.0 / 3.3–3.5 / 2.6–2.8 / 2.2–2.7 / 2.5 (fixed): neither matrix coordinate is\nconstant, as §16 (b) said. The **excess** at the ridge is 2.0–2.5 in every\nfixed series and 1.8–3.0 in every Bernoulli series, at every `n`; it is the\nonly analytic quantity that stays within a factor of 1.65 over the five\nratios (§25 (c)). The `m = 2n` fixed peaks sit at the grid edge with excess\nexactly 2.0 — the excess-2 line is the edge — and the Bernoulli `m = 2n`\nseries, whose grid does go sparser, has an interior peak at excess 2.1–3.0.\n### (c) Constancy across m / n at fixed (generator, n), n = 50–75 (six (generator, n) pairs with three to five ratios each)\n### (d) Calibrated on the m = n ridge, predicted at the other ratios (19 series; the 13 at m ≤ n / 2 are the deciding ones)\n**The `m = n / 2` cells decide against the mean degree**: calibrated at its\n`m = n` value it predicts the half-ratio ridge at 4.7 customers per product\n(fixed) and 4.8–4.9 (Bernoulli); the measured peaks are 5.24–5.64 (fixed, every\ninterval excluding 4.7) and 4.87–5.44 (Bernoulli, inside the interval at 50\nand 60, outside at 75). The excess predicts 5.86–5.92 — inside the interval\nat `n = 50` and 60 and 0.05 decades above the peak (0.03 above its interval) at 75 (fixed), 0.04–0.08 above\n(Bernoulli). At `m = n / 4` the gap opens: degree says 6.4–6.5 and 6.9–7.1,\nthe peaks are at 9.2–10.6 and 8.2–8.4; the excess says 10.7–11.1, inside the\nfixed interval at `n = 60` and 0.06–0.08 decades above it at 50 and 75, and\n0.12 decades above the Bernoulli peaks (whose excess is 1.8, not 2.4). At\n`m = n / 8` only the excess survives: 21.2 against a measured 20.7 [18.7,\n21.6], where the degree says 8.9, `optimum / n` 13.8 and treewidth 15.1.\nThe two empirical invariants — `optimum / n` and `tw_min_fill / n`, both\n≈ 0.32 at the ridge — predict the `n / 2` and `n / 4` peaks as well as the\nexcess or better, and fail by 0.14–0.18 decades at `n / 8`; they are also\n*outputs* of the instance rather than parameters of the generator, and §14\nshowed `optimum / n` is not the ridge's size-stable coordinate (0.16–0.20 on\nthe 125 × 125 ridge class against 0.28–0.43 at `n ≤ 40`). The excess is\nsize-free by construction, and the fixed `m = n` ridge has sat at `d = 3` —\nexcess 2 — at every `n` from 15 to 75 (§11, §16).\n### (e) What the excess is, and what it is not\n`(n_ones − m) / n` is `Σ_j (|C_j| − 1) / n`: each product, as a clique, needs\n`|C_j| − 1` edges to be spanned, so the excess is the number of edges in a\nspanning forest of the union of product cliques *if the products were glued\ninto a tree of cliques*, per customer. At excess 1 (`n_ones = n + m`) the\ncustomer–product incidence graph has as many edges as vertices — the\nhypergraph is at the edge of Berge-acyclicity; every unit above 1 is one\nindependent cycle of the incidence graph per customer. The ridge at excess\n≈ 2–2.4 is therefore \"one independent cycle per customer\", or \"the product\ncliques carry twice the mass of a spanning tree of cliques\". In `col_mean`\ncoordinates: **the ridge is at `col_mean ≈ 1 + 2.4 · n / m`** (fixed\ngenerator; `1 + 2 n / m` puts it at 2 / 3 / 5 / 9 / 17 for the five ratios\nagainst measured 2.0 / 3.3–3.5 / 5.2–5.6 / 9.2–10.6 / 20.7).\nIt is **an incidence-graph parameter, not a MOSP-graph invariant**. §13 showed\nhardness is a property of the labelled graph and of nothing else, so the\nstatement must be read as: for these generators, the graph the search sees is\nhardest to refute when its *generating cover* has excess about 2. The\ngraph-side analogue — the sum over maximal cliques of `|K| − 1`, per customer\n— equals the matrix excess where the products are the maximal cliques (ratio\n1.00 at the median below MOSP-graph density 0.1, 2,130 instances) but not at\nthe ridge: there, maximal cliques already outnumber products (0.8–0.85× at\nthe `m = 2n` ridge, 1.3× at `m = n`, 2.6–5× at `n / 2`, 7–14× at `n / 4`;\ncross-product triangles), and the maximal-clique excess at the peaks runs\n1.8–11.8 (CV 0.33, worse than `optimum / n`), with the `n / 8` ridge past the\n20,000-clique cap. No MOSP-graph invariant among the fourteen tested is\nconstant at the ridge across `m / n` to better than a factor of 1.9\n(bandwidth / n, 1.94); the two that come closest (`bw_rcm / n`, `optimum / n`)\nare width measures, i.e. the answer itself.\n### (f) The literature's transition is not the ridge\nInstances at 30–40 customers (`m ∈ {n, 2n}`, 16,200) binned by branching\nfactor: below 2 the largest component holds 13–53% of the customers, 88–100%\nof instances decompose and `optimum / n` is 0.10–0.13; from 4 up every\ninstance is connected and `optimum / n` is 0.26 / 0.46 / 0.65 / 0.91 in the\nbins 4–8 / 8–16 / 16–32 / ≥ 32. Median nodes: 19 / 72 / **898 / 961** / 226 /\n5. So the giant component (threshold 1 in the limit; realised at 2–4 at these\nsizes because of the repairs and finite `n`) is where the optimum becomes\nlinear in `n` — the regime the literature describes, treewidth and pathwidth\nboth `Θ(n)` above the giant threshold of `G(n, c/n)` (Lee, Lee & Oum 2012; Gao\n2012, whose result covers random intersection graphs; Kloks 1994 for `c >\n2.36`), against `O(log n)` pathwidth below it where every component is a tree\nor unicyclic — and the ridge sits an order of magnitude further in, at\nbranching factors 4–49 (excess 2), well inside the linear regime. The\nliterature's transition says where the problem stops being trivially small;\nthe ridge is where, among linear-width instances, the refutation is most\nexpensive, and that is a property of the cover's excess, not of the\nconnectivity threshold.\n### (g) The ridge's height scales with m, steeply\nAt the interpolated peak, median log10 nodes against `m` at fixed `n` (fixed\ngenerator): `n = 75`: 2.14 (m = 9) / 3.73 (18) / 5.46 (37) / 6.78 (75) /\n≥ 8.12 (150); slope of log10 height in log10 `m` **2.9 at n = 50, 3.4 at 60,\n4.9 at 75** (Bernoulli 2.0 / 2.2 / 3.5) — one to one and a half decades per\ndoubling of the products at fixed customers, and steepening with `n`. So no\nsingle parameter *collapses* the surface: §11's collapse test over the eight\nto nine (generator, ratio) series at 50–75 gives dispersion 2.0–2.4 decades\nand r² 0.38–0.56 against a cell ceiling of 0.94–0.97 for every candidate,\nbecause the series differ in height by four decades at the same location.\nThe excess locates the ridge; the number of products sets its height.\n**Finding, in one paragraph.** The ridge is not a mean-degree condition: the\nMOSP-graph mean degree at the peak runs from 3.9 at `m = 2n` to 37.6 at\n`m = n / 8` (a factor of 9.5), and the hypothesis calibrated at `m = n`\npredicts the `m = n / 2` peak at 4.7 customers per product against a measured\n5.2–5.6 whose intervals all exclude it, the `n / 4` peak at 6.4 against 9.2–10.6,\nand the `n / 8` peak at 8.9 against 20.7. The parameter that is the same\nacross `m / n` is the **excess of the product cover**, `(n_ones − m) / n =\nΣ_j (|C_j| − 1) / n`, equal to 2.0–2.5 at every fixed-generator peak from\n`m = 2n` to `m = n / 8` and 1.8–3.0 at every Bernoulli peak (n = 50–75; the\nbest constancy among fifteen candidates, CV 0.13, and the only one within a\nfactor of 1.65 across ratios); calibrated at `m = n` it predicts the other\nridges to 0.055 decades on average and the deciding `n / 8` ridge to 0.012.\nIn `col_mean` coordinates the ridge is at about `1 + 2.4 n / m` customers per\nproduct — 2 / 3 / 5–6 / 9–11 / 21 — which is also why it is at \"three\" for\n`m = n`. It is a condition on the incidence graph (one independent cycle per\ncustomer), not on a MOSP-graph invariant: the maximal-clique version of the\nsame sum is not constant (1.8–11.8) and no graph invariant tested is within a\nfactor of 1.9. The giant-component threshold of the literature (branching\nfactor 1, the onset of linear width) lies 1.5–6× below the ridge in `col_mean`\nand is where the optimum becomes linear, not where the search is hardest; the\nridge's *height* is set by `m` (1–1.5 decades per doubling at fixed `n`),\nwhich is why the `m = 2n` series is the hardest at every size.\n**Size range covered.** Peaks and predictions at `n ∈ {50, 60, 75}`, `m / n\n∈ {⅛ (n = 75 only, fixed only), ¼, ½, 1, 2}`, both generators, 50–150\ninstances per cell, 9,497 certified instances in 190 cells; the `n ≤ 40`\npeaks (`m ∈ {n, 2n}`) from the §10 campaign confirm the excess at 2.0–2.6 at\n30–40. The branching-factor bins cover 30–40. Nothing here is evidence about\n125 × 125 except through §14/§16: at `Random-125-125-2` (`col_mean` 2.76,\n`m = n`) the excess is 1.76 and at density 4 (4.2) it is 3.2, so the excess\nplaces the 125 × 125 ridge at `col_mean ≈ 3`, between Chu & Stuckey's two\nday-long classes and nearer density 2 — consistent with density 2 being 2–6×\nharder than density 4 at every size (§14), and a prediction, not a\nmeasurement.\n**Kill criterion.** None stated by the plan. Deliverable as asked: the ridge\nas a condition on one parameter across `m / n` — the excess of the cover,\n`(n_ones − m) / n ≈ 2`–2.4 — decided by the `m = n / 2` cells against the mean\ndegree and confirmed at `m = n / 4` and `m = n / 8`, with the qualification\nthat the parameter is an incidence-graph quantity and that no MOSP-graph\ninvariant does the same job.\n**Method notes.** *The grid's coarseness sets the resolution.* Cell-wise peaks\nat the Bernoulli `m = 2n` series jumped between `col_mean` 1.9 and 3.0 as `p`\nstepped 0.025 → 0.05; the parabolic vertex through three cells, with the\nbootstrap over instances within them, is what makes a 0.05-decade comparison\npossible, and the bootstrap interval is a within-cell interval only (it does\nnot see grid placement), so the \"within interval\" counts understate agreement\nand the log error is the primary score. *Kill a pool by its parent.* The\nmaximal-clique stage was first run on every instance; two near-complete\nBernoulli graphs at `n = 75` have exponentially many maximal cliques and two\nworkers ran while fourteen slept — the stage now takes a density cutoff and a\n20,000-clique cap (past it both values are NaN), and the stuck run was killed\nby `pgrep -P <parent>`. *Instance files.* `ensemble.run` writes `.mosp` files\nunless told not to; the manifest of seeds is the artifact, and the 2,750\nfiles were removed after the manifest verified. *The `m = n // 2` ratio at\n`n = 75` is 0.493*: ratios are snapped to the nearest of ⅛, ¼, ½, 1, 2 for\ngrouping and kept exact for every formula.\n## 26. The sandwich in Lean: `degeneracy ≤ pathwidth ≤ bandwidth` as theorems (plan 2 §2.10, loop0003 item 12)\n*Iteration 14 of loop0003, 2026-09-26. Code: `lean/MOSPFormalization/Sandwich.lean`\n(578 lines, 32 theorems, 3 `sorry`), `learning/sandwich.py`. Regenerate:*\n```bash\ncd lean && lake build                       # 1,638 jobs; ~2 s from the cache, the Mathlib build otherwise\npython -m learning.sandwich                 # brute force to n = 5, corpus recount, lake build + #print axioms; 5 s\npython -m pytest tests/test_sandwich.py -q  # 7 tests\n```\n*Writes `reports/sandwich_tables.md` (every table below). Reads\n`learning/data/instances.csv` for the corpus recount. Nothing here is a bound\nthe solver uses, a solver change, or a write to `solutions/`.*\n**The question.** §5 found `degeneracy + 1 ≤ optimum ≤ bw_rcm + 1` on all\n6,376 corpus instances and called the two ends \"two theorems, checked\". The\nLean development already had linear layouts, vertex separation, path\ndecompositions and Kinnersley's `vertexSeparation = pathwidth`\n(`VSEquivPW.lean`). Can the two ends be *proved* there, over those\ndefinitions, so that the checked sandwich rests on something other than\n44,000 instances? Item 09 (§24) added two theorems and one conjecture to\nstate.\n### (a) What was defined\nAll over the repository's `LinearLayout V := V ≃ Fin (Fintype.card V)` and\nMathlib's `SimpleGraph V` with `[Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj]`,\nin `namespace MOSPFormalization`:\nThe two degeneracy forms are both there because the corpus computes the\nLick–White form and the proof against vertex separation goes through the\nordering form; they are proved equal (`orderingDegeneracy_eq_degeneracy`),\nso either may be read as *the* degeneracy.\n### (b) What was proved (no `sorry`, standard axioms only)\n`#print axioms` on each of the thirteen theorems named in `learning.sandwich.PROVED`\ngives `[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]` (`reports/sandwich_tables.md`,\nlast table; `learning.sandwich --stage lean` fails if `sorryAx` ever appears on\none of them or disappears from a stated one).\n- **Upper half.** `vertexSepAt_le_bandwidthOfLayout`: at cut `i` every active\n  suffix vertex `v` has a prefix neighbour `u`, so `i < σ v ≤ σ u + b ≤ i + b`;\n  `σ` is injective, and the window `Ioc i (i + b)` has `b` positions, so\n  `card (activeSuffix) ≤ b` (`Finset.card_le_card_of_injOn` into `Finset.Ioc`,\n  `Nat.card_Ioc`). Then `vertexSepOfLayout_le_bandwidthOfLayout` (`sup'_le`),\n  `vertexSeparation_le_bandwidth` (`le_csInf` over the range, as\n  `pathwidth_le_vertexSeparation` does), and through `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth`:\n  **`pathwidth_le_bandwidth`** and its layout-level form\n  **`pathwidth_le_bandwidthOfLayout σ`** — the latter is the exact statement the\n  corpus's `bw_rcm + 1 ≥ optimum` checks, since `bw_rcm` is one layout's stretch.\n- **Lower half.** `laterNeighbors_subset_activeSuffix`: a neighbour placed after\n  `v` is in the suffix at `σ v` with `v` itself as its prefix neighbour, so\n  `card (laterNeighbors v) ≤ vertexSepAt (σ v) ≤ vertexSepOfLayout`\n  (`vertexSepAt_le_vertexSepOfLayout`, a lemma the development lacked), whence\n  `maxLaterDegree_le_vertexSepOfLayout` and **`orderingDegeneracy_le_vertexSeparation`**.\n  `degreeIn_le_card_laterNeighbors`: the *first* vertex of any `S` under `σ`\n  (`Finset.exists_min_image`) has all its `S`-neighbours after it (irreflexivity\n  and injectivity give strictness), so `minDegreeIn S ≤ maxLaterDegree σ` for\n  every `S` and every `σ`, i.e. **`degeneracy_le_orderingDegeneracy`**. Chained:\n  **`degeneracy_le_vertexSeparation`**, **`degeneracy_le_pathwidth`**.\n- **The sandwich.** `degeneracy_le_pathwidth_le_bandwidth : degeneracy G ≤ pathwidth G ∧ pathwidth G ≤ bandwidth G`,\n  for every finite simple graph.\n- **The two degeneracy forms coincide.** `orderingDegeneracy_le_degeneracy`, by\n  the greedy elimination ordering: `deleteVertex G v` is `G.comap Subtype.val`\n  on `{u // u ≠ v}`, and `degeneracy_deleteVertex_le` says deleting a vertex\n  never raises the Lick–White degeneracy (a subset of the subtype is a subset\n  of `V` with the same induced degrees, `Finset.filter_map` + `card_map`);\n  `exists_layout_maxLaterDegree_le_degeneracy` is an induction on\n  `Fintype.card V` across types (`∀ n {V : Type u} …`): pick `v` of minimum\n  degree (`Finset.exists_mem_eq_inf'` on `univ`), lay out `{u // u ≠ v}` by\n  the hypothesis, and put `v` at position 0 through\n  `(optionSubtypeNe v).symm ≫ optionCongr σ' ≫ (finSuccEquiv _).symm ≫ finCongr`;\n  `v`'s later neighbours are its whole neighbourhood, everyone else's are\n  unchanged. Hence **`orderingDegeneracy_eq_degeneracy`**.\n- **In MOSP terms.** `MOSPInstance.degeneracy_add_one_le_mospValue_of_eq`:\n  `degeneracy + 1 ≤ mospValue` for any instance with `mospValue = pathwidth + 1`\n  (Yanasse's equality, taken as a hypothesis because the development has only the\n  `≤` direction, under `IsReduced`, and that one rests on the Hall-theorem `sorry`\n  of `Reduction.lean`). `MOSPInstance.mospValue_le_bandwidth_add_one` is the\n  upper half in MOSP terms; it is proved here but *inherits* that `sorry`, so the\n  axiom table lists it as stated.\n### (c) Do the definitions compute the right thing?\nA theorem about a mis-stated definition proves nothing, so the definitions\nare checked two ways. In the kernel (`decide`, in the Lean file): on the path\n`P₃` degeneracy 1, `minDegreeIn univ` 1, later-degree and separation and\nstretch of the identity layout all 1; on `K₃` degeneracy 2 and stretch 2; on\nthe edgeless graph 0 and 0; on the **spider** (subdivided `K_{1,3}`, treewidth\n1, pathwidth 2) degeneracy 1 over all 128 subsets (`maxRecDepth 10000`),\nlater-degree 3 and separation 3 for the centre-first layout. In Python\n(`learning.sandwich`, a literal transcription of the Lean definitions by\nbrute force over subsets and permutations) on **every labelled graph on 1–5\nvertices, 1,099 graphs**:\nZero violations: the Lick–White form is networkx's core number on every\ngraph, the ordering form equals it on every graph (the proved\n`orderingDegeneracy_eq_degeneracy`, here a guard on the two *definitions*),\nand `vertexSeparation` as defined equals\n`fixed_parameter_algorithm.pathwidth.compute_pathwidth`. The first graphs\nwith `pw < bandwidth` are the four labellings of the claw `K_{1,3}`\n(degeneracy 1, pathwidth 1, bandwidth 2: the centre must sit within one\nposition of three leaves); the first with `degeneracy < pathwidth` appear at\n`n = 5` (30 of 1,024).\n### (d) The corpus recount\n`learning.sandwich --stage corpus`, from `learning/data/instances.csv`:\nThe 2,823 is §5's \"ends coincide and force the optimum on 2,823\", regenerated.\nWith (b), both columns of inequalities are now theorems about the graph\n(given `optimum = pathwidth + 1`), and the recount is a check on the\n*implementations* of `g_degeneracy`, `bw_rcm` and the certified optima, not\non the mathematics. Note how the upper end goes slack with size: tight on\n80% of the instances at ≤ 10 customers and on 2 of 90 above 75 — reverse\nCuthill–McKee is one layout, and `bandwidth ≥ pathwidth` is loose on sparse\ngraphs where a good layout for stretch is a poor one for separation.\n### (e) Stated with `sorry`: the gap list\nEach carries a docstring naming the missing step; `learning.sandwich --stage lean`\npins that they still use `sorryAx`, so promoting one to a proof will fail that\ncheck until its row is moved.\n*(A fourth, `orderingDegeneracy_le_degeneracy`, was on this list for the\nfirst twenty minutes of the session and was then proved; see (b).)*\n1. **`treewidth_le_pathwidth`** (item 09's theorem 1, `optimum ≥ tw + 1`\n   through `optimum = pw + 1`). `TreeDecomposition` and `treewidth` are\n   defined. *Gap:* a `PathDecomposition` on `Fin (length + 1)` is a\n   `TreeDecomposition` on `SimpleGraph.pathGraph (length + 1)`; this needs\n   (i) `(pathGraph n).IsTree` — Mathlib has `pathGraph_connected` but no\n   acyclicity or tree lemma for `pathGraph` (checked in the vendored\n   Mathlib), so either `isTree_iff_connected_and_card` with an edge count\n   `n − 1`, or acyclicity from the Hasse-diagram structure; (ii) the induced\n   subgraph of `pathGraph` on an interval `{i | v ∈ bag i}` (an interval by\n   the `interval` field) is connected — an induction on the interval's\n   length. The width of the transported decomposition equals `P.width` by\n   `Finset.sup' = Finset.sup` on `univ`. Estimated at 2–3 hours.\n2. **`branch_lemma`** (item 09's theorem 2). Stated as: `v`, three pairwise\n   disjoint sets `A B D` not containing `v`, no edges between different sets,\n   each inducing treewidth `≥ k` (`G.induce (↑A : Set V)`), then\n   `k + 1 ≤ pathwidth G`. *Gap:* the interval argument — in a path\n   decomposition of width `k` each branch has a bag it fills to `k + 1`\n   (needs `treewidth ≤` the width of the restriction of a path decomposition\n   to a branch, i.e. item 2's transport plus restriction), the bags of the\n   three branches are pairwise disjoint intervals of the path, and the\n   middle branch's full bag must also contain `v` (the branch is connected\n   to `v` and the other two branches lie on both sides), so it has size\n   `k + 2`. Not started; depends on item 1.\n3. **`conjecture_sqrt_tw_f6`** (item 09's surviving fitted candidate):\n   `⌊0.9428 · √(treewidth G · minClosedNeighborhood G 6)⌋₊ ≤ pathwidth G + 1`\n   for `6 ≤ card V`, stated with the optimum read as `pathwidth + 1` and\n   over `ℝ` with `Nat.floor`. §24 labels it worthless — pointwise at most\n   `max(lb_best, tw + 1)` on every certified instance — so nothing in this\n   development should be spent on it; it is here because the deliverable asks\n   for survivors to be stated. No proof is attempted or expected.\nAlso inherited: `MOSPInstance.mospValue_le_bandwidth_add_one` depends on\n`mosp_le_pathwidth_add_one`'s Hall-theorem `sorry` in `Reduction.lean`, which\npredates this loop; the graph-level theorems do not.\n### (f) What the proofs do and do not cover\nThey cover every finite simple graph, so every MOSP graph the corpus or the\ncampaign can produce, at any size; the corpus tables are for 9–134 customers\nand the brute force for 1–5 vertices. They say nothing about `optimum`\nexcept through the hypothesis `mospValue = pathwidth + 1`: the development\nhas `mospValue ≤ pathwidth + 1` for reduced instances (with a `sorry`) and no\n`≥`, so Yanasse's equality is still the trusted step between the corpus\nsandwich and these theorems, exactly as it is for the contraction and\nexpansion components of `_lower_bound`. The `decide` examples cover the\ndefinitions' *values* on four graphs; the Python brute force covers them on\nall 1,099 labelled graphs to five vertices against independent\nimplementations (networkx core number, the exact pathwidth DP). The\n`treewidth` definition is checked by nothing yet (no `decide`, no Python\ncounterpart run against it) and should be read as a candidate definition\nuntil item 1 of the gap list uses it.\n**Finding, in one paragraph.** Both ends of the corpus sandwich are now\ntheorems in the repository's Lean development: `degeneracy ≤ pathwidth` and\n`pathwidth ≤ bandwidth` for every finite simple graph, each proved through\nvertex separation and carried to pathwidth by the existing Kinnersley\ntheorem, with no `sorry` and the standard three axioms; the layout-level\nform `pathwidth ≤ bandwidthOfLayout σ` is literally the `bw_rcm` inequality\n§5 checked. The proofs are short — the upper half is a window-counting\nargument (an active vertex is within `b` of a prefix neighbour), the lower\nhalf is that every later neighbour is active at its vertex's own position\nand that the first vertex of any subset has all its subset-neighbours after\nit — and the definitions are pinned to the corpus's implementations on every\ngraph to five vertices and to the kernel on four hand-checked graphs. Item\n09's two theorems and its conjecture are stated over a new `TreeDecomposition`\nstructure and stand with `sorry` behind a three-item gap list, the load-bearing\ngap being that Mathlib has no `pathGraph.IsTree`; the two degeneracy forms,\nLick–White's and the elimination ordering's, are proved equal by the greedy\nordering, an induction on the vertex count across types. `lake build`\npasses (1,638 jobs).\n**Size range covered.** The theorems: every finite simple graph. The\ncorpus recount: 6,376 certified instances at 9–134 customers (`instances.csv`).\nThe brute-force definition check: all labelled graphs on 1–5 vertices;\nthe kernel examples: 3 and 7 vertices.\n**Kill criterion.** None stated by the plan. Deliverable as asked: two\ntheorems (plus the chain to pathwidth and the sandwich statement), and for\nthe stated items a gap list with the missing lemmas named.\n**Method notes.** *`#print axioms` is the test.* A `sorry` in a lemma three\nfiles away is invisible in a `lake build` that \"succeeds with warnings\";\n`learning.sandwich --stage lean` reads the axiom list of every named theorem\nand fails on `sorryAx` in a proved one — and on its *absence* in a stated\none, so a proof that lands must be promoted in `PROVED`/`STATED` to pass.\nLean wraps long axiom lists across lines: match with `re.DOTALL`. *Kernel\n`decide` over `Finset (Fin 7)`* (128 subsets) exceeds the default\n`maxRecDepth`; 10,000 suffices and the check takes under a second. *A doc\ncomment cannot precede `set_option … in example`* (parse error: expected\n`lemma`); use a line comment. *`G.loopless` is `Std.Irrefl G.Adj`* in this\nMathlib, not a function; `G.ne_of_adj h rfl` is the irreflexivity step.\n*`Mathlib.Data.Real.Sqrt` is deprecated* in favour of `Mathlib.Analysis.Real.Sqrt`.\n*The lake cache is the whole budget*: the incremental build is 2 s; a\nMathlib rebuild would be the session.\n","numLines":570,"startLine":715,"totalLines":1284}}}
